import{cg as a,ch as o,ci as n,cj as s,ck as i,cl as r,cm as l,cn as h,co as d,cp as c,cq as u,cr as p,cs as m,ct as g,cu as f,cv as y,cw as w,cx as b,cy as v,cz as k}from"./index-mrg-xRe4.js";import"./react-vendor-E9sCIgAP.js";import"./query-vendor-BDwphoGe.js";import"./supabase-vendor-B4YknJm4.js";const T={title:"Long and short: what they are and how you profit when the market falls",seoTitle:"Long and Short: What They Are and How You Profit When the Market Falls | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"What long and short mean, with simple examples. How to short on the chart, why a short can lose more than you put in, and what a short squeeze is (the GameStop story).",excerpt:"In a long you profit from a rise, in a short from a fall. Simple examples, where the risk is higher, how to short on the chart and what a short squeeze is.",category:"Trading for beginners",coverAlt:"Long and short: in rising and falling markets",html:`
<p class="lead">There are two ways to make money in the markets. In a <strong>long</strong> you profit when price goes up, and in a <strong>short</strong> you profit when price goes down. Long is the normal thing everyone knows. Short sounds a bit strange at first, but once you understand it, it's one of a trader's most important tools.</p>

<h2>What is a long?</h2>
<p>A long is like everything else in life: <strong>buy low and sell high</strong>. You buy a stock or a contract at a certain price, wait for the price to rise, sell, and make the difference.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> we bought an Apple share at $100, and it rose to $120. We made $20 on each share, that's 20%. If we bought 100 shares, we paid $10,000 and sold for $12,000.</p>

<h2>What is a short?</h2>
<p>In a short you do the opposite: <strong>first you sell, and only then you buy</strong>. The goal is to sell high and buy low.</p>
<p>I know it sounds illogical, because you're selling something you don't have. Think of it like a loan: you sell now, and later you buy it back to return what you sold. The system lets you do this with one click.</p>
<p><strong>Example:</strong> we sold a Tesla share at $100, and it fell to $90. We bought it back at $90 and made 10%. But if after we sold at $100 the price went up, and we had to buy at $120, we lost $20.</p>

<h2>Bulls and bears: what bullish and bearish mean</h2>
<p>In the markets there's always a war between the <strong>bulls</strong> and the <strong>bears</strong>. It's worth knowing the terms, because you'll hear them everywhere:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>bull</strong> attacks from the bottom up. That's why a long is called a <strong>bullish move</strong>, and the people going long are the "longs", the bulls. A <a href="/academy/glossary#bull-market">bull market</a> is a market in an uptrend.</li>
<li>A <strong>bear</strong> attacks from the top down. That's why a short is called a <strong>bearish move</strong>, and the people going short are the "shorts", the bears. A <a href="/academy/glossary#bear-market">bear market</a> is a market in a downtrend.</li>
</ul>
<p>The colors work the same way: <strong>green</strong> marks a rise and <strong>red</strong> marks a fall. When you're short, on most platforms your position will show in red.</p>

<h2>Where is the potential higher, and where is the risk?</h2>
<p>This is a question I love asking in the course, because the answer surprises a lot of people:</p>
<table class="rules">
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Long</th><th>Short</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Maximum profit</strong></td><td>No ceiling. Buy at 100 and sell at 300, you made 200%. 500% and 1,000% are possible too</td><td>100% at most. Even if the company goes bankrupt and the price falls to 0</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Maximum loss</strong></td><td>What you put in. If the price falls to 0, you lose the amount you bought with</td><td>No ceiling. You sold at 100 and the price rose to 200, you lost 100%. It rose to 300, you lost 200%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In other words, <strong>in a short you can lose more than the money you put in</strong>. That's one of the reasons you never enter a trade without a <a href="/academy/glossary#stop-loss">stop</a>, and especially not a short.</p>

<h2>How you actually short, on the chart</h2>
<p>In a short, every order is the opposite of a long. This is how it looks on my platform (<span class="en">NinjaTrader</span>, on the Nasdaq contract):</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Entry:</strong> <span class="en">Sell Market</span> to enter right now, or <span class="en">Sell Limit</span> above the current price, to enter only when price reaches the point you chose.</li>
<li><strong>Stop:</strong> above the entry, because if price goes up you're losing. In a short the stop is a buy order: <span class="en">Buy Stop Market</span>.</li>
<li><strong>Target:</strong> below the entry, with a <span class="en">Buy Limit</span> order. To close a short you have to buy.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/long-short/ls-short-chart.webp" alt="A short trade on the chart in NinjaTrader: a stop order above the entry and a target below it" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>I explained every order type, and when to use each one, in the article <a href="/blog/order-types">Order types: market, limit and stop</a>.</p>

<h2>What is a short squeeze?</h2>
<p>This is where the two things we learned come together. Everyone who's short has a stop above them, and that stop is a <strong>buy order</strong>. Now imagine lots of people went short in the same area, and all their stops sit in the same place above the price.</p>
<p>The moment price gets there, all the stops trigger at once: buy, buy, buy. All those purchases <strong>fuel the rise</strong>, and price flies up even further. That's a <strong>short squeeze</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>The famous story: GameStop (<span class="en">GME</span>), 2021.</strong> Hedge funds and institutions sat on the stock in a short, at crazy percentages. A group of small traders, organized on <span class="en">Reddit</span>, decided to buy together and push the price up. Within about three weeks the stock rose from roughly $4.5 to $119, more than 25 times, and the shorts lost billions of dollars.</p>
<p>If you want to find stocks with a high short percentage, you can go to the <span class="en">Finviz</span> screener and filter by <span class="en">Short Float</span>, for example above 20% or 30%. It's dangerous, but it's good to know the phenomenon, because it explains sharp moves that seem to come "out of nowhere".</p>

<div class="cta"><h2>Ready for the next step?</h2><p>Next, learn the orders you'll use in every trade in <a href="/blog/order-types">Order types: market, limit and stop</a>. And if you already want to trade a prop firm account, <a href="/comparison">compare the prop firms</a> side by side.</p></div>
`,faq:[{q:"What's the difference between long and short?",a:"In a long you buy and profit when the price rises. In a short you first sell, then buy back, and profit when the price falls."},{q:"How can I sell something I don't have?",a:"The system allows it. Think of it like a loan: you sell now, and later buy back to close the position. The profit or loss is the difference between the prices."},{q:"Is a short riskier than a long?",a:"Yes. In a long the maximum loss is the amount you put in. In a short there's no ceiling on the loss, because the price can keep rising. That's why you never go short without a stop."},{q:"Where do you put the stop in a short?",a:"Above the entry price, with a Buy Stop Market order. To close a short you have to buy, so both the stop and the target are buy orders."},{q:"What is a short squeeze?",a:"When price rises into the area where many shorts have their stops. Those stops are buy orders, and when they trigger together they push the price up even further."}]},A={title:"Order types: market, limit, stop and stop limit",seoTitle:"Order Types: Market, Limit, Stop and Stop Limit | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"The difference between market, limit, stop and stop limit orders, and what bid, ask and spread are. When to use each order, in a long and a short, with chart screenshots.",excerpt:"Market, limit, stop and stop limit, bid, ask and spread: what each order does, when to use it, and why a stop loss should only be a stop market.",category:"Trading for beginners",coverAlt:"Order types: market, limit, stop",html:`
<p class="lead">Before your first trade, you have to know the orders: <strong>market, limit, stop and stop limit</strong>, and also the <strong>bid and the ask</strong>. These are the tools you buy, sell and protect your account with. The buttons look a little different on every platform, but the orders are the same. If you understand them on one platform, you'll manage on any platform.</p>

<h2>How is the price on the screen set?</h2>
<p><strong>The price you see is the price of the last trade that was executed.</strong> If there's a buyer who wants to buy at 99 and a seller who wants to sell at 101, the price doesn't move. It only moves when there's a trade: a buyer and a seller agree on 100, and that's the new price.</p>
<p>And here's what's important to understand: <strong>the price on the screen isn't necessarily the price you can buy at.</strong> If you see 100, there may be no seller at 100 right now, and the nearest seller is at 101.</p>

<h2>Bid, ask and spread</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong><a href="/academy/glossary#bid">Bid</a>:</strong> the highest price someone is willing to pay right now. It's the nearest buyer, below the current price.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/academy/glossary#ask">Ask</a>:</strong> the lowest price someone is willing to sell at right now. It's the nearest seller, above the current price.</li>
<li><strong><a href="/academy/glossary#bid-ask-spread">Spread</a>:</strong> the gap between the bid and the ask. In the example: between 99 and 101.</li>
</ul>
<p>These buyers and sellers are waiting with limit orders. If they used market orders, they'd already be in a trade.</p>

<h2>Market order (<span class="en">Market</span>)</h2>
<p>The most common and simplest order: <span class="en">Buy Market</span> or <span class="en">Sell Market</span>. You're basically telling the system: <strong>buy for me now, as fast as possible, at the best price you can get.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The advantage:</strong> instant execution.</li>
<li><strong>The drawback:</strong> you may well not get a good price. In the example above, a <span class="en">Buy Market</span> will buy at 101, not 100. And if there are no sellers at 101 and 102, it will buy at 103. That's called <a href="/academy/glossary#slippage">slippage</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limit order (<span class="en">Limit</span>)</h2>
<p>Limit, as in a limitation. You set the price in advance, and <strong>the order will execute only at that price.</strong> For example, a <span class="en">Buy Limit</span> at 97: don't buy for me at any other price. A <span class="en">Sell Limit</span> at 104: sell for me only when the price reaches 104.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The advantage:</strong> full control over the price.</li>
<li><strong>The drawback:</strong> it won't always fill. Very often the price comes close to your level, doesn't reach it, and goes the other way. And you missed the trade.</li>
</ul>
<p>A limit is also used for a target: you're in a trade, you place a <span class="en">Sell Limit</span> at the target price, and the trade closes in profit exactly there.</p>

<h2>Stop order (<span class="en">Stop Market</span>)</h2>
<p>The stop order is there <strong>to stop a loss.</strong> You're in a trade, no matter how you got in, and the price is going against you. The stop takes you out at the point you set in advance, before the loss becomes really big.</p>
<p><strong>In trading you always, always work with a stop.</strong> It's the difference between failure and success. Without a stop, one trade can go deep and wipe out the account.</p>
<p>The drawback: sometimes the stop will "burn" you, and the price will then come back in the direction you wanted. That's part of the game.</p>

<div class="box warn"><strong>Why a stop market and not a stop limit?</strong>A limit executes only at the exact price you asked for. In a sharp drop there may be no buyer at exactly that price, and the price will simply skip over the order and keep falling, while you're still in the trade. With a stop market, the moment the price touches the level, you're out at the best price available. Maybe not a perfect price, but you're out. That's why I use only a <span class="en">Stop Market</span> for a stop loss.</div>

<h2>Stop limit order (<span class="en">Stop Limit</span>)</h2>
<p>So when do you use a stop limit? When you want to <strong>buy at a price higher than the current price</strong>, or <strong>sell at a price lower than it</strong>.</p>
<p>It sounds strange: the price is 100 now, why would you want to buy at 105? There are reasons, for example when you want to enter only after the price breaks through a certain area. But the system can't accept a regular limit order above the price, because from its point of view you can buy cheaper right now. So the order has to be a stop order: a <span class="en">Buy Stop Limit</span> above the price, or a <span class="en">Sell Stop Limit</span> below it.</p>

<h2>My tip: <span class="en">Buy Bid</span> and <span class="en">Sell Ask</span> instead of market</h2>
<p>When I want to get in or out right here and now, <strong>I don't use a market order.</strong> Instead I use <span class="en">Buy Bid</span> (buying at the nearest buyer's price, a little below the current price) or <span class="en">Sell Ask</span> (selling at the nearest seller's price, a little above it).</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The advantage:</strong> you almost always get a better price than with a market order. Often it fills that same moment.</li>
<li><strong>The drawback:</strong> sometimes it takes a few seconds, and sometimes the price runs away and you miss the trade.</li>
</ul>
<p>For me the advantages win. And when there's time, the best thing is to wait with a limit order at the level you chose in advance.</p>

<h2>Summary: which order for which situation</h2>
<table class="rules">
<thead><tr><th>What you want to do</th><th>In a long</th><th>In a short</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Enter now</td><td><span class="en">Buy Bid</span> or <span class="en">Buy Market</span></td><td><span class="en">Sell Ask</span> or <span class="en">Sell Market</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Enter at a better price than the current one</td><td><span class="en">Buy Limit</span> below the price</td><td><span class="en">Sell Limit</span> above the price</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enter only after the price breaks a level</td><td><span class="en">Buy Stop Limit</span> above the price</td><td><span class="en">Sell Stop Limit</span> below the price</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stop loss</td><td><span class="en">Sell Stop Market</span> below the entry</td><td><span class="en">Buy Stop Market</span> above the entry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Profit target</td><td><span class="en">Sell Limit</span> above the entry</td><td><span class="en">Buy Limit</span> below the entry</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In a short everything is reversed, because to close a short you have to buy. There's a full explanation of shorting in the article <a href="/blog/long-short">Long and short</a>.</p>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/order-types/ot-chart-orders.webp" alt="Orders on the chart in NinjaTrader: the right-click order menu, with limit and stop orders" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>In NinjaTrader, for example, you place most orders with a right-click on the chart, at the price level you want. Other platforms look a little different, but the orders are the same. If you trade a prop firm account on <span class="en">Tradovate</span>, you can also set a <a href="/blog/tradovate-daily-stop">daily stop</a> there, so one bad day doesn't blow the account.</p>

<div class="cta"><h2>Know your orders? Now manage the risk</h2><p>Once the orders are clear, the next step is <a href="/blog/risk-reward-1-to-3-losing-streaks">managing the risk on every trade</a>.</p></div>
`,faq:[{q:"What's the difference between a market order and a limit order?",a:"A market order executes immediately, at the best price available at that moment, so sometimes at a poor price. A limit order executes only at the price you set in advance, so sometimes it doesn't execute at all."},{q:"What are bid and ask?",a:"The bid is the highest price a buyer is willing to pay right now. The ask is the lowest price a seller is willing to sell at right now. The difference between them is called the spread."},{q:"Why is the price I got different from the price I saw?",a:"The price on the screen is the price of the last trade. With a market order you buy at the nearest seller's price, and sometimes at a price further away. That difference is called slippage."},{q:"Which order do you use for a stop loss?",a:"A stop market: Sell Stop Market in a long and Buy Stop Market in a short. With a stop limit, the price can skip over the order in a strong move, and then you stay in the trade."},{q:"When do you use a stop limit?",a:"When you want to buy above the current price or sell below it, for example to enter only after the price breaks through a certain area."}]},I={title:"Trading styles: day trading, swing trading or long-term investing",seoTitle:"Day Trading, Swing Trading or Long-Term Investing: What's the Difference? | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"The difference between day trading, swing trading and long-term investing: holding time, risk, profit potential, screen time and the type of analysis. With a comparison table and what scalping is.",excerpt:"Long-term investing, swing or day trading? How they differ in risk, profit, screen time and type of analysis, with a table comparing all three.",category:"Trading for beginners",coverAlt:"Trading styles: day, swing and long-term",html:`
<p class="lead">There are three main styles of investing and trading in the markets: <strong>long-term investing, swing trading and day trading.</strong> The difference between them isn't just how long you hold a trade. It's also how much risk you take, how much you can make, how much time you need to spend in front of the screen, and what type of analysis fits. In this article I'll go over each one, and at the end you'll find a table comparing them.</p>

<h2>Long-term investing: "set and forget"</h2>
<p>You put the money in and look at it once a month or two. Once a quarter, when the company publishes its reports, you check that it's still good and profitable. There's almost no hands-on work.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The risk is low,</strong> if you invest in indices, like the <span class="en">S&amp;P 500</span> and the Nasdaq, or in big, stable companies like Apple, Microsoft and Google. Over the long term the risk is very low. Not zero, because anything can happen, but low.</li>
<li><strong>The profit potential is also relatively low,</strong> especially if you don't have a lot of money. Say you put in $100,000 and made 6% a year: that's $6,000, before tax and fees. It's nice, but it's not what will grow you fast.</li>
<li><strong>Over the years it works.</strong> Someone who puts money in every month, over many years, can see very nice growth with low risk.</li>
</ul>
<p>On the chart you look at weekly or monthly candles. A daily chart is too short a timeframe for this kind of investing. Personally, for long-term investing I really like indices: there's no hands-on work at all.</p>

<h2>Swing trading: days to weeks</h2>
<p>You buy today and sell tomorrow, in two days, in a week or two. The goal is to take a good opportunity on the chart and profit from it over a few days.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The analysis is mostly technical,</strong> on a daily or weekly chart. The fundamentals sit in the background: if there's a war right now and oil prices might rise, I'll take that into account. But I won't sit on reports, because it's a short period.</li>
<li><strong>The risk and the profit potential are medium.</strong> Over the long term, good companies tend to rise. In the short term you can't know: maybe you landed in a period of declines, and maybe an announcement suddenly came out that knocked the market down.</li>
<li><strong>Looking once a day is enough,</strong> to decide whether to sell or hold.</li>
</ul>
<p>It isn't as easy as it sounds. You need to stay alert so you don't miss opportunities, and the profit depends a lot on how much money you put in.</p>

<h2>Day trading: buy and sell on the same day</h2>
<p><a href="/academy/glossary#day-trading">Day trading</a> is <strong>the riskiest trading, with the highest profit potential.</strong> You can make nice "daily salaries" with it: $500, $1,000 and $2,000 a day. It's possible, for someone who knows how to do it.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The analysis is technical only,</strong> on one-minute, 5-minute, 15-minute and hourly charts. You can check the daily chart to understand the direction. The weekly and monthly matter less.</li>
<li><strong>The risk is high.</strong> A lot can happen within a day. Price can rise, fall and whipsaw back and forth for no reason at all.</li>
<li><strong>You have to sit on the chart,</strong> without getting up from it. Personally, I make a point of sitting an hour, an hour and a half at most every day, and leaving with the daily salary.</li>
<li><strong>It takes experience,</strong> and it's very much mental and psychological. I wrote about that side in the article <a href="/blog/trading-psychology">The mental side of trading</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is mainly what I do. I also do swings and a bit of investing, but what I show on YouTube and in the courses is mainly day trading, because that's where the biggest money is. Most prop firm traders trade exactly like this: day trading futures, closed before the end of the day.</p>

<h3>And what is scalping?</h3>
<p><a href="/academy/glossary#scalping">Scalping</a> is a type of day trading. You try to take <strong>small profits with small risks,</strong> for example $50 to $100 per trade, and you take relatively many trades. The risk-reward ratio is about 1:1, sometimes even a bit less, because you're counting on a high win rate.</p>
<p>It's a method I like and use a lot, and it also suits someone who doesn't have a lot of time in front of the chart. But it suits traders who already understand the chart better. Many beginners try it and get burned: one trade loses $600, they win it back slowly with small trades, and then another big loss, until the account is blown. It demands quick decisions, and it's <strong>not for someone who struggles to cut a loss fast.</strong> If you find yourself holding a losing trade because "maybe it'll come back", it's not for you. And like any method, you start on demo.</p>

<h2>The table: the three styles side by side</h2>
<table class="rules">
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Day trading</th><th>Swing trading</th><th>Long-term investing</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Holding time</strong></td><td>Minutes to hours</td><td>Days to weeks</td><td>Years</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Goal</strong></td><td>Fast profits on small moves</td><td>Taking short-term opportunities</td><td>Stability and steady growth</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Analysis</strong></td><td>Technical</td><td>Technical (and a little fundamental)</td><td>Fundamental</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Chart</strong></td><td>1 minute, 15 minutes, hourly</td><td>Daily, weekly</td><td>Weekly, monthly</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Risk</strong></td><td>Very high</td><td>Medium</td><td>Relatively low</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Monitoring</strong></td><td>Constant, during trading</td><td>Once a day</td><td>Once every few months</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Advantage</strong></td><td>High, fast profits</td><td>Good profit without sitting in front of the screen all day</td><td>Low risk, no stress and no time investment</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Drawback</strong></td><td>Stress and high risk, takes experience and self-control</td><td>You need to stay alert, the profit depends on the amount</td><td>Takes a long time to grow</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>I explained which analysis fits each style in the article <a href="/blog/technical-vs-fundamental-analysis">Technical vs. fundamental analysis</a>.</p>

<div class="cta"><h2>Starting from the basics?</h2><p>Learn the foundations: <a href="/blog/long-short">long and short</a>, <a href="/blog/order-types">order types</a> and <a href="/blog/technical-vs-fundamental-analysis">the types of analysis</a>. And if you chose day trading, you can trade with a prop firm's capital: <a href="/comparison">compare the prop firms</a>.</p></div>
`,faq:[{q:"What's the difference between day trading and swing trading?",a:"In day trading you buy and sell on the same day, on minute and hourly charts. In swing trading you hold a trade from a few days to a few weeks, and look at a daily or weekly chart."},{q:"Which style is the riskiest?",a:"Day trading. It has the highest profit potential, but also the highest risk, and it takes experience and self-control."},{q:"How long do you need to sit in front of the screen in day trading?",a:"It depends on the method. I personally trade an hour to an hour and a half a day. During that time you need to be in front of the chart the whole time."},{q:"What is scalping?",a:"A type of day trading where you take small profits with small risks, and take relatively many trades. It demands quick decisions, and it's not for someone who struggles to cut a loss."},{q:"Which style do prop firm traders use?",a:"Most prop firm traders day trade futures, because at most firms you have to close your positions before the end of the trading day."}]},x={title:"Technical vs. fundamental analysis: the difference and which to use",seoTitle:"Technical vs. Fundamental Analysis: The Difference and Which to Use | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"What technical analysis is and what fundamental analysis is, who each one suits, how to combine them, and how to read a candlestick. A simple explanation for beginners.",excerpt:"Fundamental analysis looks at the company and the world, technical analysis looks at the chart. The difference, why short-term trading looks mainly at the chart, and how to read a candlestick.",category:"Trading for beginners",coverAlt:"Technical vs. fundamental analysis: what's the difference?",html:`
<p class="lead">When you analyze a stock, an index or a contract, there are two main types of analysis. <strong>Fundamental analysis</strong> looks at the company from the inside and at what's happening in the world. <strong>Technical analysis</strong> looks at the chart. In this article I'll explain the difference, why in short-term trading I look mainly at the chart, and how to read a candlestick, which is the basis of all technical analysis.</p>

<h2>What is fundamental analysis?</h2>
<p>In fundamental analysis you take a company and look at it from the inside: <strong>how much it earns, how much it spends, whether it has debt, and who its competitors are.</strong> Some companies spend far more than they earn, for example a drug company that's still developing a product. Then you check whether there are approvals, whether a trial is planned, and so on.</p>
<p>Fundamental analysis also includes <strong>what's happening in the world</strong>: wars, COVID, tension between superpowers. If there's a big event that could knock the market down, you take it into account.</p>

<h2>What is technical analysis?</h2>
<p>Technical analysis is based on <strong>the chart.</strong> You look at how price behaved in the past, and based on that you try to predict what will happen next.</p>
<p><strong>Why does it work?</strong> Say that in the past, every time the price reached 100, it flew up. That means at 100 there are lots of people willing to buy. And if every time it reached 200 it fell, there are probably lots of people willing to sell at 200. It's a market like any other: try to sell your apartment for two million and nobody buys, drop it to one million and suddenly there are plenty of buyers. Prices tend to repeat themselves, and that's how <a href="/academy/glossary#support">support</a> and <a href="/academy/glossary#resistance">resistance</a> areas form.</p>

<h2>Why I look mainly at the chart</h2>
<p>You can be the biggest genius in the world, read every financial report, and even send them to <span class="en">ChatGPT</span>. But let's be real: <strong>we have no edge in the reports.</strong> The big banks and investment houses analyze them with the best people there are, and they have direct contact with the CEOs. I won't understand the reports better than they do.</p>
<p><strong>The chart is my edge.</strong> You can't hide things from the chart. If the reports look amazing and the stock looks like the strongest one out there, but the chart is falling, as far as I'm concerned something is going on. The chart tells me the story, including what I can't know.</p>

<h2>The differences, and how to combine them</h2>
<table class="rules">
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Technical analysis</th><th>Fundamental analysis</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>What you look at</strong></td><td>Charts, prices, past moves</td><td>Reports, revenue, debt, news</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Who it suits</strong></td><td>Short-term trading: intraday, a week, up to a month or two</td><td>Anyone who wants to hold for a few quarters or more</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In practice you combine them: <strong>you focus on the chart, and in the background you understand what's happening in the market.</strong> If there's a war right now pushing oil prices up, or a company is about to publish its reports, you take it into account. For the long term you can put money into good companies and let it run for years. But in day trading and swing trading, only the chart. I explained which type of analysis fits which style in the article <a href="/blog/trading-styles">Trading styles: day trading, swing or long-term investing</a>.</p>
<p>And when you trade futures, as prop firm traders do, one of the "fundamental" things with the biggest intraday impact is economic releases. It's worth checking their times every morning in an economic calendar.</p>

<h2>How to read a candlestick</h2>
<p>Technical analysis starts with reading a <a href="/academy/glossary#candlestick-chart">candlestick chart</a>. Each candle shows what happened in one period, according to the chart you chose: on a 5-minute chart each candle is 5 minutes, on a daily chart each candle is a day, and on a monthly chart each candle is a month.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Green candle (up):</strong> the bottom of the body is the opening price, and its top is the closing price. In the example: we opened at 20 and closed at 50.</li>
<li><strong>Red candle (down):</strong> the opposite. The top of the body is the opening price, and the bottom is the close. We opened at 50 and closed at 20.</li>
<li><strong>The tails (wicks):</strong> the prices reached during the candle. In the example: we went down to 10 and also up to 60, but closed inside the body.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every candle looks different, and each one tells a story. For example, a candle with a long tail at the bottom says price fell hard during the candle, but couldn't close down there, and came back up.</p>

<div class="cta"><h2>Want to keep learning?</h2><p>Continue with the basics: <a href="/blog/long-short">long and short</a> and <a href="/blog/order-types">order types</a>. And if you want to trade with a prop firm's capital, <a href="/comparison">compare the prop firms</a>.</p></div>
`,faq:[{q:"What's the difference between technical and fundamental analysis?",a:"Technical analysis is based on the chart and on how price behaved in the past. Fundamental analysis is based on the company's financial data, like revenue, profits and debt, and on news and events in the world."},{q:"Which analysis is better?",a:"It depends on the timeframe. For day trading and swing trading, the chart is what matters. For investing over a few quarters or more, fundamental analysis fits better. In practice you combine them: focus on the chart, and take into account what's happening in the market."},{q:"Why does technical analysis work?",a:"Because prices tend to repeat themselves. If in the past many people bought at a certain price and the price rose from there, there's a chance buyers will be there next time too. That's how support and resistance areas form."},{q:"How do you read a candlestick?",a:"In a green candle the bottom of the body is the opening price and the top is the closing price. In a red candle it's the opposite. The tails show the highest and lowest prices during the candle."}]},P={title:"Overtrading: what it is, how to spot it and how to stop",seoTitle:"Overtrading: What It Is, How to Spot It and How to Stop | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"Overtrading is any action in trading that doesn't come from a plan. The signs that you're in it, and 6 simple tools to stop it: a fixed number of trades, trading hours, breaks, a checklist and a mental journal.",excerpt:"Any action in trading that doesn't come from a plan is overtrading. How to spot the signs, and 6 tools that help you stop before the account is blown.",category:"Mindset",coverAlt:"Overtrading: a trader in front of screens with dozens of buy and sell orders",html:`
<p class="lead">Overtrading is the silent killer of trading accounts. It doesn't look dangerous: one more trade, a little more time in front of the screen. But it's responsible for a large share of the accounts that get blown, even among traders who read charts very well.</p>

<h2>What is overtrading?</h2>
<p>The simplest definition: <strong>any action in trading that doesn't come from a clear plan is overtrading.</strong> You entered a trade that isn't your setup, because of an urge, pressure, impatience, a wish to win back a loss or a wish to make more. That's overtrading.</p>
<p>It shows up mainly in two things: <strong>too many trades a day</strong>, and <strong>too many hours in front of the screen</strong>. The longer you sit, the more bored you get, and out of boredom you press the button.</p>

<h2>How it happens (to everyone)</h2>
<p>You sit for three hours, waiting for a trade. Finally you get in, and the trade loses. Now you don't have the patience to wait another three hours, so you take another trade, to win it back. And another one.</p>
<p>And there's the classic trap: "the trade I'm waiting for is about to come." While you wait, you take another trade and another trade. You stay another half hour and another hour, and the trade doesn't come.</p>

<h2>The signs: how to tell you've slipped into overtrading</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Trade after trade, under pressure.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Losing control:</strong> entering trades in unrelated places, in the middle of a move, without a clear setup.</li>
<li><strong><span class="en">FOMO</span>:</strong> the move is running away, and you jump in because "you can't miss it".</li>
<li><strong>Playing with the stop:</strong> moving it further away when price goes against you.</li>
<li><strong>Your body is signaling:</strong> shifting uncomfortably in your chair, forcing a trade, and after the entry asking yourself "why did I get in?"</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to stop: 6 tools</h2>
<h3>1. A number of trades set in advance</h3>
<p>Three, four, five, even ten, depending on your method. What matters is that the number is set before you open the chart, not during the session.</p>
<h3>2. Fixed trading hours</h3>
<p>I, for example, trade in a fixed time window of about an hour and a half, and not beyond it. When the time is up, you get up, even if "the trade is about to come".</p>
<h3>3. A break after every loss</h3>
<p>Took a loss? Get up. Coffee, something to eat, fresh air, five to ten minutes. If you lost two trades in a row, you can close the computer for 15 minutes or half an hour. The chart will give you more opportunities, I promise.</p>
<h3>4. A checklist before every trade</h3>
<p>Why I'm getting in, how, and what I'm waiting to see happen. When you have a clear checklist, you enter only when it's met, and not "just because".</p>
<h3>5. A mental journal</h3>
<p>Besides the trade journal, also mark your mental mistakes: overtrading, trading outside your hours, trying to win back a loss, impatience. At the end of the month you see where you slip the most, and focus on improving only that. In the <a href="https://journal.antartrade.co.il" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trading journal</a> you can mark mistakes like these next to every trade.</p>
<h3>6. A trading plan</h3>
<p>All these tools come together in one written <a href="/blog/trading-plan">trading plan</a>, which you don't change in the middle of a trading day.</p>

<div class="box"><strong>The less you trade, the more you'll make</strong>People ask me: if you know how to make a thousand dollars in half an hour, why not trade four hours and make four thousand? The answer: if I sit in front of the chart for four hours, not only won't I make four thousand, I'll lose. They say trading is the hardest way to make easy money.</div>

<h2>Read more</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="/blog/trading-psychology">The mental side of trading: why most traders lose</a></li>
<li><a href="/blog/risk-reward-1-to-3-losing-streaks">How many losses in a row to expect, and why it matters</a></li>
</ul>
`,faq:[{q:"What is overtrading?",a:"Any action in trading that doesn't come from a clear plan: a trade that isn't your setup, taken out of an urge, pressure, impatience or a wish to win back a loss. It usually shows up as too many trades a day and too many hours in front of the screen."},{q:"How many trades a day is too many?",a:"There's no single right number. What matters is setting the number in advance, according to your method, and not going beyond it during the trading day."},{q:"What do you do after a losing trade?",a:"Get up for a short break, at least a few minutes, without your phone. After two losing trades in a row it's worth stepping away from the computer for 15 minutes to half an hour, and analyzing the chart again when you come back."},{q:"How do you track mental mistakes?",a:"In a mental journal: mark every time there was a mistake like overtrading, trading outside your hours or trying to win back a loss. At the end of the month you see which mistake repeats the most, and focus on it."}]},S={title:"How to build a trading plan: what to write, and why you don't change it mid-day",seoTitle:"Trading Plan: How to Build One and What to Write in It | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"What to write in a trading plan: hours, instrument, entry conditions, size, maximum loss per trade and per day, a daily target and a number of trades. How many days it will take to reach the target, a checklist for opening the day and a mental mistakes journal.",excerpt:"A written trading plan takes emotion out of the game. What to write in it, how to calculate how many days it will take to reach the target, and why you must never change it in the middle of a trading day.",category:"Mindset",coverAlt:"Trading plan: strategy, risk management, entry and exit",html:`
<p class="lead">If there's one thing that will save you from all the mental mess of trading, it's a written trading plan. It sounds boring, but it's the foundation of success. A plan you follow like a robot takes emotion out of the game.</p>

<h2>Why do you need a trading plan?</h2>
<p>Think of someone on a diet. They have an excellent plan, but every few days they feel like a bit of chocolate and a bit of pizza. They won't get results. Trading is exactly the same.</p>
<p>And most traders know this script: you profit on Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday, and then in one day you lose everything you made, and more, because of one mistake. In other words, you already know how to profit on most days. What's missing is something that stops that one bad day. <strong>In a written plan there's no room for emotion</strong>, so it can't cause the big mistakes.</p>

<h2>What to write in the plan</h2>
<table class="rules">
<thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>What to write</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Trading hours</td><td>A fixed time window, and you don't go beyond it. At the start you can trade more to get to know the chart, but later an hour or two a day, at most.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Instrument</td><td>What you trade: for example <span class="en">NQ</span> or <span class="en">MNQ</span>. One thing, two at most. Don't spread yourself thin.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Entry conditions</td><td>Your setup: what has to happen for you to get in. If you have several setups, write them all.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Size</td><td>How many contracts, at most. Start with small sizes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum loss per trade</td><td>For example $200 or $300. Based on this and on where the stop is, you set the size.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maximum loss per day</td><td>A daily stop, also set in the system. The goal is not to reach it. It's there for emergencies, when the market suddenly flies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Daily profit target</td><td>When you reach it, you stop. If you're in a winning trade that can still run, move up the stop and let it run. But once you're out, the day is over.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Number of trades a day</td><td>For example up to three or four. And you can add a rule: I lost two, I'm out.</td></tr>
<tr><td>More rules</td><td>Rules you learned the hard way. For example: never add to a losing trade, don't enter against the momentum, wait patiently and don't chase a trade.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If you get stressed when you reach your stop, and it really hurts, the size is probably wrong. Lower the size. Maybe you'll reach a payout a few days later, but it will be much easier on you mentally.</p>

<h2>The most important rule: don't change the plan in the middle of a trading day</h2>
<p>You can and should improve the plan: raise or lower the number of trades, change hours, update the daily target and stop. But <strong>never in the middle of a trading day, and never in the middle of a trade</strong>, when emotion is working on you. You change it only when the chart is closed, at the end of the day, and not right after a losing day.</p>

<h2>How many days will it take to reach the target?</h2>
<p>Part of the plan is understanding the numbers. Say a $50,000 account with a $3,000 target, a $500 daily target and a $500 daily stop. What decides it is how many of your days are profitable:</p>
<table class="rules">
<thead><tr><th>Profitable days</th><th>Average profit per day</th><th>Trading days to 3,000</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>75% (3 out of 4)</td><td>$250</td><td><strong>12</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>60% (6 out of 10)</td><td>$100</td><td><strong>30</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>This calculation gives hope and a plan of action. If the win rate is low, maybe it's worth raising the daily target or lowering the daily stop, and checking again. Our <a href="/calculators">trader calculators</a> have tools for this kind of calculation.</p>

<h2>A checklist for opening a trading day</h2>
<p>Everyone builds their own, and there's no single right way. An example structure:</p>
<ol>
<li>Daily chart: what's the market's story? Direction, trend, important areas.</li>
<li>15-minute chart: the same process, at a higher resolution.</li>
<li>5-minute chart: fine-tune the levels.</li>
<li>Check a related contract too (for example <span class="en">ES</span> when you trade <span class="en">NQ</span>).</li>
<li>Check the <a href="/economic-calendar">economic calendar</a> for important releases.</li>
</ol>

<h2>A mental mistakes journal</h2>
<p>At the start of my journey I had a table on the wall with the mistakes that kept repeating for me: trading past my hour, overtrading, too many trades, too big a size, impatience. Every time I made a mistake I added a check mark, and after a few days I saw where most of the mistakes were concentrated. Then I improved exactly that thing.</p>
<p>Today you can do this in the <a href="https://journal.antartrade.co.il" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trading journal</a>, where you mark mistakes next to every trade. Every small change like this makes a big difference. Read the <a href="/blog/trading-journal-guide">trading journal guide</a> as well.</p>

<h2>Read more</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="/blog/overtrading">Overtrading: what it is and how to stop</a></li>
<li><a href="/blog/trading-psychology">The mental side of trading</a></li>
</ul>
`,faq:[{q:"What should be in a trading plan?",a:"Fixed trading hours, what you trade, entry conditions (the setup), size, maximum loss per trade and per day, a daily profit target, a number of trades a day, and personal rules you learned from experience."},{q:"When are you allowed to change the trading plan?",a:"Only when the chart is closed, at the end of a trading day, and not right after a losing day. Never in the middle of a trading day or in the middle of a trade, because then emotion makes the decisions."},{q:"How long will it take me to pass a $50,000 evaluation?",a:"It depends on the share of profitable days. For example, with a $500 daily target and daily stop: if 75% of days are profitable, about 12 trading days. If 60%, about 30."},{q:"Why do you need both a daily stop and a maximum loss per trade?",a:"The loss per trade sets the size you enter with. The daily stop is a safety net for unusual cases, when the market suddenly jumps, and it stops a bad day before it turns into a blown account."}]},q={title:"The mental side of trading: why most traders lose (and how not to be one of them)",seoTitle:"The Mental Side of Trading: Why Most Traders Lose | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"Why everyone profits on demo and loses live, what to do after a big loss and after a big win, why consistency is the most important rule, and when to get up from the chair. From the mindset chapter of Noam Antar's course.",excerpt:"The mental side is 90% of trading. Why on demo we're all world champions, what to do after a loss and after a win, and how consistency and breaks protect the account.",category:"Mindset",coverAlt:"The mental side of trading: discipline vs. emotion",html:`
<p class="lead">I always say the mental side is 90% of trading. You can learn the technical tools in a few lessons: reading a chart, drawing lines, finding support and resistance. But what separates a trader who makes money from a trader who loses is almost always the head, not the chart.</p>

<h2>The monkey parable: why most traders lose</h2>
<p>Think of a monkey pressing a buy or sell button at a random point. What are its chances of being right? About 50%. The price either goes up or down.</p>
<p>So how is it that a monkey has a fifty-fifty chance, and most people, however smart they are, don't succeed at trading? The answer: <strong>emotions</strong>. We bring in fear, greed, the urge to win back a loss and the fear of losing a profit. These emotions turn a 50/50 chance into much less.</p>

<h2>Why on demo we're all world champions</h2>
<p>On demo everyone makes money. You get in, set a target and a stop, walk away, and come back to a profit. Live, on the same chart, with the same orders and the same numbers, suddenly everything changes. You feel like someone is "moving the chart" right to your stop.</p>
<p>The chart didn't change. <strong>The only difference between demo and a real account is in your head.</strong> On demo losing doesn't hurt, so we follow the plan. Live, fear and greed come in, and the mistakes come with them.</p>
<p>And one more thing worth remembering: a $700 loss hurts more than a $1,000 win feels good. That pain is what makes us move stops, hold losers and close winners too early.</p>

<h3>How to move from demo to a real account</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Start with the smallest size there is,</strong> for example one micro contract, and feel how it affects you.</li>
<li><strong>Trade real sizes on demo too.</strong> There's no point practicing with 10 contracts on demo and then trading one live.</li>
<li><strong>Always a target and a stop,</strong> both for the trade and for the whole day.</li>
<li><strong>If you're stressed inside a trade,</strong> the size is probably too big. Lower the size.</li>
<li><strong>Film yourself trading.</strong> That's how I started uploading videos to YouTube. When you watch it later, you see on your face exactly when the stress kicked in.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The problem isn't the loss, it's what you did after it</h2>
<p>A loss is part of the statistics. If you lose one trade out of three, there's no reason to be upset about one loss. The next trade will probably win. But that's only true when you're following the plan.</p>
<p>The problem starts after the loss: you want to win it back, you increase size, take random trades, get back to zero, drop again, and in the end you blow the account. We've all been there.</p>
<div class="box"><strong>Winning or failing isn't the loss itself</strong>It's how you behaved after it. Whoever said "thank you, market, see you tomorrow" won. Whoever tried to take revenge on the market, even if they won the money back that same day, will blow it next time.</div>
<h3>What to do after a big loss</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop trading that very moment.</strong> Close the computer, even if you think you'll win it all back.</li>
<li><strong>After a break,</strong> an hour or two, analyze what happened: did you get stopped out according to the plan, or was there a mental mistake (adding to a loser, overtrading, trying to win it back)?</li>
<li><strong>You can take tomorrow off too.</strong> That's fine.</li>
<li><strong>Come back with smaller sizes</strong> than usual, and let your confidence return gradually.</li>
</ol>
<p>Why is it so hard to stop? Because it feels like such a waste: you worked three days, and blew it all in one. But it's better to reach a payout a week later than to blow the account.</p>

<h2>You also need to know how to behave after a win</h2>
<p>After a big win we're also not fit to keep trading. One of two things happens: either you get aggressive ("we already made a thousand, let's make more"), or every trade is taken in fear of losing the profit. In the second case, the 800 profit turns into minus 200, you try to win it back, and you blow it.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A daily profit target:</strong> when you reach it, or get close to it, stop. Don't chase the last $50.</li>
<li><strong>A fixed trade size:</strong> don't increase size after wins, and not after losses either.</li>
<li><strong>Made money? Finish early.</strong> It's better to end a day feeling good. Losing a profit hurts more than a normal loss.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Consistency: the most important rule</h2>
<p>Consistency means doing the same thing every day. If you know how to make $400-500 a day, do it today, tomorrow and the day after. Losing in the same range is also fine. What isn't fine: one day making a thousand, another day losing four thousand.</p>
<p>Think about it: $300 a day looks like little when the potential is two thousand. But $300 over ten trading days is $3,000, and you can multiply that across several accounts. <strong>Trading isn't a 50-meter sprint, it's a marathon.</strong> That's also exactly what the prop firms' <a href="/academy/glossary#trading-consistency">consistency rule</a> wants to see.</p>
<h3>How to grow</h3>
<ol>
<li>Start with one account and a small size.</li>
<li>Reach a payout consistently.</li>
<li>Only then grow, gradually: to two or three accounts, then four, five, eight. Copying trades between accounts is done with <span class="en">Copy Trade</span> software.</li>
</ol>
<p>Someone who jumps from one account to twenty feels the pressure on every trade. Growing gradually keeps your head in place.</p>

<h2>Breaks: when to get up from the chair</h2>
<p>My wife says she knows I'm losing by the creaking of the chair: I start shifting uncomfortably. The body always signals before the head. The signs:</p>
<ul>
<li>Shifting uncomfortably in your chair.</li>
<li>Forcing a trade, even when the chart isn't giving one.</li>
<li>Going outside your method ("maybe I'll get in here after all").</li>
<li>After the entry you ask yourself: "why did I get into this trade?"</li>
</ul>
<p>When that happens, <strong>get up.</strong> The chart isn't going anywhere. Ten minutes, coffee, fresh air, and no phone. When you come back, analyze the chart again as if you're starting a new day. And if you can't spot the moment, schedule breaks in advance: every half hour, and after every losing trade.</p>

<h2>Don't trade without another income</h2>
<p>I get a lot of messages from people who quit their job to focus on trading. That's a mistake. Trading is a profession, and like any profession it takes time to learn. Someone who needs the payout to pay the bills won't get there, because financial pressure ruins decisions. Trade with peace of mind, with a steady income on the side.</p>

<h2>The tools that help you keep your head straight</h2>
<ul>
<li>A written <a href="/blog/trading-plan">trading plan</a> that you don't change in the middle of the day.</li>
<li><a href="/blog/overtrading">Know what overtrading looks like</a> and stop it in time.</li>
<li>A <a href="https://journal.antartrade.co.il" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trading journal</a> where you also mark mental mistakes, not just trades. At the end of the month you see where you slip the most, and improve one thing at a time.</li>
</ul>

<div class="cta"><h2>Turn it into rules</h2><p>The mental side holds when it's written down. Start with your own <a href="/blog/trading-plan">trading plan</a>, and see <a href="/blog/risk-reward-1-to-3-losing-streaks">how many losses in a row to expect</a>, so a losing streak doesn't catch you off guard.</p></div>
`,faq:[{q:"How much of trading is mental?",a:"In my opinion about 90%. You learn the technical tools relatively fast. What separates a trader who makes money from a trader who loses is the ability to follow a plan, stop in time and not let fear and greed run the trades."},{q:"Why do I make money on demo and lose on a real account?",a:"The chart and the orders are the same. The difference is emotions: on a real account, fear of losing, the urge to win back a loss and fear of losing a profit come in. Start with the smallest size there is, and grow gradually."},{q:"What do you do after a big loss?",a:"Stop trading that moment, analyze the loss after a break, and come back with smaller sizes than usual. You can also take the next day off."},{q:"Why should you stop trading after a big win?",a:"Because after a big win you tend either to get aggressive, or to trade in fear of losing the profit. Either way it's easy to lose the profit and more. A daily profit target helps you stop in time."},{q:"Can you quit your job and live only from trading?",a:"Not at the start. It takes time to learn the profession, and someone who needs the money to pay bills trades under pressure and makes bad decisions. It's best to trade with a steady income on the side."}]},O={title:"Why a 1:3 ratio burns your account: the math behind the mental collapse",seoTitle:"Why 1:3 Burns the Account: Losing Streaks by Win Rate | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"How many losses in a row to expect in 100 trades at 30%, 40%, 60% and 70% win rates, how often it repeats, and why 1:3 with a 30% win rate is dangerous for a funded account.",excerpt:"1:3 trades look great in a screenshot, but at a 30% win rate you're almost certain to go through 7 losses in a row in 100 trades. The numbers, the formula, and what it means for a funded account.",category:"Risk management",coverAlt:"A 1:3 risk-reward ratio: win rate, psychology and trading pressure",html:`
<p class="lead">Nothing looks better on Instagram than a 1:3 or 1:4 trade: buying the bottom, selling the top, a screenshot and likes. What doesn't make the photo is the road there: a loss, and another loss, and another loss. In this article we'll check in numbers what really happens over 100 trades when your win rate is low, how many losses in a row to expect, how many times it will repeat, and why it can break you mentally even before the account breaks.</p>

<!-- 1 -->
<h2>The pretty trade nobody shows you the price of</h2>
<p>Good traders who take 1:3 or 1:4 trades usually end up around a 30% to 40% win rate. That makes sense: the further the target, the fewer trades reach it. Mathematically it can be completely profitable. The problem isn't in the spreadsheet, it's in your head.</p>
<p>When you hold a trade and get stopped out, hold another one and get stopped out, again and again, the mental failure builds up. Very often, by the time the winning trade arrives, you've already done something silly: moved the stop closer, exited early, increased size to "win it back". And then even the winning trade doesn't save the result.</p>
<p>So let's talk numbers. I'm a mortgage advisor by day and a trader by night, I'm a numbers person, and I'll try not to go too deep into the math.</p>

<!-- 2 -->
<h2>How many losses in a row should you expect in 100 trades?</h2>
<p>The table shows the chance that at least once in 100 trades you'll get a losing streak of this length or longer, by win rate:</p>
<table class="rules">
  <thead><tr><th>Losses in a row</th><th>30% win rate</th><th>40% win rate</th><th>60% win rate</th><th>70% win rate</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>5 or more</td><td>99.9%</td><td>97.6%</td><td>45.9%</td><td>15.3%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6 or more</td><td>99%</td><td>87.3%</td><td>21.2%</td><td>4.8%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7 or more</td><td>94.9%</td><td>68.9%</td><td>8.9%</td><td>1.4%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8 or more</td><td>85.8%</td><td>49%</td><td>3.6%</td><td>0.4%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>10 or more</td><td>58%</td><td>20.5%</td><td>0.6%</td><td>Less than 0.1%</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The numbers in this article are calculated with an exact method, so they may differ slightly from my video. The conclusion is the same.</p>
<p>Look at the 30% column: you'll almost certainly lose 7 trades in a row, and in more than half the cases you'll reach 10 consecutive losses. At a 40% win rate, 6 losses in a row are almost guaranteed.</p>
<p>Now look at a 60% to 70% win rate, the area of 1:1 trades. There even 5 losses in a row aren't guaranteed anymore, and 10 losses in a row almost never happen. That's a huge mental difference: you're not failing, failing, failing. You're failing, winning, failing, winning. You see wins along the way, and that's what keeps you in the game.</p>
<div class="box"><strong>In simple words</strong>The longest losing streak you can reasonably expect in 100 trades: about <strong>10</strong> at a 30% win rate, <strong>7</strong> at 40%, <strong>4</strong> at 60%, and <strong>3</strong> at 70%.</div>

<!-- 3 -->
<h2>How many times will the nightmare repeat?</h2>
<p>OK, you lost 8 trades in a row. Will it happen only once? Not necessarily.</p>
<p>At a 30% win rate, the simple formula says a streak of 8 losses "will happen" about 5 times in 100 trades. But it double counts streaks: if you lost trades 1 to 8 and also trade 9, it counts that as two streaks (1 to 8 and 2 to 9). When you count each separate streak once, it turns out that in 100 trades you'll get on average about <strong>1.6</strong> separate streaks of 8 losses or more. In other words, it's not rare at all for it to happen twice.</p>
<p>At a 60% win rate, by contrast, a streak of 6 losses or more will happen on average only <strong>0.24</strong> times in 100 trades. In most cases it won't happen at all, and if it does, probably just once.</p>
<p>Another mental reason to choose 1:1 or 1:1.5 trades: tighten your targets, raise your win rate. If you want to be mentally strong, this is the place to start.</p>

<!-- 5 -->
<h2>The happy side: winning streaks</h2>
<p>So far we've talked about losses. Now the opposite: what's the chance of a streak of winning trades in 100 trades?</p>
<table class="rules">
  <thead><tr><th>Wins in a row</th><th>30% win rate</th><th>40% win rate</th><th>60% win rate</th><th>70% win rate</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>3 or more</td><td>86.4%</td><td>98.8%</td><td>Almost 100%</td><td>Almost 100%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4 or more</td><td>43.3%</td><td>80.1%</td><td>99.9%</td><td>Almost 100%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5 or more</td><td>15.3%</td><td>45.9%</td><td>97.6%</td><td>99.9%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6 or more</td><td>4.8%</td><td>21.2%</td><td>87.3%</td><td>99%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7 or more</td><td>1.4%</td><td>8.9%</td><td>68.9%</td><td>94.9%</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>At a 30% win rate, 6 winning trades in a row is almost a dream: less than 5%. At a 60% win rate it happens in 87% of cases, and at 70% almost always.</p>
<h3>A winning streak = a payout on the table</h3>
<p>Say you trade 1:1 with $400 of risk per trade. 6 winning trades in a row are $2,400. At some prop firms that's already a payout. And at a 60% win rate it doesn't happen just once: on average you'll get about <strong>1.8</strong> separate streaks of 6 wins or more in 100 trades, so probably twice.</p>
<p>At a 30% win rate, by contrast, the average is 0.05 such streaks. Even if it happened to you once, the chance of it repeating in the same 100 trades is almost zero.</p>

<!-- 6 -->
<h2>Same expected profit, a completely different experience</h2>
<p>Here's the point that matters most. Let's compare two traders risking $300 per trade:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Trader A:</strong> 1:3 with a 30% win rate. Expected profit: 0.3 × 3 − 0.7 = <strong>+0.2R</strong> per trade, that is +$60.</li>
  <li><strong>Trader B:</strong> 1:1 with a 60% win rate. Expected profit: 0.6 × 1 − 0.4 = <strong>+0.2R</strong> per trade, also +$60.</li>
</ul>
<p>On paper, exactly the same profit. In practice, trader A can expect a streak of about 10 losses in 100 trades, and trader B a streak of about 4. Now add a funded account with a $2,000 Drawdown: 7 losses in a row at $300 already break it.</p>
<table class="rules">
  <thead><tr><th>Win rate</th><th>Chance of 7 losses in a row in 100 trades</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>30% (1:3)</td><td><strong>94.9%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>40% (1:2)</td><td><strong>68.9%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>60% (1:1)</td><td><strong>8.9%</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>70%</td><td><strong>1.4%</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>And that's only a consecutive streak. In practice an account can also fail from losses spread between wins, so reality is even harder. You can check this in the <a href="/calculators#survival" data-link="calculators">evaluation pass odds calculator</a>.</p>

<!-- 7 -->
<h2>Bottom line: we came to bring money home</h2>
<p>I'm not saying you can't trade 1:3. If it works for you, great. But if your win rate is so low that you break mentally, ask yourself why you came. We came to bring money home, not to take screenshots for Instagram.</p>
<p>Often when I post my trading, people tell me: "you only do scalps". True. I do what gets me the best results. I don't want to sit in front of the chart for 10 hours waiting for price to reach the sky, when I can get the same profit in a short trade with a bigger contract size. My win rate is high, and a few good winning streaks in 100 trades are enough for me. That's a lot of money, those are payouts on the table.</p>
<div class="box"><strong>My tip (Noam Antar, the author)</strong>Start working with statistics and math. That's what separates traders from gamblers. Trading can be gambling, but when you do it with the right statistics and math, it becomes a profession.</div>

<div class="cta">
  <h2>Check your own streaks</h2>
  <p>Enter your win rate, ratio, risk and Drawdown in the <a href="/calculators#streaks" data-link="streaks-calculator">losing and winning streaks calculator</a>, and see how many losses in a row to expect and whether the account will hold. And if you're buying a prop firm evaluation, the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span> gives you the highest discount.</p>
</div>

<p class="disc">This article is for information and learning only and is not investment advice. The probabilities assume independent trades with a fixed win rate; they are a model, not a forecast. Trading futures involves high risk.</p>
`,faq:[{q:"Is a 1:3 risk-reward ratio bad?",a:"Not necessarily. 1:3 with a 30% win rate gives a positive expected profit of 0.2R per trade, exactly like 1:1 with a 60% win rate. The difference is in the streaks: at a 30% win rate a streak of about 10 losses in 100 trades is reasonable, and that's very hard mentally and dangerous for an account with a small Drawdown."},{q:"How many losses in a row should I expect in 100 trades?",a:"It depends on the win rate. The longest losing streak you can reasonably expect is about 10 at a 30% win rate, 7 at 40%, 4 at 60% and 3 at 70%."},{q:"Why are the numbers in the article different from the formula 1 − (1 − q^L)^(N − L + 1)?",a:"The quick formula treats overlapping windows of trades as if they were independent, so it overstates the chance. The numbers in the article are calculated exactly, trade after trade. For example, at a 30% win rate the chance of 10 losses in a row in 100 trades is about 58%, not 93%."},{q:"How does this relate to a funded account?",a:"In an account with a $2,000 Drawdown and $300 of risk per trade, 7 losses in a row fail the account. At a 30% win rate the chance of such a streak in 100 trades is about 95%, and at a 60% win rate only about 9%."}]},F={title:"How to pass a prop firm evaluation: the beginner's way and the fast way for advanced traders",seoTitle:"How to Pass a Prop Firm Evaluation: For Beginners and Advanced Traders (2026) | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"How to pass a prop firm evaluation: for beginners, fixed risk, a technical stop and statistics. For advanced traders, all in, trading news in large evaluations, and evaluations with a consistency rule, with the calculations and the risks.",excerpt:"Two ways to approach a prop firm evaluation: beginners learn cheaply from evaluations with fixed risk and statistics, and advanced traders pass fast, with the calculations and risks of each method.",category:"Guides",coverAlt:"Two ways to pass a prop firm evaluation",html:`
<p class="lead">There are two ways to approach a prop firm evaluation, and they suit two kinds of traders. Beginners need to learn from evaluations cheaply. Advanced traders who already get payouts want to pass fast and reach the funded account. In this guide I'll explain both ways, including the advantages and the risks.</p>

<h2>First of all: know the rules</h2>
<p>If you understand 100% of the prop firm's rules and what it wants from you, you've already done half the way to a payout. Before buying an evaluation, check on the firm's page: the target, the stop (intraday trailing or end of day), the maximum number of contracts, the consistency rule, the minimum trading days, and what's allowed during economic releases. You'll find an explanation of all these rules in the <a href="/blog/tik-nostro">complete guide to prop firm accounts</a>, and every firm's numbers in the <a href="/comparison">firm comparison</a>.</p>

<h2>Path 1: beginners, learn from evaluations cheaply</h2>
<p>Evaluations are the biggest gift prop firms have given traders. Until not long ago, anyone who wanted to learn to trade lost thousands of dollars of their own money and quit. Today you can learn for the cost of an evaluation. So at this stage, <strong>the goal isn't to pass fast, but to keep the evaluation alive as long as possible and learn.</strong> Passing an evaluation by luck is worth nothing, because you'll probably blow the funded account.</p>
<h3>1. Risk a small, fixed amount on every trade</h3>
<p>On a $50,000 account: about $300-500 per trade (on larger accounts, proportionally more). That way two mistakes don't blow the evaluation.</p>
<h3>2. A risk-reward ratio of at least 1:1.5</h3>
<p>For example, risk 300 to make at least 450-500.</p>
<h3>3. Technical stop first, size second</h3>
<p>You don't decide on "a 20-point stop" in advance. You find where the right stop is technically, for example below support, and calculate the size from the distance to it:</p>
<div class="box"><strong>Number of contracts = risk amount ÷ (stop distance in points × point value)</strong>For example: $400 of risk, a 100-point stop, an <span class="en">MNQ</span> contract ($2 per point): 400 ÷ (100 × 2) = 2 contracts. On platforms like <a href="/tradingview-guide">TradingView</a> you can enter the risk amount, and the size is calculated automatically.</div>
<h3>4. Build statistics</h3>
<p>When every trade risks the same amount, after a month you can see the real picture. A 50% win rate with a 1:1 ratio? Maybe try higher targets, even if the win rate drops to 40%. A very low win rate? Your trade selection probably needs work. For that you need a <a href="/blog/trading-journal-guide">trading journal</a>. It's also worth checking <a href="/blog/risk-reward-1-to-3-losing-streaks">how many losses in a row to expect</a> at your win rate.</p>
<h3>5. Patience and a plan</h3>
<p>This may be the most important thing evaluations teach you: wait for the right trade and work by a <a href="/blog/trading-plan">trading plan</a>. Start with the cheapest evaluations, with an end-of-day (EOD) stop and a 50,000 account.</p>

<h2>Path 2: advanced traders, pass an evaluation fast</h2>
<p>Traders who have already withdrawn money don't want to spend weeks on an evaluation. They want to reach the funded account and get paid. Here there are a few approaches traders use. They're legitimate, but it's important to understand the risk: <strong>you knowingly accept that some evaluations will be blown.</strong> It only pays off when the evaluations are cheap.</p>
<h3>"All in": maximum contracts and one target</h3>
<p>You enter one trade with the maximum number of contracts allowed, and a target that brings you exactly to passing the evaluation. Either the evaluation passes, or it's blown.</p>
<p><strong>How to calculate the target:</strong> (evaluation target + commissions) ÷ number of contracts ÷ $20 per point (on the <span class="en">NQ</span> contract). Multiply the result in points by 4 to get ticks, and that's how you set the target on the platform.</p>
<table class="rules">
<thead><tr><th>50,000 evaluation (3,000 target)</th><th>Contracts allowed</th><th>Move needed</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Apex, Legacy accounts</td><td>10 minis</td><td><strong>About 15 points</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Bulenox, trailing plan</td><td>7 minis</td><td><strong>About 21.5 points</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Accounts with 3-5 contracts</td><td>3-5 minis</td><td><strong>30-50 points</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>As of October 2026, Apex allows trading 10 minis only on Legacy accounts, which are still sold. On accounts that allow fewer contracts it's much harder: 15 points sometimes move in a minute, but 40-50 points need a long move. Check the exact number of contracts on each firm's page, because it changes.</p>
<p><strong>Important rules:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Only when the market is volatile:</strong> when the <span class="en">ATR</span> is high (above 25-30 points per candle). In quiet hours the price won't reach the target.</li>
<li><strong>Enter at a good level</strong>, at support or resistance, not in the middle of a move.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the trailing stop:</strong> on an account with an intraday trailing drawdown, a trade that reached a 2,000 profit and came back can blow the evaluation, because the stop rose along with the profit.</li>
<li><strong>Careful with the template on the funded account:</strong> if you saved a template with 10 contracts on the platform, it's easy to enter with it by mistake on the funded account too.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Trading economic releases in large evaluations</h3>
<p>In large evaluations, like 250,000 or 300,000, you need a move of 30-40 points or more, and it's hard to get on a normal day. Some traders use major economic releases, when the market jumps hard, to pass.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Only at firms that allow it in the evaluation.</strong> Check the rules tab. You can see release times in an <a href="/economic-calendar">economic calendar</a>.</li>
<li><strong>It's a gamble.</strong> During a release the price can go up and down in seconds, and the system doesn't always "catch" the price. You can be in a big profit and get stopped out a moment later.</li>
<li><strong>Not in expensive evaluations, and not in evaluations with a consistency rule.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h3>Evaluations with a consistency rule</h3>
<p>At firms with a consistency rule in the evaluation (for example 40% or 50%), you can't pass in one day. With a 50% rule on an evaluation with a 3,000 target, your most profitable day can't exceed 1,500. An approach that works: make close to the allowed maximum in the first two days, and complete the rest of the minimum days afterward. If there was a mistake at the start, at least you didn't waste days. Check each firm's and plan's consistency rule in the <a href="/comparison">comparison</a>, and do the math in the <a href="/calculators">consistency calculator</a>.</p>

<div class="box warn"><strong>What not to do: long and short at the same time on two accounts</strong>Some traders open a long on one account and a short on another, so that "one of them passes". That's against prop firm rules, and it can lead to them refusing to pay you a payout or banning you. It also doesn't teach you to trade. Don't do it.</div>

<h2>Which path is right for you?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Haven't been paid out yet?</strong> Path 1. Cheap evaluations, fixed risk, statistics and patience.</li>
<li><strong>Already been paid out, and you know how to hold a funded account?</strong> You can consider path 2, on cheap evaluations and knowing that some will be blown.</li>
</ul>
<p>Either way, the mental side decides. Read <a href="/blog/trading-psychology">The mental side of trading</a> and <a href="/blog/overtrading">Overtrading</a> as well.</p>

<div class="cta"><h2>Want to start right?</h2><p>Find the current prices, after the discount code <span class="code">ANTAR</span>, on the <a href="/promotions">deals page</a>, and compare the firms' rules side by side in the <a href="/comparison">comparison</a>.</p></div>
`,faq:[{q:"How do you pass a prop firm evaluation the first time?",a:"First learn the firm's rules. Then risk a small, fixed amount on every trade (about $300-500 on a 50,000 account), with a risk-reward ratio of at least 1:1.5, set the size by the technical stop, and work patiently by a plan."},{q:"Can you pass an evaluation in one day?",a:"At firms without a consistency rule in the evaluation, you can. Advanced traders do it with the maximum number of contracts and a calculated target, when the market is volatile. It's risky, and some evaluations get blown, so it only pays off on cheap evaluations."},{q:"How many points do you need to pass a 50,000 evaluation with 10 minis?",a:"About 15 points on NQ: the $3,000 target plus commissions, divided by 10 contracts, divided by $20 per point. As of October 2026, Apex allows 10 minis only on Legacy accounts."},{q:"Can you trade during economic releases in the evaluation?",a:"It depends on the firm. Some firms allow it in the evaluation and forbid it on the funded account. Check the rules tab on the firm's page. Even when it's allowed, it's a high-risk gamble."},{q:"Can you open a long on one account and a short on another?",a:"No. It's against prop firm rules, and it can lead to a refused payout or a ban."}]},L={title:"What is a prop firm account? The complete guide to trading a prop firm's capital (2026)",seoTitle:"What Is a Prop Firm Account? The Complete Guide to Trading a Prop Firm's Capital (2026) | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"A prop firm account is a trading account a prop firm gives you after you pass an evaluation. How it works, what it costs, how the firms make money, which rules matter and where to start.",excerpt:"What a prop firm account is, how an evaluation works, what it costs, why the funded account is still a demo and how prop firms make money. The complete guide.",category:"Guides",coverAlt:"How a prop firm account works: buy an evaluation, reach the target and keep within the stop",html:`
<p class="lead">A <strong>prop firm account</strong> (also called a "funded account") is a trading account a prop firm gives you after you pass its trading evaluation. You trade the firm's account, not your own money. If you make money, you withdraw most of the profit. If you lose, the only amount you lost is the price of the evaluation.</p>
<p>I'm Noam Antar, a futures day trader since 2019. In this guide I'll explain exactly how the model works, what it costs, how the firms themselves make money, and which rules matter most before you buy your first evaluation.</p>

<h2>Why most traders used to fail, and what changed</h2>
<p>You've probably heard that most people fail at day trading. Until a few years ago, anyone who wanted to trade had to bring tens of thousands of dollars of their own money. Up against banks, investment houses and big funds, most private traders lost that money in a short time, and quit.</p>
<p>Prop firms changed the equation. Instead of risking $20,000, you risk only the price of the evaluation. Even if you blew an evaluation, or ten evaluations, you learned a lot at a low cost, and didn't lose your savings.</p>

<h2>How does it work? Step by step</h2>
<p>Let's take a $50,000 account as an example, which is the base account at most firms:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Buy an evaluation.</strong> You pay once (at some firms the payment renews every month until you pass).</li>
<li><strong>Reach the target.</strong> In the example: a $3,000 profit, meaning the account has to reach 53,000.</li>
<li><strong>Keep within the stop.</strong> In the example: you can't lose $2,000. If the account drops to the stop, the evaluation is "blown".</li>
</ol>
<p>The evaluation is a <strong>demo account with real market data</strong>. The candles, moves and prices are identical to what happens on the exchange, but you don't risk real money, only the price of the evaluation.</p>
<p>After you pass the evaluation you get a <strong>funded account</strong>, and from that account you can withdraw profits.</p>

<h2>How much does a prop firm account cost?</h2>
<p>The price depends on the firm and the account size. An evaluation for a $50,000 account usually costs tens of dollars, and there are almost always deals. Every firm's current prices, after discount, are in the <a href="/comparison">prop firm comparison</a> and on the <a href="/promotions">deals page</a>. With the discount code <span class="en">ANTAR</span> (in capital letters) you always get the highest discount available at that moment.</p>
<p>Note two things:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Some firms charge an activation fee</strong> after you pass the evaluation, and some don't. It's listed on each firm's page.</li>
<li><strong>At the start, evaluations are a learning tool.</strong> That's why I recommend starting with the cheapest evaluations: if you blew a $20-30 evaluation, it's part of learning, not a disaster.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How do prop firms make money?</h2>
<p>This is the first question everyone asks: what's in it for them? It sounds too good to be true.</p>
<p><strong>The answer is simple: most of prop firms' revenue comes from selling evaluations.</strong> Most people buy an evaluation, blow it, and buy another one. For the firm, every blown evaluation is pure profit. It has no problem with you buying 20 evaluations. It will be very happy if you blow them all.</p>
<p>That's why the rules are built so that only consistent, disciplined traders reach a payout. It's not a scam. It's a clear business model, and once you understand it, you know exactly what to do: work by the rules, and don't give the firm the "pleasure" of another blown evaluation.</p>

<h2>Funded account: demo or real money?</h2>
<p>Here's a detail many people don't know: <strong>even after you pass the evaluation, the funded account is in most cases still a demo account.</strong> It isn't connected to the exchange.</p>
<p>Why? Because the firm knows most people who pass the evaluation will blow the funded account too. It doesn't want to lose real money on traders who haven't proven themselves yet. So it works like this:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>If you blew the funded account,</strong> the firm didn't lose money, because it was a demo.</li>
<li><strong>If you reached a payout target,</strong> the firm pays you <strong>out of its own pocket</strong>. The money you withdraw is real money in every sense.</li>
<li><strong>A trader who proves themselves</strong> over time, with payouts and consistency, is moved at the firm's discretion to a <strong>live account</strong> connected to the real exchange.</li>
</ul>
<p>From experience: a live account isn't always an upgrade. When it's the firm's real money, the rules usually get stricter. Quite a few traders prefer to stay on the funded account and withdraw profits from it regularly.</p>
<h3>When can you withdraw money?</h3>
<p>At most firms you first need to build a <strong>safety buffer</strong> above the account size. In the $50,000 example: you reach 52,000, and what's above that you can withdraw. The firms want you to leave a buffer so the account isn't blown right after the payout. The exact thresholds for each firm and plan are in the "Payout terms" tab on the firm's page.</p>

<h2>The rules you must know before your first evaluation</h2>
<p>I always say: if you know the rules 100%, you've already done half the way to a payout. These are the four main rules:</p>
<h3>1. The trailing drawdown</h3>
<p>The stop doesn't stay in place. It <strong>rises together with your profit</strong>: you made 1,000, and the stop rose by 1,000. There are two main types:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Intraday trailing (<span class="en">Intraday</span>):</strong> the stop moves in real time, <strong>even on unrealized profit</strong> inside an open trade.</li>
<li><strong>End-of-day trailing (<span class="en">EOD</span>):</strong> the stop updates only by the balance you ended the day with. It gives more breathing room, so it usually suits beginners better.</li>
</ul>
<p>On the funded account the stop stops rising at a certain point. In our example it locks at 50,000 once you reach 52,000. From that moment you can keep making money without the stop chasing you.</p>
<p>📖 More in the glossary: <a href="/academy/glossary#intraday-trailing-drawdown">intraday trailing drawdown</a> · <a href="/academy/glossary#eod-trailing-drawdown">end-of-day trailing drawdown</a>. And how to see where the account's stop actually is: <a href="/blog/tradovate-drawdown">the Tradovate guide</a>.</p>

<h3>2. The consistency rule</h3>
<p>Firms don't want "gamblers" who make a huge amount in one day and withdraw. So they set a rule that one day can't be more than a certain percentage of total profits. The percentage changes from firm to firm, for example 30%, 40% or 50%, and some plans have no consistency rule at all.</p>
<p><strong>How to calculate it:</strong> your most profitable day, divided by the consistency percentage. For example: your biggest day was $1,200, and the consistency rule is 30%. 1,200 ÷ 0.3 = $4,000. That's the total profit you need to request a payout. You can calculate it in the <a href="/calculators">consistency calculator</a>.</p>
<p>My tip: set a fixed, moderate daily target, and let time do its work.</p>

<h3>3. Trading during economic releases</h3>
<p>Some firms forbid holding an open trade around major releases, like interest rates, inflation and employment data. Usually this means a few minutes before and after the release. Even where it's allowed, I don't recommend it. You can see the week's releases in an <a href="/economic-calendar">economic calendar</a>.</p>

<h3>4. Scaling limit</h3>
<p>At some firms you start with a small number of contracts, and get more contracts as the account grows. Like stairs.</p>
<p>And there are more rules worth knowing, like DCA, hedging, micro scalping and flipping. What's allowed and what isn't at each firm is in the "Rules" tab on the firm's page.</p>
<p>These rules aren't only there to protect the firm. They teach discipline, patience and risk management, and it's worth following them on a personal account too.</p>

<h2>Futures and leverage, in short</h2>
<p>Most prop firm accounts in the US trade <strong>futures</strong>, for example on the Nasdaq index (<span class="en">NQ</span>) or the <span class="en">S&amp;P 500</span> (<span class="en">ES</span>). In day trading it works exactly like a stock: there's a symbol, a chart, you buy and sell. The difference is that each contract is <strong>leveraged</strong>:</p>
<table class="rules">
<thead><tr><th>Contract</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Value of one point</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Nasdaq mini</td><td><span class="en">NQ</span></td><td><strong>$20</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Nasdaq micro</td><td><span class="en">MNQ</span></td><td><strong>$2</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="en">S&amp;P 500</span> mini</td><td><span class="en">ES</span></td><td><strong>$50</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="en">S&amp;P 500</span> micro</td><td><span class="en">MES</span></td><td><strong>$5</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In other words, one <span class="en">NQ</span> contract that rises 10 points is a $200 profit. My recommendation: focus on one contract, or two at most, and don't jump between them.</p>

<h2>Which prop firm should you choose at the start?</h2>
<p>My approach:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Start with the firm whose evaluations are cheapest.</strong> At this stage the evaluation is a learning tool.</li>
<li><strong>Choose an end-of-day (<span class="en">EOD</span>) stop</strong> and a 50,000 account.</li>
<li><strong>Get at least one payout,</strong> and only then move to firms with more advanced payout terms.</li>
</ol>
<p>You can compare all the firms, rules, prices and payout terms in the <a href="/comparison">prop firm comparison</a>. If you're not sure what suits you, there's a short <a href="/matching-quiz">matching quiz</a>.</p>

<h2>How much can you make on a prop firm account?</h2>
<p>There's no single answer, and it's important to say it honestly: most people don't reach a payout. Those who do enjoy two advantages that regular trading doesn't have:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Several accounts at once.</strong> With <span class="en">Copy Trade</span> software (<a href="/replikanto-guide">Replikanto</a>, <a href="/tradesyncer-guide">Tradesyncer</a>), one trade is copied to several accounts. A $300 profit on one account becomes $3,000 on ten accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Gradual growth.</strong> One account, a first payout, and only then a second account, a third and so on.</li>
</ul>
<p>The investment is small compared to the potential, but it's a profession in every sense, and it takes time.</p>

<h2>Who it suits, and who it doesn't</h2>
<p><strong>It doesn't suit</strong> someone looking for fast money, someone who expects results within two weeks, or someone who isn't willing to lose along the way.</p>
<p><strong>It suits</strong> someone who treats it as a profession: learns, looks at charts, works by rules, and above all is patient. In my opinion it can be a side activity of about an hour a day, and it doesn't have to replace a job.</p>

<div class="cta"><h2>Want to start right?</h2><p>Learn the basics first: <a href="/blog/long-short">long and short</a> and <a href="/blog/order-types">order types</a>. Then <a href="/comparison">compare the prop firms</a> and read <a href="/blog/how-to-pass-prop-firm-evaluation">how to pass an evaluation</a>.</p></div>
`,faq:[{q:"What's the difference between a prop firm account and a regular trading account?",a:"In a regular account you trade your own money. In a prop firm account you trade a firm's account, and risk only the price of the evaluation."},{q:"Is a prop firm account a scam?",a:"Not at the well-known firms. Most of the firms' revenue comes from blown evaluations, so the rules are built so that only consistent traders reach a payout. It's important to choose a firm with a history of paying."},{q:"Is the money you withdraw real money?",a:"Yes. Even if the funded account is a demo, the payout is paid to you in real money, out of the firm's pocket."},{q:"How much does a prop firm account cost?",a:"An evaluation for a $50,000 account usually costs tens of dollars, depending on the firm and the deal. Current prices are in the prop firm comparison on the site."},{q:"Do you need experience to start?",a:"It's worth knowing the basics: candles, long and short, and order types. Start with the beginner articles on the blog."}]},R={title:"What is a footprint chart? The complete guide to reading footprint",seoTitle:"What Is a Footprint Chart? The Complete Guide to Reading Footprint | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"A footprint guide: Bid and Ask, Limit vs. Market, aggressive buyers and sellers, imbalance, delta, POC, Value Area and absorption, with screenshots and how to set it up on TradingView.",excerpt:"What you see inside a footprint candle: Bid and Ask, Limit vs. Market orders, imbalance, delta, POC, Value Area and absorption, and how to set it all up on TradingView.",category:"Order flow",coverAlt:"What is a footprint chart? The complete guide",html:`
<p class="lead">Footprint is one of the most powerful tools in the world of order flow: it shows what happened <strong>inside</strong> each candle - how much was bought and sold at every price, who was aggressive, and where there was an imbalance. In this guide we'll build the understanding from the ground up: Bid and Ask, Limit and Market orders, and then go over everything you see in a footprint candle and how to set it up on TradingView.</p>

<div class="toc"><strong>In this guide:</strong>
<ol>
  <li><a href="#bid-ask">The basics: Bid and Ask</a></li>
  <li><a href="#limit-market">Limit and Market orders - passive vs. aggressive</a></li>
  <li><a href="#read">How to read a footprint candle</a></li>
  <li><a href="#imbalance">Imbalance</a></li>
  <li><a href="#value-area">Value Area and POC</a></li>
  <li><a href="#delta">Delta</a></li>
  <li><a href="#absorption">Absorption</a></li>
  <li><a href="#tradingview">How to open and set up footprint on TradingView</a></li>
  <li><a href="#atas">Footprint in ATAS</a></li>
  <li><a href="#next">What's next</a></li>
  <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
</ol></div>

<h2 id="bid-ask">The basics: Bid and Ask</h2>
<p>Before you read footprint, you have to understand the basics. Say the current price is 100:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bid (demand)</strong> - people who want to <strong>buy</strong>, waiting below the current price: at 99, at 98, at 97.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask (supply)</strong> - people who want to <strong>sell</strong>, waiting above the current price: at 101, at 102, at 103.</li>
</ul>
<p>It's always like this: below the price there are buyers who want to buy cheaper, and above it there are sellers who want to sell higher.</p>

<h2 id="limit-market">Limit and Market orders - passive vs. aggressive</h2>
<p>Everyone waiting on the Bid and the Ask sits in a <strong>Limit order</strong>: "buy (or sell) for me only at this specific price, and at no other price".</p>
<p>A <strong>Market order</strong> says something else: "buy for me now, this moment, at the best price available".</p>

<div class="box"><strong>Question: if the price on the chart is 100, can you buy at 100?</strong>No. There's no seller at 100 - the nearest seller is at 101. If you click <span class="en">Buy Market</span>, you get 101. And if you click <span class="en">Sell Market</span>, the sale executes against the nearest buyer - at 99.</div>

<h3>Market orders are what move the price</h3>
<p>Say there are 10 contracts at each of the levels 99, 98 and 97, and someone sends a 30-contract market sell order. There aren't enough buyers at 99, so the order "eats" 99, then 98 and then 97 - and the price falls. The same in reverse with a big market buy: it pushes the price up.</p>
<div class="pair">
  <div class="sell"><b>Market = aggressive</b>Wants the goods now, "settles" on the price - and that's what moves the market.</div>
  <div class="buy"><b>Limit = passive</b>Sits patiently and waits only for the price it chose.</div>
</div>
<p><strong>A trade happens only when a market order meets a limit order</strong> - aggressive meets passive. Two limit orders don't meet: someone who wants to buy at 99 and someone who wants to sell at 101 simply wait.</p>

<h2 id="read">How to read a footprint candle</h2>
<p>In a footprint candle there are two columns of numbers for every price level:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Right column - Ask:</strong> trades in which <strong>aggressive buyers</strong> (at market) bought from passive sellers sitting in a limit order.</li>
  <li><strong>Left column - Bid:</strong> trades in which <strong>aggressive sellers</strong> (at market) sold to passive buyers sitting in a limit order.</li>
</ul>
<div class="box"><strong>A trick to remember</strong>The right side (green) = aggressive buyers. The left side (red) = aggressive sellers. It's a bit confusing, because the Ask column actually belongs to the sellers in limit orders - but what the number measures is how much was <em>bought aggressively</em> at that level.</div>
<p><strong>Read diagonally:</strong> compare the aggressive buying at a certain level with the aggressive selling at the level below it. For example, 1.83K buys against 1.87K sells - almost even.</p>

<h2 id="imbalance">Imbalance</h2>
<p>When the diagonal difference is large, there's an <strong>imbalance</strong>. For example: 491 aggressive buys against 195 sells, and 298 against 30. The platform marks levels like these (with a green line beside the candle) as an imbalance.</p>
<p>It's a real imbalance - a measured difference between buyers and sellers, not an estimate based on the shape of the candles.</p>
<p>On TradingView you can set the ratio that counts as an imbalance and how many levels in a row to mark it - we'll get to that in the settings.</p>

<h2 id="value-area">Value Area and POC</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Value Area</strong> - the area in the candle where 70% of the candle's volume traded (marked with dashed lines). If a large part of the candle's move happened outside this area, it happened without much volume.</li>
  <li><strong>POC - Point of Control</strong> - the level with the highest volume in the candle (marked in white). It's a strong area worth watching.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="delta">Delta</h2>
<p>Delta is the difference between the total of the Ask column and the total of the Bid column in the candle:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A red candle with 6.67K aggressive buys minus 7.24K aggressive sells = <strong>a negative delta of -569</strong>. A red candle with negative delta - makes sense.</li>
  <li>A green candle with 6.32K minus 4.77K = <strong>a positive delta of 1.54K</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>On a real footprint chart on TradingView, it all appears together - the POC, the imbalances and the delta under each candle.</p>

<h2 id="absorption">Absorption</h2>
<p>Absorption is one of the most powerful things you can spot in footprint.</p>
<p>Picture a red candle: at the top of the candle there was strong aggressive buying - 311 against 94, and 142 against 17 - and still the candle closed down. How? Because the Ask column isn't only aggressive buyers - these are buyers who bought <strong>from sellers in limit orders</strong>. Big passive sellers sat there, "absorbed" all the buying, and the price fell.</p>
<p>The same in reverse in a green candle: at the bottom of the candle there was aggressive selling - 488 against 127, and 113 against 0 - but the candle closed up, because big passive buyers in limit orders absorbed the selling.</p>

<h2 id="tradingview">How to open and set up footprint on TradingView</h2>
<p>Footprint on TradingView is a <strong>chart type</strong>, not an indicator - so don't look for it in the indicator list. It requires a Premium subscription (at the time of writing).</p>
<ol>
  <li>Open the chart type menu (where you switch between candles and a line).</li>
  <li>Choose <span class="en">Volume Footprint</span>. If it doesn't appear, search for it and mark it with a star.</li>
  <li>Zoom in on the chart to see the numbers inside the candles.</li>
</ol>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/footprint/fp-04.webp" alt="A footprint chart on TradingView" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box warn"><strong>Note</strong>I teach this on TradingView because that's where footprint is easiest to see and understand visually - but the data TradingView shows in footprint isn't as accurate as in dedicated order flow software. For learning and understanding it's excellent; for trading based on footprint I work with ATAS.</div>

<h3>The important settings</h3>
<p>Double-click the candle to open the settings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Row size</strong> - automatic or manual. In manual you set how many ticks are in each row: few ticks = many detailed levels, many ticks = fewer levels. It's worth playing with this by the hour and the session (New York, London).</li>
  <li><strong>What to display</strong> - for example buy and sell, or delta.</li>
  <li><strong>Value area</strong> - the share of volume the Value Area marks (the default is 70%; 80% or 50% are possible too).</li>
  <li><strong>POC</strong> - display and color.</li>
  <li><strong>Imbalance</strong> - the ratio that counts as an imbalance. Mine is 300% (3 times). 250% is looser and will show more imbalances; 400% is stricter and will show only the significant ones.</li>
  <li><strong>Stacked levels</strong> - how many imbalances in a row are needed to mark an area. At 1, every imbalance is marked; at 3, only a run of three or more.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/footprint/fp-07.webp" alt="A footprint chart on TradingView with imbalance areas marked" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h2 id="atas">Footprint in ATAS</h2>
<p>ATAS is the order flow software I work with, and probably one of the most advanced today. It shows footprint more accurately, and you can go over a candle with a magnifying glass and see the numbers at every level. It also has a heatmap (Heat Map) that shows where the Limit orders sit in the order book.</p>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/footprint/fp-10.webp" alt="A footprint chart in ATAS" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/footprint/fp-09.webp" alt="The heatmap (Heat Map) in ATAS" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h2 id="next">What's next?</h2>
<p>In this guide we got to know what you see in footprint: who's aggressive and who's passive, imbalance, POC, Value Area, delta and absorption. The next step is understanding how to use it in practice - where to look for them on the chart, and how to combine them with a strategy.</p>
<ul>
  <li><a href="/atas-guide">The complete guide to ATAS</a> - the order flow software, with its plans, prices and how to connect a funded account.</li>
  <li><a href="/deepcharts-guide">The DeepCharts guide</a> - another order flow platform, with footprint charts and market depth.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<dl class="faq">
  <dt>What's the difference between footprint and a regular candle?</dt>
  <dd>A regular candle shows only the open, close, high and low. Footprint also shows what happened inside the candle: how much was bought and sold aggressively at every price level.</dd>
  <dt>Do you need a paid TradingView subscription?</dt>
  <dd>At the time of writing - yes, footprint on TradingView requires a Premium subscription.</dd>
  <dt>What is delta in footprint?</dt>
  <dd>The difference between the total aggressive buying (Ask) and the total aggressive selling (Bid) in the candle.</dd>
  <dt>What is an imbalance?</dt>
  <dd>A large diagonal gap between aggressive buyers and aggressive sellers at neighboring levels, by a ratio you set (for example 3 times).</dd>
  <dt>Is TradingView's data accurate?</dt>
  <dd>Not as accurate as dedicated order flow software. TradingView is excellent for learning; for trading, software like ATAS is recommended.</dd>
</dl>

<div class="cta">
  <h2>Want to go deeper into order flow?</h2>
  <p>Read the <a href="/atas-guide">ATAS guide</a> to see the software I trade footprint with. And when buying evaluations and software - the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span>.</p>
</div>

<p class="disc">This guide is for information and learning only and is not investment advice or a recommendation to act. Trading futures involves high risk and can lead to financial loss. The numbers in the examples are for illustration.</p>
`,faq:[{q:"What's the difference between footprint and a regular candle?",a:"A regular candle shows only the open, close, high and low. Footprint also shows what happened inside the candle: how much was bought and sold aggressively at every price level."},{q:"Do you need a paid TradingView subscription?",a:"At the time of writing - yes, footprint on TradingView requires a Premium subscription."},{q:"What is delta in footprint?",a:"The difference between the total aggressive buying (Ask) and the total aggressive selling (Bid) in the candle."},{q:"What is an imbalance?",a:"A large diagonal gap between aggressive buyers and aggressive sellers at neighboring levels, by a ratio you set (for example 3 times)."},{q:"Is TradingView's data accurate?",a:"Not as accurate as dedicated order flow software. TradingView is excellent for learning; for trading, software like ATAS is recommended."}]},C={title:"The complete guide to setting a daily stop in Rithmic",seoTitle:"How to Set a Daily Stop in Rithmic: The Complete Guide | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"A step-by-step guide to setting a daily stop in Rithmic through R Trader Pro: Auto Liquidate, Loss Limit and Auto Liquidate Threshold, with screenshots.",excerpt:"A step-by-step guide to setting a daily stop in Rithmic through R Trader Pro: Auto Liquidate, Loss Limit and Auto Liquidate Threshold, with screenshots.",category:"Risk management",coverAlt:"Setting a daily stop in Rithmic",html:`
<p class="xlink">Trading through Tradovate? <a href="/blog/tradovate-daily-stop" data-link="tradovate-guide">See the guide to setting a daily stop in Tradovate</a></p>

<h2>What is a daily stop and why should you set one?</h2>
<p>A daily stop is one of the most important risk management tools in trading. Its purpose is to limit the cumulative loss during the trading day and prevent a negative trading day from turning into a significant loss that could hurt the account.</p>
<p>Unlike a <span class="en">Stop Loss</span> order, which limits the loss on a specific trade, a daily stop is there to limit the cumulative losses on the account.</p>
<p>Even experienced traders can fall into a losing streak, make decisions out of frustration or try to win back losses with more trades. This is called <span class="en">Revenge Trading</span>.</p>
<p>Setting a daily stop in advance helps you keep trading discipline, cut down on impulsive decisions and protect your capital from unusual losses.</p>
<p>A daily stop (<span class="en">Daily Loss Limit</span>) is a risk management mechanism that lets a trader preset the maximum loss they're willing to take during the trading day. In Rithmic you set it with the <span class="en">Auto Liquidate</span> mechanism, which automatically closes your positions when the account reaches the loss threshold you set.</p>

<div class="box"><strong>What is Rithmic?</strong>Like Tradovate, Rithmic connects the prop firm with the trading software. Many traders who work with order flow software, like ATAS or Bookmap, connect through Rithmic. You set the daily stop in the <span class="en">R | Trader Pro</span> software.</div>

<h2>How do you set a daily stop in Rithmic? Step by step</h2>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Log in to R | Trader Pro and open the Trader Dashboard</h3>
<p>Open <span class="en">R | Trader Pro</span> and log in to your trading account with the login details you got from the broker or the prop firm. Open the <span class="en">Trader Dashboard</span> from the software's toolbar. This window shows the trading accounts linked to your user.</p>
<figure class="narrow"><img src="${a.url}" alt="R Trader Pro with the Trader Dashboard button on the toolbar" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Open the risk management settings</h3>
<p>Right-click the account you want to set the daily stop on, and choose the first option, <span class="en">View Risk Parameters</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="${o.url}" alt="The Trader Dashboard in Rithmic with the right-click menu and View Risk Parameters" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The account numbers are hidden in the picture.</figcaption></figure>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>Go to the trader's tab</h3>
<p>The window that opens has three tabs. The first two show parameters set by the broker (<span class="en">FCM Administrator</span>) and by the prop firm (<span class="en">IB Administrator</span>). Go to the third tab, <span class="en">Risk Parameters set by the Trader</span>. This is where you set your own risk limits.</p>
<figure><img src="${n.url}" alt="The Risk Parameters window in Rithmic with the Risk Parameters set by the Trader tab" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">4</span>Turn on automatic liquidation</h3>
<p>In the <span class="en">Auto Liquidate</span> column, choose <span class="en">Enabled</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="${s.url}" alt="The Auto Liquidate column in Rithmic with the Enabled option" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">5</span>Choose Loss Limit</h3>
<p>In the <span class="en">Auto Liquidate Criteria</span> column, choose <span class="en">Loss Limit</span> and click outside the field. The list also has other options, like a minimum account balance, but for a daily stop you choose <span class="en">Loss Limit</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="${i.url}" alt="The Auto Liquidate Criteria list in Rithmic with the Loss Limit option" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">6</span>Set the daily loss amount</h3>
<p>In the <span class="en">Auto Liquidate Threshold</span> column, enter the maximum daily loss. In the screenshot it's set to 500, meaning a $500 daily stop. If you want to limit it to $300, enter 300.</p>
<figure><img src="${r.url}" alt="The Auto Liquidate Threshold column in Rithmic with an amount of 500" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">7</span>Save the settings</h3>
<p>Click <span class="en">Apply</span> and confirm. It's a good idea to open the risk settings window again and make sure all the values were saved on the right account.</p>

<div class="box"><strong>My tip (Noam Antar, the author)</strong>What I always tell my students: set a daily stop, say $500, and your mission is never to reach it. That means if you've lost $400, you walk away from the computer. It's a hard mission, but if you stick to it, you'll improve a lot in everything related to risk management.</div>

<div class="cta">
  <h2>Looking for a prop firm?</h2>
  <p>On <a href="/comparison" data-link="comparison">AntarTrade</a> you'll find a comparison of the prop firms, a free trading journal, and more guides. And when buying evaluations, the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span>.</p>
</div>

<p class="disc">This guide is for information and instruction only and is not investment advice. Rithmic's settings and interface may change, and prop firms have their own risk rules. Before trading, make sure the settings were saved on the right account.</p>
`,faq:[]},D={title:"The guide to setting a daily stop and managing risk in Tradovate",seoTitle:"Daily Stop and Risk Management in Tradovate: The Complete Guide | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"How to set a daily stop in Tradovate: Daily Loss Limit, weekly limits, profit targets, locking the settings and Manual Lockout, step by step with screenshots.",excerpt:"How to set a daily stop in Tradovate: Daily Loss Limit, weekly limits, profit targets, locking the settings and Manual Lockout, step by step with screenshots.",category:"Risk management",coverAlt:"Setting a daily stop in Tradovate",html:`
<p class="xlink">Trading through Rithmic? <a href="/blog/rithmic-daily-stop" data-link="rithmic-guide">See the guide to setting a daily stop in Rithmic</a></p>

<h2>What is a daily stop and why should you set one?</h2>
<p>A daily stop (<span class="en">Daily Loss Limit</span>) is one of the most important risk management tools in trading. Its purpose is to limit the cumulative loss during the trading day and prevent a negative trading day from turning into a significant loss that could hurt the account.</p>
<p>Unlike a <span class="en">Stop Loss</span> order, which is meant to limit the loss on a specific trade, a daily stop applies to the cumulative trading results on the account.</p>
<p>Even experienced traders can fall into a losing streak, make decisions out of frustration or try to win back losses with more trades. This is called <span class="en">Revenge Trading</span>.</p>
<p>Setting a daily stop in advance helps you keep trading discipline, cut down on impulsive decisions and protect your capital from unusual losses. And when you can't stop yourself, the system does it for you.</p>

<div class="box"><strong>What is Tradovate?</strong>Tradovate is a platform that connects the prop firm (for example Apex, Take Profit or Lucid) with the trading software you trade through, like TradingView or NinjaTrader. It's where you set the account's risk limits.</div>

<p>Tradovate has two risk management tools:</p>
<ol class="toc">
  <li><strong>Risk Settings:</strong> automatic loss and profit limits, daily and weekly.</li>
  <li><strong>Manual Lockout:</strong> manually locking trading for a time you choose.</li>
</ol>

<!-- ============ PART 1 ============ -->
<h2>Part 1: setting a daily stop in Risk Settings</h2>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Log in to Tradovate</h3>
<p>Log in to your Tradovate account and click <span class="en">Start Simulated Trading</span>. Prop firm accounts are in Simulation mode (the screen shows <span class="en">tradovate prop</span>).</p>
<figure><img src="${l.url}" alt="The Select a Trading Mode screen in Tradovate with the Start Simulated Trading button" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Open the platform settings</h3>
<p>Once you're in, click the settings icon (<span class="en">Application Settings</span>) in the top-right corner.</p>
<figure><img src="${h.url}" alt="The Tradovate trading screen with the settings icon in the top-right corner" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>Choose the account and open Risk Settings</h3>
<p>In the settings menu, go to the <span class="en">Accounts</span> tab. If several accounts appear, find the account you want to set the daily stop on and click <span class="en">Risk Settings</span> next to it.</p>
<figure><img src="${d.url}" alt="The Accounts tab in Tradovate's settings with the Risk Settings button" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">4</span>Turn on the daily loss limit and enter the stop</h3>
<p>Find <span class="en">Daily Loss Limit</span>, turn it on with the switch next to it, and enter the daily loss amount in dollars. For example, to limit the daily loss to $300, enter 300.</p>
<figure><img src="${c.url}" alt="The Risk Settings window in Tradovate with Daily Loss Limit, Weekly Loss Limit, Daily Profit Trigger, Weekly Profit Trigger and Prevent changes" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box warn"><strong>Important: how the stop is calculated</strong>According to the explanation on Tradovate's screen, the daily loss limit is calculated on the net profit and loss (<span class="en">Net P/L</span>) from the start of the session (5 pm CDT). It includes all commissions, as well as realized and open profit and loss, and it's checked in real time. In other words, an open trade that's in a loss counts too, and commissions bring you closer to the stop.</div>
<p>When you hit the limit, your positions are closed and trading on the account is locked until the end of the session. If Tradovate's standard rules are stricter than your settings, Tradovate's rules trigger first.</p>

<h3><span class="n">5</span>The other options in the window</h3>
<p>The same window has four more options, and it's worth knowing all of them:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Weekly Loss Limit</span>, a weekly loss limit:</strong> the maximum amount you're willing to lose in a week. When you reach it, your positions are closed and trading is locked until the start of next week.</li>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Daily Profit Trigger</span>, a daily profit target:</strong> when you reach the profit you set for the day, your positions are closed and trading is locked until the next day.</li>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Weekly Profit Trigger</span>, a weekly profit target:</strong> the same at the weekly level, locked until the start of next week.</li>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Prevent changes when trading is locked by a risk setting</span>:</strong> full explanation below. This is the setting you most don't want to miss.</li>
</ul>
<div class="box"><strong>A profit target: yes or no?</strong>A profit target is a bit tricky. It closes your positions the moment you reach it, so it can stop you from continuing a trade that's going your way. On the other hand, many traders make money, keep trading, lose the profit and end up blowing the account. If that happens to you, a profit target can help.</div>

<h3><span class="n">6</span>Locking the settings: Prevent changes</h3>
<p>According to the explanation on Tradovate's screen, after the account is locked you can remove the lock at any moment, simply by raising the amount or turning the limit off. In a moment of frustration, that's exactly the last thing you want to be possible.</p>
<p>When you turn on <span class="en">Prevent changes when trading is locked by a risk setting</span>, and one of the limits locks trading, the risk settings themselves are also locked until the end of the trading day (between 4 and 5 pm CDT). That way you can't "release" yourself in the middle of the day.</p>

<h3><span class="n">7</span>Save the settings</h3>
<p>Click <span class="en">Save</span>. Make sure the options you turned on stayed on and the amounts appear on the right account.</p>

<!-- ============ PART 2 ============ -->
<h2>Part 2: manual lock with Manual Lockout</h2>
<p>Sometimes you don't need to wait for the stop. A bad day, a few losses in a row, or just a feeling that you're not at your best, and you know it's better to stop. That's what <span class="en">Manual Lockout</span> is for.</p>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Click Manual Lockout</h3>
<p>The button is on the top bar of the trading screen, next to the account selection.</p>
<figure><img src="${u.url}" alt="The Manual Lockout button on Tradovate's top bar" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Choose how long to lock</h3>
<p>In the window that opens, choose the lock duration in <span class="en">Select lock time</span>: until the end of the trading day (<span class="en">End of trading day</span>), 15 minutes, 30 minutes, an hour, or another time of your choice (<span class="en">Custom</span>). Then click <span class="en">Lock trading</span>.</p>
<figure class="narrow"><img src="${p.url}" alt="The Lock trading for all simulation accounts window in Tradovate with the lock time selection" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box warn"><strong>Before you lock, note</strong>
  <ul>
    <li>The lock applies to <strong>all</strong> the Simulation accounts connected to the same user, not just the account shown.</li>
    <li>Open positions are closed immediately.</li>
    <li>You can't open new trades.</li>
    <li>Trading opens again only after the time you chose is up.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="box"><strong>My tip (Noam Antar, the author)</strong>What I always tell my students: set a daily stop, say $500, and your mission is never to reach it. That means if you've lost $400, you walk away from the computer. It's a hard mission, but if you stick to it, you'll improve a lot in everything related to risk management.</div>

<div class="cta">
  <h2>Looking for a prop firm?</h2>
  <p>On <a href="/comparison" data-link="comparison">AntarTrade</a> you'll find a comparison of the prop firms, a free trading journal, and more guides. And when buying evaluations, the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span>.</p>
</div>

<p class="disc">This guide is for information and instruction only and is not investment advice. Tradovate's settings and interface may change, and prop firms have their own risk rules. Before trading, make sure the settings were saved on the right account.</p>
`,faq:[]},W={title:"How to see where your account's stop (Drawdown) is in Tradovate",seoTitle:"How to See Where Your Account's Stop (Drawdown) Is in Tradovate | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"A short guide with screenshots: how to add the Accounts window in Tradovate and see your prop firm account's Drawdown level, the point where the account is blown.",excerpt:"It's a question I get a lot: you trade a prop firm account through Tradovate and want to know where your Drawdown is. In other words, the point where, if the account reaches it, it's blown. Here's how to add it to your screen in a few minutes.",category:"Risk management",coverAlt:"Where is my account's stop in Tradovate",html:`
<p class="lead">It's a question I get a lot: you trade a prop firm account through Tradovate and want to know where your Drawdown is. In other words, the point where, if the account reaches it, it's blown. Here's how to add it to your screen in a few minutes.</p>

<h2>Step by step</h2>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Go to the Tradovate website and click Login</h3>
<p>Go to <a href="https://www.tradovate.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tradovate.com</a> and click <span class="en">LOGIN</span> at the top.</p>
<figure><img src="${m.url}" alt="The Tradovate website with the LOGIN button" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Log in with the details from the prop firm</h3>
<p>Enter the username and password and click <span class="en">Login</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="${g.url}" alt="The Tradovate login screen with Username, Password and the Login button" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The username is hidden in the picture.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="box"><strong>Where do you find the login details?</strong>You get the Tradovate username and password from the prop firm. For example, if you have an account with Apex, you'll find them in Apex's dashboard on their website. It works the same at the other prop firms that work with Tradovate: the details are waiting for you in your personal area on the firm's website.</div>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>Choose Simulation</h3>
<p>On the <span class="en">Select a Trading Mode</span> screen, click <span class="en">Start Simulated Trading</span>. Prop firm accounts are in Simulation mode.</p>
<figure><img src="${f.url}" alt="The Select a Trading Mode screen in Tradovate with the Start Simulated Trading button" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">4</span>Click the plus</h3>
<p>On the trading screen, click the plus button in the top-left corner.</p>
<figure><img src="${y.url}" alt="The plus button in the top-left corner of Tradovate" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">5</span>Drag Accounts onto the screen</h3>
<p>A window opens with all the tools you can add. Find <span class="en">Accounts</span>, and instead of clicking it, drag it to an empty spot on the screen and let go. Wherever you drop it, that's where it stays.</p>
<figure><img src="${w.url}" alt="The Drag &amp; Drop to Place on Dashboard window in Tradovate with the Accounts tool" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box"><strong>Note</strong>Don't click the tool, drag it. You only do this once, and the window stays on the screen next time too.</div>

<h3><span class="n">6</span>Open the settings: Select Columns</h3>
<p>The <span class="en">Accounts</span> window now shows your accounts. At the top of the window there's a settings icon (<span class="en">View Settings</span>). Click it and choose <span class="en">Select Columns</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="${b.url}" alt="The settings icon in the Accounts window in Tradovate" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">7</span>Add the Drawdown column and save</h3>
<p>The window that opens has two lists: <span class="en">Available</span> (columns you can add) and <span class="en">Visible</span> (columns that are shown). Find <span class="en">Drawdown Auto Liq Level</span> in the list and drag it to the <span class="en">Visible</span> side, next to <span class="en">Account</span>. Then click <span class="en">Save</span>.</p>
<figure class="narrow"><img src="${v.url}" alt="The column selection window in Tradovate with Drawdown Auto Liq Level and the Save button" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">8</span>That's the account's stop</h3>
<p>The <span class="en">Accounts</span> window now shows the <span class="en">Drawdown Auto</span> column. The amount in it is the Drawdown level: if the account reaches this amount, it's blown.</p>
<figure><img src="${k.url}" alt="The Accounts window in Tradovate with the Drawdown Auto column" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The account numbers are hidden in the picture.</figcaption></figure>

<div class="box"><strong>My tip (Noam Antar, the author)</strong>This is the point you must never reach. It's worth looking at it before every trading day, and setting yourself a daily stop that leaves a margin from it.</div>

<p>Want the system to stop you before you get there? Here's <a href="/blog/tradovate-daily-stop" data-link="tradovate-guide">the guide to setting a daily stop in Tradovate</a>.</p>

<div class="cta">
  <h2>Looking for a prop firm?</h2>
  <p>On <a href="/comparison" data-link="comparison">AntarTrade</a> you'll find a comparison of the prop firms, a free trading journal and more guides. And when buying evaluations, the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span>.</p>
</div>

<p class="disc">This guide is for information and instruction only. Drawdown rules vary between prop firms and account types, and on accounts with a trailing Drawdown the level can move. Check the binding rules with your prop firm.</p>
`,faq:[{q:"What is the Drawdown on a prop firm account?",a:"The Drawdown is the minimum amount the account can drop to. If the balance reaches that amount, the account is blown and closed."},{q:"Where do you see the Drawdown in Tradovate?",a:"Add the Accounts window to the screen, and in its settings (Select Columns) add the Drawdown Auto Liq Level column. The amount in it is the account's Drawdown level."},{q:"Where do you find the Tradovate username and password?",a:"In your personal area on the prop firm's website. For example, on an Apex account the details are in Apex's dashboard."},{q:"Can the Drawdown amount change?",a:"Yes. On accounts with a trailing Drawdown (Trailing), the level rises as the account makes money. That's why it's worth checking it before every trading day."},{q:"Do you have to add the Accounts window every time?",a:"No. Once you drag it onto the screen and save the column, it stays there next time you log in."}]},M={title:"How to withdraw money from the buffer at TakeProfit: closing the account and withdrawing, step by step",seoTitle:"How to Withdraw from the Buffer at TakeProfit: Closing the Account and Withdrawing | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"A guide with screenshots: how to close an account at TakeProfit and withdraw 50% of the profits inside the buffer, when it's worth it, and what the rules are.",excerpt:"At TakeProfit you can only withdraw what's above the buffer. But there's an option many traders don't know: closing the account and withdrawing part of the money inside the buffer.",category:"Payouts",coverAlt:"Withdrawing money from the buffer at TakeProfit",html:`
<p class="lead">At TakeProfit you can only withdraw what's above the buffer. But there's an option many traders don't know: closing the account and withdrawing part of the money inside the buffer. In this article I explain the rules, when it can be useful, and show with screenshots how I actually did it.</p>

<h2>What the buffer is at TakeProfit</h2>
<p>The buffer is a safety cushion you have to build in the account before you start withdrawing. For example, on a $50,000 account the buffer is $2,000: you need to reach $52,000, and only what's above that amount can be withdrawn.</p>
<p>In other words, if the account stands at $51,000, you have nothing to withdraw in a regular payout. That $1,000 is inside the buffer.</p>

<h2>The rules: a regular payout vs. a withdrawal from the buffer</h2>
<h3>A regular payout</h3>
<p>You withdraw only what's above the buffer, with an 80/20 profit split: 80% to you and 20% to the firm. The account keeps running as usual.</p>

<h3>A withdrawal from the buffer: only when closing the account</h3>
<p>According to TakeProfit's rules, a trader may also withdraw profits from inside the buffer, but only after closing the account. In that case you get <strong>50%</strong> of the profits inside the buffer.</p>
<p>There used to be an 80% tier after 60 trading days, but it was removed. TakeProfit's official article was updated on 9.10.2026.</p>
<div class="box warn"><strong>Note</strong>Closing the account is final. Once you close it you can't trade it, reset it or access it again. Check the current rules on TakeProfit's website before you decide.</div>

<h2>When it's useful: my story</h2>
<p>I had a TakeProfit account that was above the buffer, at about $52,000. On a travel day, on the way to the airport, I decided to trade from my phone. In hindsight that was a mistake. I entered a trade, the market moved fast against me, and I got out with a big $1,578 loss.</p>
<p>The account dropped to $50,422. In other words, I had $422 left inside the buffer, which was also my distance from the account's stop.</p>
<p>I had two options: fight to save the account with $422 of room, or close it and take half of the $422. I have other accounts, so I had no reason to work hard to save this particular one. I closed it and got $211 into my wallet.</p>
<div class="box"><strong>My tip (Noam Antar, the author)</strong>If you were already high in the account and it came down, and you feel you're on your way to blowing it, you can stop while it's still in profit, close it and withdraw part of that profit. It's better to take half of the profit than to blow the account and get nothing.</div>

<h2>How to close an account and withdraw, step by step</h2>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Click Close account</h3>
<p>In TakeProfit's dashboard (<span class="en">Control Center</span>), choose the account and click <span class="en">Close account</span> on the right side of the account card.</p>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/takeprofit-buffer/tp-01-account.webp" alt="The account card in TakeProfit's dashboard with the Close account button" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The account and login details are hidden in the picture.</figcaption></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Confirm the closing</h3>
<p>A message appears warning that after closing you won't be able to reset the account, trade it or access it, and that the action can't be undone. If you've decided, click <span class="en">YES</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/takeprofit-buffer/tp-02-confirm.webp" alt="The confirmation window for closing a PRO account at TakeProfit with the NO and YES buttons" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>A Withdraw button appears</h3>
<p>After closing, a <span class="en">Withdraw</span> button appears instead of <span class="en">Close account</span>, and the account card shows how much is available to withdraw (<span class="en">Available for Withdrawal</span>). For me it was $422.</p>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/takeprofit-buffer/tp-03-withdraw-btn.webp" alt="The account card after closing with Available for Withdrawal and the Withdraw button" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">4</span>If an error appears, wait until the next day</h3>
<p>When I tried to withdraw right after closing, I got a message that withdrawals are unavailable while the trading session is active. As with regular payouts at TakeProfit, the system updates the day after. I came back the next day, and the withdrawal went through.</p>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/takeprofit-buffer/tp-04-error.webp" alt="The error message Withdrawals are unavailable while your trading session is active" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">5</span>Enter the amount and confirm</h3>
<p>In the <span class="en">Withdraw</span> window, enter the amount you want to withdraw. You don't have to withdraw it all, and you can also withdraw later. The window shows the profit split and the amount you'll receive. For me: a $422 withdrawal, of which $211 went to me, that is 50%. Click <span class="en">Confirm &amp; Withdraw</span>.</p>
<figure class="narrow"><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/takeprofit-buffer/tp-05-withdraw.webp" alt="The Withdraw window at TakeProfit: a withdrawal amount of 422, the profit split and the 211 received" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">6</span>The money is in the wallet</h3>
<p>Go to the wallet, and you'll see the deposit there. From there you withdraw it like any regular payout from TakeProfit.</p>
<figure><img src="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/blog/takeprofit-buffer/tp-06-wallet.webp" alt="The TakeProfit wallet with a $211 deposit in the history" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The wallet balance and wallet number are hidden in the picture.</figcaption></figure>

<div class="cta">
  <h2>Buying a TakeProfit account?</h2>
  <p>On the <a href="/take-profit" data-link="takeprofit">TakeProfit page</a> you'll find all the rules and prices. And when buying evaluations, the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span> gives you the highest discount.</p>
</div>

<p class="disc">This article is based on the process as I actually carried it out, at the time of writing, and is for information only. It should not be seen as financial advice. TakeProfit's rules, percentages and processing times may change, so check the current terms on the firm's website before closing an account.</p>
`,faq:[{q:"Can you withdraw from the buffer without closing the account?",a:"No. In a regular payout you withdraw only what's above the buffer. A withdrawal from inside the buffer is possible only after closing the account."},{q:"How much will I get if I close the account?",a:"According to TakeProfit's rules, 50% of the profits inside the buffer. The 80% tier after 60 trading days was removed (the official article was updated on 9.10.2026)."},{q:"Can you reopen a closed account?",a:"No. Closing is final, and you can't reset the account or trade it again."},{q:"Why did I get an error when I tried to withdraw?",a:"If the message says withdrawals are unavailable while the trading session is active, you need to wait until the next day, when the system updates."},{q:"When is it worth closing an account and withdrawing from the buffer?",a:"For example when the account came down from a high, is still in profit, and you don't want to keep fighting for it. Instead of risking it until it's blown, you can close it and take part of the profit."}]},e="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/images/blog/atas-guide",_={title:"How to use ATAS: a guide from installation to the heatmap, copy trading and replay",seoTitle:"How to Use ATAS: A Step-by-Step Guide with Screenshots | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"How to work with ATAS, step by step: which plan and which data to buy, installation and connection, footprint, indicators, trading from the chart, templates, the heatmap, copy trading and replay.",excerpt:"The written version of my ATAS tutorial video: plans and data, installation and connection, the chart and footprint, trading from the chart, templates, the Heatmap, copy trading and Replay. With marked screenshots.",category:"Guides",coverAlt:"An ATAS guide from installation to the heatmap: Heatmap, Footprint, Market Profile and Order Flow",html:`
<p class="xlink">Looking for current prices or the discount code? <a href="/atas-guide" data-link="atas-guide">See the full ATAS page</a></p>

<p class="lead"><span class="en">ATAS</span> is the software that changed my trading the most. It shows what happens inside every candle: who bought, who sold, and where the big orders are waiting. This guide is the written version of my tutorial video, arranged in the order you'll need things: which plan and which data to buy, how to install and connect, how to work with the chart, and which advanced tools are worth knowing. In every screenshot I marked in blue where to click.</p>

<div class="box"><strong>Before you start, in short</strong>
  <ul>
    <li>To trade futures you need <span class="en">ATAS</span> on the <span class="en">Pro</span> or <span class="en">Ultra</span> plan.</li>
    <li>Without real-time data the software is worthless. Buy <span class="en">dxFeed</span> data, or connect a prop firm's <span class="en">Rithmic</span> account.</li>
    <li>The software runs on <span class="en">Windows</span>. There's a way to run it on a <span class="en">Mac</span> too, and the explanation is on the <span class="en">ATAS</span> website.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<ol class="toc">
  <li><strong>Which plan and which data</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Installation and connection</strong></li>
  <li><strong>The chart: top bar, drawing tools and indicators</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Chart and footprint settings</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Trading from the chart</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Templates and Workspace</strong></li>
  <li><strong>The advanced tools: the heatmap, copy trading and replay</strong></li>
</ol>

<!-- ============ PART 1 ============ -->
<h2>Part 1: which plan and which data</h2>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Choose a plan: Pro or Ultra</h3>
<p><span class="en">ATAS</span> has four plans: the free <span class="en">Start</span>, <span class="en">Plus</span>, <span class="en">Pro</span> and <span class="en">Ultra</span>. You can pay monthly, for three months, yearly or for a lifetime license, and the longer the period, the lower the monthly price. There's a refund within 14 days of the first purchase, so you can try it with no risk. In my opinion three months is great for a first try, and anyone who already knows they like the software will save with a year or a lifetime license.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/01-pricing-plans.webp" alt="The ATAS pricing page: the Start, Plus, Pro and Ultra plans, with Pro and Ultra marked" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p><span class="en">Start</span> and <span class="en">Plus</span> aren't relevant for anyone trading futures. The <span class="en">ATAS</span> pricing page says that on them futures data is delayed by 15 minutes, and you can't connect real-time data.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/02-start-plus-delay.webp" alt="The ATAS comparison table: on Start and Plus, futures data is delayed by 15 minutes" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>The difference between <span class="en">Pro</span> and <span class="en">Ultra</span> is mainly more indicators and tools. <span class="en">Ultra</span> also includes the <span class="en">MBO Bundle</span>, which shows icebergs and stops, among other things. I work with <span class="en">Ultra</span>, and in my opinion anyone who wants to be a professional won't stop at the difference. You'll always find the current prices and the discount code on the <a href="/atas-guide#pricing" data-link="atas-guide">ATAS page</a>.</p>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Buy data: dxFeed or Rithmic</h3>
<p>Without real-time data you'll see everything delayed, and that's worthless. There are two main ways:</p>
<p><strong>dxFeed:</strong> on the <span class="en">ATAS</span> help page, search for "<span class="en">Connecting to dxFeed</span>", and go from there to the order page. Choose <span class="en">Non-Professional</span>, otherwise the price is much higher. For futures like <span class="en">NQ</span> and <span class="en">ES</span>, choose <span class="en">CME Market Depth</span>, which includes both <span class="en">Level 1</span> and <span class="en">Level 2</span> (market depth). At the time of recording it cost $39 a month.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/04-dxfeed-cme.webp" alt="The dxFeed order page with CME Market Depth marked" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p><strong>Rithmic through a prop firm:</strong> buy an evaluation from a prop firm that works with <span class="en">Rithmic</span>, and connect it to <span class="en">ATAS</span>. At <span class="en">Take Profit Trader</span>, for example, you choose <span class="en">Rithmic Data Feed</span> at the platform selection step.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/05-takeprofit-rithmic.webp" alt="The platform selection step at Take Profit Trader, with Rithmic Data Feed marked" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>After buying, send them a message asking them to turn on the <span class="en">Level 2</span> data (<span class="en">Depth of Market</span>). According to what their support rep told me at the time of recording, one exchange is included in the evaluation, and <span class="en">CME</span> covers both <span class="en">NQ</span> and <span class="en">ES</span>. An extra exchange cost $15 then. The advantage: you have an evaluation you can trade, not just data. Another advantage: according to the explanation in the video, the <span class="en">MBO Bundle</span> of the <span class="en">Ultra</span> plan works only with <span class="en">Rithmic</span> data.</p>
<div class="box"><strong>Discount code</strong>At <span class="en">Take Profit Trader</span>, the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span> gives the highest discount available at that moment. The full details, including how to connect the account to <span class="en">ATAS</span>, are on the <a href="/atas-guide#steps" data-link="atas-guide">ATAS page</a>.</div>

<!-- ============ PART 2 ============ -->
<h2>Part 2: installing and connecting ATAS</h2>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Download and install</h3>
<p>After buying, go to your personal area on the <span class="en">ATAS</span> website, and on the left choose <span class="en">Download ATAS</span>. The installation is completely standard: <span class="en">Next</span>, <span class="en">Next</span>, <span class="en">Finish</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/03-download.webp" alt="The download page in the ATAS personal area, with the Download ATAS for Windows button marked" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Log in for the first time</h3>
<p>On the login screen, type the username and password you registered with at <span class="en">ATAS</span>. The first time, leave the <span class="en">Workspace</span> as it is. When choosing the version, most people choose <span class="en">Latest</span>, the stable and popular version. I work with <span class="en">Beta</span> because I like trying new things, but a beta version can have bugs.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/06-login-latest.webp" alt="The ATAS login screen with the Latest version selection marked" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box warn"><strong>Can't connect?</strong>Security software (antivirus) can block the first connection. Turn it off for a moment, connect to <span class="en">ATAS</span>, and turn it back on. From the second time on, everything connects normally.</div>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>Connect the data</h3>
<p>In the main window, click <span class="en">Connections</span>, then <span class="en">Add</span> (1). Choose what you bought, <span class="en">dxFeed</span> or <span class="en">Rithmic</span>, and type the username and password you got by email, or from the prop firm. For a demo connection, add <span class="en">ATAS Sim</span>. In the <span class="en">Auto connect</span> column (2), choose what connects by itself when the software opens. The prop firm account, for example, can be left without auto-connect and connected manually.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/07-connections.webp" alt="The Connections window in ATAS: 1 the Add button, 2 the Auto connect column" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<figure><img src="${e}/08-connection-rithmic.webp" alt="Adding a Rithmic connection in ATAS: 1 the username, password and server fields, 2 the Finish button" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<!-- ============ PART 3 ============ -->
<h2>Part 3: the chart</h2>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Open a chart</h3>
<p>Click <span class="en">Chart</span> in the main window (1). You can open a regular chart, or choose one of the ready-made charts (2), like <span class="en">Footprint chart</span>, <span class="en">Imbalance Chart</span> or <span class="en">TPO Chart</span>. Choose the contract, for example <span class="en">NQ</span> or <span class="en">ES</span>, and always check <span class="en">Continuous</span>, so the chart rolls from contract to contract by itself. When you zoom in on the chart, the candles open into footprint.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/09-new-chart.webp" alt="Opening a chart in ATAS: 1 the Chart button, 2 the list of ready-made charts" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>The chart's top bar</h3>
<p>From left to right it has:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Chart linking:</strong> choose the same color on two charts (for example 5 minutes and 15 minutes), and they sync.</li>
  <li><strong>The contract:</strong> switch here to oil, gold or any other contract.</li>
  <li><strong>The timeframe:</strong> 5 minutes, an hour, a day. The small arrow has more options: seconds, ticks, volume, delta, <span class="en">Range</span>, <span class="en">Renko</span> and more.</li>
</ul>
<p>To show more days back, uncheck <span class="en">Auto</span> (2) and choose how many days. In the same bar you also choose the number of contracts for trading from the chart, and the display type: candles, bars or a line.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/10-timeframe-days.webp" alt="Choosing a timeframe in ATAS: 1 the timeframe types, 2 the Auto box for the number of days" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>The drawing tools</h3>
<p>In the bar on the left, or under the pencil in the top bar, you'll find all the drawing tools: lines (1), a trendline (<span class="en">F2</span>), Fibonacci, shapes and more. At the bottom there's a magnet, which sets how much a drawing snaps to the candles. Every tool has its own settings, and it's worth going through them one by one.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/11-drawing-tools.webp" alt="The drawing tools menu in ATAS: 1 lines, 2 Long Position" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p><strong>Long Position and Short Position</strong> (2, <span class="en">Ctrl+L</span> and <span class="en">Ctrl+S</span>): double-clicking the tool opens its settings. Set an account size (for example 50,000) and what percentage to risk on each trade (for example 1%), and the tool shows how many contracts you can take based on the stop distance. In the example in the video, a 25-point stop on <span class="en">NQ</span> is one contract, that is $500, one percent of the account. Save the settings as a template and check <span class="en">Global</span>, and they'll work on every chart.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/12-long-position.webp" alt="The Long Position tool on an ATAS chart, with a target, a stop and the number of contracts" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Two more useful tools:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Volume Profile for a specific area:</strong> found under <span class="en">Volume Analysis</span>. On my setup it's on the <span class="en">F3</span> key. Stretch it over an area, and a double-click opens the settings for the colors, the <span class="en">POC</span> and the <span class="en">Value Area</span>.</li>
  <li><strong>The magnifying glass</strong> (<span class="en">Magnifier</span>): move it over a candle and see its footprint, without zooming.</li>
</ul>

<h3><span class="n">4</span>Indicators</h3>
<p>Click <span class="en">Indicators</span> in the chart's top bar, choose an indicator from the list (1) and click <span class="en">Apply</span> (2).</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/13-indicators.webp" alt="The indicators window in ATAS: 1 the list of indicators, 2 the Apply button" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>A few indicators I recommend:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bar Timer:</strong> how much time is left until the candle closes. You can switch it to the current time.</li>
  <li><strong>Stacked Imbalance:</strong> areas with several imbalance levels in a row. In the settings you set the ratio, for example 300%.</li>
  <li><strong>Big Trades:</strong> one of the most powerful tools. It marks big trades on the chart (1).
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Threshold:</strong> <span class="en">Auto Filter</span> adapts itself to the volume, at <span class="en">Strong</span>, <span class="en">Medium</span> or <span class="en">Weak</span> levels. You can also set a manual threshold, for example above 80 contracts.</li>
      <li><strong>Display:</strong> in the display settings you choose circles or squares. If you uncheck <span class="en">Show above chart</span> (2), the markers move behind the candle. <span class="en">Show value</span> shows the number of contracts inside each circle.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Volume Profile:</strong> a daily or weekly volume profile, with a <span class="en">POC</span> and <span class="en">Value Area</span>.</li>
  <li><strong>Delta</strong> and <strong>Depth Of Market</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>To hide an indicator you don't want to see right now, click its eye in the list in the top-left corner of the chart.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/14-big-trades.webp" alt="Big Trades in ATAS: 1 the big trades on the chart, 2 Show above chart" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">5</span>The right side: market depth and the trading panel</h3>
<p>The <span class="en">Depth of Market</span> is shown on the price axis: how many contracts are waiting in limit orders at each price. In the example in the video, 66 contracts were waiting to buy below the price. It saves you the space of a separate <span class="en">DOM</span>. In the bottom-right corner there's a <span class="en">Trading Panel</span> for trading from the chart (part 5), a <span class="en">DOM Trader</span>, and a date picker for jumping to a certain point on the chart. The scale button in the top bar sets how big the numbers in the footprint are shown.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/15-footprint.webp" alt="An ATAS footprint chart zoomed in, with Bid x Ask at every price level and the market depth on the axis" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<!-- ============ PART 4 ============ -->
<h2>Part 4: chart and footprint settings</h2>
<p>Right-click the chart and then <span class="en">Settings</span>, or the gear in the top-right corner. There are four tabs (1): <span class="en">Visual Settings</span>, <span class="en">Footprint settings</span>, <span class="en">Trading Settings</span> and <span class="en">Templates</span>.</p>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Visual Settings: how the chart looks</h3>
<p>Here you change the background color (2), the colors of <span class="en">Open</span>, <span class="en">High</span>, <span class="en">Low</span> and <span class="en">Close</span>, the crosshair, the current price line and the colors of the candles and their outlines. There's no right or wrong here: everyone picks the colors that are comfortable for their eyes.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/16-visual-settings.webp" alt="Visual Settings in ATAS: 1 the tabs, 2 the background color" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Footprint settings: what you see inside the candle</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What's shown inside the candle:</strong> <span class="en">Bid x Ask</span>, volume or delta. I work with <span class="en">Bid x Ask</span>.</li>
  <li><strong>Border type:</strong> it's worth switching to <span class="en">Candle</span>, so you see the candle itself around the footprint.</li>
  <li><strong>Maximum Level (1):</strong> the level with the most activity in the candle, that is the candle's <span class="en">POC</span>. You can set it by volume or by delta, and give it a border so it stands out.</li>
  <li><strong>Value Area (2):</strong> the area where 70% of the candle's volume traded. It's worth giving it a color.</li>
  <li><strong>The imbalance ratio:</strong> for example 150% or 300%.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="${e}/17-footprint-settings.webp" alt="Footprint settings in ATAS: 1 Maximum Level, 2 the Value Area color" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>Trading Settings and cloning a window</h3>
<p>In <span class="en">Trading Settings</span> you choose whether to see the markers of the trades you took (<span class="en">Trades</span>) and of your orders on the chart. If you don't want to see them, choose <span class="en">Hidden</span>. And another tip: right-click the chart and then <span class="en">Clone window</span> opens a copy of the exact same chart, with all the settings. Change its timeframe, and you have the same chart on 5 minutes and on 15 minutes.</p>

<!-- ============ PART 5 ============ -->
<h2>Part 5: trading from the chart</h2>
<p>Open the <span class="en">Trading Panel</span> in the bottom-right corner of the chart. At the top of the panel, choose the account. In my live streams I hide it, so people don't see which account I'm trading.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/18-trading-panel.webp" alt="The ATAS Trading Panel: 1 the buy and sell buttons, 2 SL/TP, 3 Close" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The buy and sell buttons (1):</strong>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="en">Buy Market</span> and <span class="en">Sell Market</span> enter immediately, at the market price.</li>
      <li><span class="en">Buy Bid</span> places a limit order on the nearest bid, and <span class="en">Sell Ask</span> on the nearest ask.</li>
      <li>You can also trade with a right-click on the chart: <span class="en">Buy Limit</span>, <span class="en">Sell Stop</span> and so on.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>SL/TP (2):</strong> preset a stop and target in ticks, points or percentages, and save them as a template. On <span class="en">NQ</span>, 4 ticks are one point. After the entry, the stop and target appear on the chart by themselves, and you can drag them with the mouse.</li>
  <li><strong>OCO:</strong> when one of the orders fills, the other is canceled.</li>
  <li><strong>Close (3):</strong> closes everything. <span class="en">Cancel All</span> cancels all orders.</li>
</ul>
<p>When the trade is in profit, one click on <span class="en">Breakeven</span> moves the stop to the entry price.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/19-breakeven.webp" alt="The Breakeven button in the ATAS Trading Panel, during an open trade" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<!-- ============ PART 6 ============ -->
<h2>Part 6: templates and Workspace</h2>
<p>This is the most confusing part, so in short:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>An indicator template:</strong> in an indicator's settings, for example <span class="en">Big Trades</span>, click <span class="en">Save as template</span> and give it a name. Next time, choose the saved settings from the list, and you can keep several versions of the same indicator. The same works for drawing tools, like <span class="en">Long Position</span> and Fibonacci.</li>
  <li><strong>A chart template:</strong> saves the whole screen. Right-click, <span class="en">Settings</span>, the <span class="en">Templates</span> tab, then <span class="en">Save a template with the current settings</span>. In the window that opens, choose what to save (1): the chart settings, the drawings, the indicators and the timeframe. Give it a name and click <span class="en">Save</span> (2). On another chart, choose the template in the same tab and click <span class="en">Apply</span>. With the <span class="en">Import</span> button (3) you import a template you received.</li>
  <li><strong>Workspace:</strong> the whole window arrangement: which screen each chart is on, the <span class="en">Heatmap</span> and the tabs. That way, when you open the software, everything opens the way you arranged it. You manage it with the <span class="en">Workspaces</span> button in the main window, where you can also save, export and import.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="${e}/20-save-template.webp" alt="Saving a chart template in ATAS: 1 what to save, 2 Save, 3 Import" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<!-- ============ PART 7 ============ -->
<h2>Part 7: the advanced tools</h2>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>The main window</h3>
<p>The <span class="en">ATAS</span> main window is where you open everything from:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Chart:</strong> a new chart.</li>
  <li><strong>Smart Tape:</strong> the list of trades in real time. Next to it there's also a <span class="en">Bid/Ask Tape</span>.</li>
  <li><strong>Heatmap:</strong> the heatmap.</li>
  <li><strong>Connections:</strong> the data connections.</li>
  <li><strong>Workspaces:</strong> the window arrangements.</li>
  <li><strong>Following Manager:</strong> copy trading.</li>
  <li><strong>Replay:</strong> trading on historical data.</li>
</ol>
<p>There are more tools there, like <span class="en">Watchlist</span>, <span class="en">Smart DOM</span>, <span class="en">Options Board</span> and alerts. You can drag any window and dock it to another screen or another side.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/21-main-window.webp" alt="The ATAS main window bar, with the important tools numbered" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>The heatmap</h3>
<p>The <span class="en">Heatmap</span> shows the order book over time. Strong bands are places where many limit orders sit, and on the side you see how many contracts are at each price. The bubbles are market orders that came in. In the settings you choose the color palette, and the filter level (how much noise to hide). You can also choose whether the bubbles are shown by volume or by delta, and there's even a <span class="en">3D</span> view.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/22-heatmap.webp" alt="The ATAS Heatmap on NQ: bands of limit orders and bubbles of market orders" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>Copy trading with the Following Manager</h3>
<p>Open the <span class="en">Following Manager</span>, then <span class="en">Add Copy Group</span>:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Choose the leader account (1).</li>
  <li>Set the copy mode (2). I recommend <span class="en">Orders</span> rather than <span class="en">Positions</span>. With <span class="en">Orders</span> the order itself is copied the moment you place it, and with <span class="en">Positions</span> copying starts only when a position opens, and then another account may miss the entry.</li>
  <li>It's worth turning on <span class="en">Notifications</span>.</li>
  <li>Add the accounts that will copy (3). For each account set a <span class="en">Ratio</span>: a ratio of 2 means one contract on the leader account and two on this account. <span class="en">Reverse</span> flips the direction.</li>
</ol>
<figure><img src="${e}/23-copy-trading.webp" alt="Adding a copy trading group in ATAS: 1 the leader account, 2 the copy mode, 3 the copying accounts" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">4</span>Replay: trading on historical data</h3>
<p>In <span class="en">Replay</span> you connect to a special replay connection, choose a date range (1) and the contract (2), and click <span class="en">Play</span>. The candles run as if in real time. Choose a speed, pause at any moment (3), and you can even open a trade and see what would have happened. It's excellent for backtesting and practice.</p>
<figure><img src="${e}/24-replay.webp" alt="The Replay window in ATAS: 1 the date range, 2 the contract, 3 the speed and pause" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box warn"><strong>Can't trade after Replay?</strong>After you stop and exit <span class="en">Replay</span>, sometimes you can't go back to trading on the real account. Go back to the <span class="en">Replay</span> window, click the green button to reconnect it, and then you can connect to your accounts and trade again.</div>

<h3><span class="n">5</span>General settings and hotkeys</h3>
<p>In the top-right corner of the main window there are general settings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sounds and time zone.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Commissions.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>The Value Area percentage:</strong> the default is 70%.</li>
  <li><strong>Hot Keys:</strong> this is where you set keyboard shortcuts. Choose a category, for example <span class="en">Trading</span> (1), and an action, for example <span class="en">Buy Market</span> (2), and press the combination you want, for example <span class="en">Ctrl+M</span>. That way you enter a trade, cancel orders or open Fibonacci with one key.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="${e}/25-hotkeys.webp" alt="The Hot Keys window in ATAS: 1 the Trading category, 2 the Buy Market action" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h2>To wrap up</h2>
<p><span class="en">ATAS</span> is big, complicated software, and even I don't know every corner of it. But anyone who knows the things in this guide is already working with it the right way. Like anything, you need to play with it and practice. And remember: trading is hard and risky, but possible, if you treat it like a profession and learn it properly.</p>

<div class="cta">
  <h2>Ready to start?</h2>
  <p>On the <a href="/atas-guide" data-link="atas-guide">ATAS page</a> you'll find the current prices, the discount code, and the guide to connecting a <span class="en">Take Profit Trader</span> account through <span class="en">Rithmic</span>.</p>
</div>

<p class="disc">This guide is for information and instruction only and is not investment advice. Prices, discounts and terms of ATAS, the data providers and the prop firms are correct as of the video's recording and may change. Trading futures involves risk.</p>
`,faq:[{q:"What is ATAS?",a:"ATAS is trading and Order Flow analysis software. It shows what happens inside every candle (footprint), the order book (Heatmap and market depth), big trades and more. Futures traders, on NQ and ES for example, use it both to analyze and to trade directly from the chart."},{q:"Which ATAS plan do you need for futures?",a:"Pro or Ultra. On Start and Plus, futures data is delayed by 15 minutes, and you can't connect real-time data. Ultra has more tools, including the MBO Bundle."},{q:"What data do you need for ATAS?",a:"One of two ways: buy dxFeed data (for CME contracts choose CME Market Depth as Non-Professional), or connect a prop firm's Rithmic account and ask the firm to turn on Level 2."},{q:"Can you connect a prop firm account to ATAS?",a:"Yes, if the firm works with Rithmic. At Take Profit Trader, for example, you choose Rithmic when buying, and connect the account in Connections. The full explanation is on the ATAS page on the site."},{q:"What's the difference between a template and a Workspace in ATAS?",a:"A template saves how one chart looks: the indicators, colors and settings. A Workspace saves the whole window arrangement: which screen each chart is on, the Heatmap and the tabs."},{q:"Why can't I trade on my real account after Replay?",a:"Go back to the Replay window, click the green button to reconnect it, and then connect to your accounts again. After that you can trade as usual."}]},z={title:"TradingView guide: how to use TradingView, area by area",seoTitle:"TradingView Guide: How to Use TradingView, Area by Area | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"A written guide to TradingView: watchlists, alerts, chart settings, bar replay, indicators, drawing tools, the long position tool that calculates your size, and a demo account.",excerpt:"Every area of the TradingView screen: watchlists and alerts, the top toolbar, the drawing tools and the tool that calculates your size, and the demo account.",category:"Guides",coverAlt:"A TradingView guide, area by area",html:`
<p class="lead"><span class="en">TradingView</span> is the most popular charting software in the world, and in my opinion also the best. It has everything: charts, indicators, watchlists, alerts, fundamental data, a demo account and even trading. In this guide I'll go through the software with you by area of the screen: the right side, the top toolbar, the left toolbar and the bottom panel. That way you'll know exactly where everything is.</p>

<div class="box">The plans, connecting a prop firm account and the FAQ are on the <a href="/tradingview-guide">TradingView page</a>.</div>

<h2>Signing up: free or paid?</h2>
<p>Personally I recommend a subscription, but it isn't a must. <strong>There's a free version,</strong> it's just missing a lot of things, and it has tons of ads that are really annoying. You can start for free, and if you see you're into it and you like it, upgrade.</p>
<ul>
<li>Sign up through the link on our <a href="/tradingview-guide">TradingView page</a>, for example with your email. You'll get an email with a link to verify the account.</li>
<li>Choose a plan. The paid plans have a <strong>30-day trial.</strong> You need to enter a phone number and card details, and if you don't want to pay in the end, cancel before you're charged.</li>
<li>After logging in, scroll down on the home page and click the button that opens a <strong>full-screen chart.</strong> Everything in this guide starts there.</li>
</ul>
<div class="box tool"><strong>🎁 A perk for signing up through us</strong>Anyone who signs up through our link gets <strong>$15 off the next plan,</strong> after the first purchase. The discount doesn't apply to the first purchase itself. And if the purchase goes through the link, it also supports the site, at no extra cost to you.<a class="btn" href="/tradingview-guide">To the TradingView page</a></div>

<h2>First settings: the profile menu</h2>
<p>In the top-left corner there's a circle with the first letter of your name. Click it, and there are three settings worth setting up right at the start:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dark mode:</strong> dark or light, whatever suits you. Personally I like dark.</li>
<li><strong>Drawings panel:</strong> show or hide it.</li>
<li><strong>Language:</strong> the interface language.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How to search for a stock or a contract</h3>
<p>In the search box in the top toolbar, type what you're looking for, for example Tesla. There's also a faster way: <strong>just stand on the chart and start typing,</strong> for example "Apple". The search window opens by itself, and you don't need to click anything.</p>

<h2>The right side: watchlist, data and alerts</h2>
<h3>Watchlist</h3>
<p>In the right icon bar, open the watchlist, and create a new list with your own name. Add symbols by clicking <strong>Add symbol</strong>, searching and clicking the plus.</p>
<p>To keep things organized, add <strong>sections</strong> and give them names: for example a "Tech" section with Apple, a "Chips" section with Intel and Nvidia, and an "Airlines" section with American Airlines. Next to each symbol you see the last price, the change and the change in percent. Under the three dots you can choose more columns, like volume.</p>

<h3>Fundamental data</h3>
<p>When you stand on a symbol in the list, you can drag the bottom part of the panel up, and see <a href="/blog/technical-vs-fundamental-analysis">fundamental data</a>: revenue, earnings, dividends, reports and statistics. Clicking "More" gives you a full picture of the company. If it doesn't interest you, just drag it back down, and it hides.</p>

<h3>Alerts</h3>
<p>The alarm clock icon on the right shows all your alerts. The fastest way to add an alert: hover the mouse over the price you want, for example 22, click <strong>the plus next to the price scale,</strong> and choose "Add alert".</p>
<p>Double-click the alert to choose the condition: the price (or a moving average, or anything else) crossing up, crossing down, entering a channel and so on. You can choose whether the alert fires <strong>only once or every time</strong> it happens, and how to receive it: email, sound or a notification in the app. An alert you no longer need, just delete.</p>

<h2>The top toolbar</h2>
<h3>Sharing, snapshots and full screen</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Publish an idea:</strong> if you analyzed something special, you can publish it to the community.</li>
<li><strong>Snapshot (the camera):</strong> download as an image, copy the image, copy a link, or post straight to <span class="en">X</span>. Handy when you want to send someone an analysis.</li>
<li><strong>Full screen:</strong> enlarges and shrinks the screen.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Chart settings</h3>
<p>You get to the settings from the gear in the toolbar, or faster: <strong>double-click the candles.</strong> What's worth knowing there:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Candle colors:</strong> they don't have to be red and green. You can use black and white, blue, yellow, whatever you like.</li>
<li><strong>Trading hours:</strong> regular, or extended, meaning including the pre-market and after-market.</li>
<li><strong>Watermark:</strong> the big text in the background, for example "INTC, 5". You can remove it or change its transparency.</li>
<li><strong>Template:</strong> at the bottom of the window you can save your settings under a name, or go back to the default.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Autosave and a layout of several charts</h3>
<p>You can choose for the software to <strong>save automatically</strong> every change you make on the chart. With the layout button you split the screen into several charts, for example Intel on the left and Nvidia on the right, two companies from the same sector, to compare them. There are many types of layouts, and in each one you can add more charts.</p>

<h3>Bar replay: practice on the past</h3>
<p>This is one of the most useful tools for practice. Click <strong>Replay,</strong> choose a point in the past, and the chart is "erased" from that point. Now you can move forward candle by candle and check what you thought would happen, or press <span class="en">Play</span> and let it run by itself, at the speed you choose (3x, 5x, 7x).</p>
<p>Best of all: during the replay you can also <strong>buy and sell.</strong> Choose a quantity, click buy (or sell for a short), place a stop and target, and see the profit and loss in real time, as if it's happening now. That's how you do <a href="/academy/glossary#market-replay">manual backtesting</a>. To exit the replay, click the <span class="en">X</span>, and the chart returns to the present.</p>

<h3>Indicators</h3>
<p>With the indicators button you search for what you want, for example <span class="en">EMA 20</span> or volume, and click to add it to the chart. An indicator you use a lot, mark <strong>with a star,</strong> and it appears in your favorites. There are also <strong>indicator templates,</strong> to save a group of indicators you like.</p>
<p>Some indicators appear in a separate pane below the chart, like <span class="en">MFI</span>, and you delete those from below. Others appear on the chart itself, like moving averages, and then they're listed in the top-left corner. Next to each one there's an <strong>eye</strong> to hide it, settings (or double-click the indicator) and delete. Tip: an indicator you don't need right now but use a lot is better hidden than deleted. That way it stays available.</p>

<h3>Chart type, timeframes and moving around the chart</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chart type:</strong> line, candles and more. I mainly use a line chart and a <a href="/academy/glossary#candlestick-chart">candlestick</a> chart.</li>
<li><strong>Timeframe (interval):</strong> how much time each candle represents: a minute, 5 minutes, 30 minutes, an hour, a day.</li>
<li><strong>Moving around:</strong> drag the chart with the mouse, and stretch the time scale (at the bottom) and the price scale (on the side). If everything gets messed up, <strong>a double-click on the price scale</strong> resets the chart.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Comparing two symbols</h3>
<p>With the compare button (the plus) you add another symbol to the same chart. For example, compare the <span class="en">S&amp;P 500</span> contract (<span class="en">ES</span>) with the Nasdaq contract (<span class="en">NQ</span>). It's worth switching to a line chart so it's easy to read. In the period I recorded, the Nasdaq fell 6.73% and <span class="en">ES</span> fell 5.73%, meaning <span class="en">ES</span> was stronger. The numbers change with the period shown on the screen. Right-click the scale to choose a percent view, indexed to 100 or logarithmic. I wrote about these two contracts in the article <a href="/blog/tik-nostro">What is a prop firm account</a>.</p>

<h2>The left toolbar: drawing tools and trade planning</h2>
<h3>The cursor</h3>
<p>The first tool in the toolbar is the cursor type: a cross, a dot, or <strong>demonstration.</strong> I like demonstration: hold <span class="en">Alt</span> on the keyboard and draw on the chart, and the moment you let go, the drawing disappears by itself. Great when you're explaining something on the chart to someone.</p>

<h3>Lines, the magnet and favorites</h3>
<p>The line tools include a <a href="/academy/glossary#trendline">trendline</a>, a ray, a horizontal line, a parallel channel and more. Draw a line from point to point.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Magnet:</strong> snaps the line to the candles' open, close and extreme points. I don't like it, I prefer freehand, but you should know it's there.</li>
<li><strong>Favorites:</strong> any tool you like, mark with a star, and it's added to a small favorites toolbar. You can drag that toolbar anywhere on the chart, so your tools are always at hand.</li>
</ul>
<p>Further down the toolbar there are more tools, like <strong>Fibonacci,</strong> and patterns like head and shoulders, Elliott waves and cycles. Go through them and see what you know. To delete all the drawings at once, click <strong>the trash can</strong> at the bottom of the toolbar, and choose whether to delete the drawings, the indicators or both.</p>

<h3>Long position and short position: the tool that calculates your size</h3>
<p>This is a very, very important tool. In the "Forecasting and measurement tools" menu there's <strong>Long Position</strong> and <strong>Short Position.</strong> Mark the entry on the chart, where the stop will be and where the target is, and the tool shows how many points (or dollars) there are to the target and the stop, and the <a href="/academy/glossary#risk-reward-ratio">risk-reward ratio</a>.</p>
<p>The most useful part: double-click the tool to set <strong>an account size and how much you want to risk,</strong> in dollars or as a percentage of the account. For example, risking $500 on a trade. The tool calculates by itself <strong>how much to buy</strong> based on the stop distance. When you move the stop, the quantity changes: a wider stop, fewer units. That way you always lose exactly the amount you planned at the stop.</p>
<p>By the way, it's better to learn to calculate this yourself too, and that's exactly what the <a href="/calculators/position-size">position size calculator</a> on the site does. And this tool combines great with bar replay: mark a trade, enter the quantity the tool gave you, and let the chart run to see what would have happened.</p>

<h3>Arrows, shapes, text and templates</h3>
<p>There are endless things to mark: arrows, a rectangle, a triangle, an ellipse and a <strong>path</strong> (you mark point after point, and a double-click finishes it). You can add text and notes, and change any shape's color, size and border with a double-click.</p>
<p><strong>A tip I use:</strong> I mark gaps with a rectangle with the text "gap". After I styled it once, I save it <strong>as a template</strong> ("Save as" in the settings window). Next time, mark a rectangle, choose the template, and the styling comes automatically. In the same window there's also <strong>Extend right,</strong> which stretches the rectangle up to the current price.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Emojis:</strong> you can add those too, if you want.</li>
<li><strong>Measure:</strong> measures how far price moved, in percent, candles and time. Shortcut: hold <span class="en">Shift</span> and drag on the chart.</li>
<li><strong>Zoom in:</strong> zoom into a specific area.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The bottom panel: screener, strategies and the demo account</h2>
<h3>Stock screener</h3>
<p>At the bottom of the screen there's a <strong>stock screener.</strong> If it opens too low, drag it up. You can filter by almost anything, for example stocks by performance over the last six months, or dividends. The screener is very advanced, but I personally use <span class="en">Finviz</span>'s screeners more.</p>

<h3>The <span class="en">Pine</span> editor and the replay panel</h3>
<p>In the <span class="en">Pine</span> editor you build indicators and strategies in code, and test a strategy on the past automatically, without going candle by candle. And the replay panel is the same tool we saw earlier in the top toolbar.</p>

<h3>The trading panel and the demo account (<span class="en">Paper Trading</span>)</h3>
<p>In the trading panel you connect to a broker, for example <span class="en">Interactive Brokers</span>, and trade straight from the chart. There's a list there of all the brokers that work with TradingView. If you trade a prop firm account, you can connect through <span class="en">Tradovate</span>. There's a full explanation on the <a href="/tradingview-guide">TradingView page</a>.</p>
<p>And there's also a <strong>demo account,</strong> which is very important for practice. Click <span class="en">Paper Trading</span> and create a new account:</p>
<ol>
<li>Give it a name, for example "Practice".</li>
<li>Choose how much money it will have. <strong>I recommend an amount that's real for you.</strong> If you plan to work with $10,000, open a $10,000 account.</li>
<li>You can choose for it to calculate commissions too, so it's realistic.</li>
</ol>
<p>In the account you see the balance, realized and unrealized profit and loss (meaning how much you've already closed, and how much is still open inside a trade), margin, all your orders, the history and the trading journal. And if you lost and want to start over, reset the account in the settings.</p>

<h3>How to place an order on demo, with a stop and target</h3>
<p>Click the plus next to the price scale, at the level you want, and choose a buy order. Choose a quantity, place a stop and target, and the window shows <strong>how much you'll lose if the stop is hit, and how much you'll make at the target.</strong> You can also buy and sell from the buy and sell buttons at the top, but the plus is more comfortable for me. I wrote about the order types themselves, market, limit and stop, in the article <a href="/blog/order-types">Order types</a>.</p>
<p><strong>This is how I combine the tools:</strong> on a $10,000 account I don't want to lose more than $300 on a trade. I mark the trade with the long position tool, with $300 of risk, and it tells me to buy 172 units. Now I place an order on demo with the same quantity, the same stop and the same target. If the stop is hit, I'll lose $300, exactly as planned.</p>
<p>Practice on demo, and take it seriously. It's the best tool there is for learning to trade without risking money.</p>

<div class="cta"><h2>What's next?</h2><p>Haven't signed up yet? Through the link on our <a href="/tradingview-guide">TradingView page</a> you get $15 off the next plan, after the first purchase. You'll also find the plans and how to connect prop firm accounts there. And if you're starting from zero, learn the basics: <a href="/blog/long-short">long and short</a> and <a href="/blog/order-types">order types</a>.</p></div>
`,faq:[{q:"Is TradingView free?",a:"There's a free version, but it's missing a lot of things and has a lot of ads. The paid plans have a 30-day trial, and you can cancel before you're charged."},{q:"How do you open a demo account on TradingView?",a:"In the trading panel at the bottom of the screen, choose Paper Trading and create a new account. Give it a name, choose an amount, and you can choose for it to calculate commissions too. It's best to choose an amount similar to what you'll really trade with."},{q:"How do you practice on historical data?",a:"With the Replay button in the top toolbar, choose a point in the past, and move forward candle by candle or with automatic playback. You can also buy and sell along the way, with a stop and target, and see the result in real time."},{q:"How does TradingView calculate how much to buy?",a:"In the long position or short position tool in the left toolbar, set an account size and a risk amount, in dollars or percent. Once you mark an entry and a stop, the tool calculates the quantity so that at the stop you lose exactly the amount you set."},{q:"Is there a discount for signing up to TradingView?",a:"Anyone who signs up through our link gets $15 off the next plan, after the first purchase. The discount doesn't apply to the first purchase itself."},{q:"Can you change the interface language?",a:"Yes. In the profile menu in the top-left corner, choose Language. In the same menu you also choose dark or light mode."}]},t="/client-previews/antartrade/club-demo/images/blog/replikanto-install",E={title:"How to install and set up Replikanto: a step-by-step copy trading guide",seoTitle:"Installing Replikanto Step by Step: Copy Trading in NinjaTrader | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"An installation guide to Replikanto with screenshots: buying with a discount code, generating a Machine ID, installing in NinjaTrader, setting the leader account and ratios, and the must-have settings for funded accounts.",excerpt:"The complete guide to copying trades between accounts: how to buy Replikanto, generate a Machine ID, install it in NinjaTrader, and copy your first trade without risking your funded accounts.",category:"Guides",coverAlt:"Installing Replikanto: a NinjaTrader trade copied to four accounts",html:`
<p class="xlink">Want to know first what Replikanto is, what it costs and who it suits? <a href="/replikanto-guide" data-link="replikanto-guide">See the full Replikanto page, with current prices</a></p>

<p>You've asked me hundreds of times to explain how I copy trades from account to account, so here's the complete guide: how to buy <span class="en">Replikanto</span>, how to install it in NinjaTrader, how to set it up and how to copy your first trade. All the screenshots are taken from my video.</p>

<div class="box"><strong>What you need before you start</strong>
  <ul>
    <li>A Windows computer with <span class="en">NinjaTrader 8</span>. Replikanto is a NinjaTrader add-on, and doesn't work without it.</li>
    <li>The accounts you want to copy to, connected to NinjaTrader.</li>
    <li>My discount code: <span class="code">ANTAR</span>.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<ol class="toc">
  <li><strong>Purchase and generating a Machine ID</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Installing in NinjaTrader</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Settings and copying your first trade</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Must-have settings for funded accounts</strong></li>
</ol>

<!-- ============ PART 1 ============ -->
<h2>Part 1: purchase and generating a Machine ID</h2>
<p>The Replikanto license is tied to your computer through a NinjaTrader identifier called a <span class="en">Machine ID</span>. You enter it already on the payment page, so it's worth having NinjaTrader open before you start buying.</p>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Go to the FlowBots product page</h3>
<p>Go to the website of <span class="en">FlowBots</span>, the company that develops Replikanto, through the link on the <a href="/replikanto-guide#pricing" data-link="replikanto-guide">Replikanto</a> page. If you buy through my link I earn a little from it, and it's always nice to show some support.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/01-product-page.webp" alt="The Replikanto product page on the FlowBots website" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Choose a license type</h3>
<p>Scroll down and choose the license that suits you under <span class="en">License Type</span>: yearly or lifetime, for one computer or two. There's also a 7-day trial license (<span class="en">Trial</span>) for simulation accounts, and I recommend starting with it to make sure everything works before you move to real accounts. After choosing, click <span class="en">Add to cart</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/02-license-type.webp" alt="Choosing the Replikanto license type, here a 7-day trial license" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box"><strong>How much does it cost?</strong>The prices in the screenshots are from the day I filmed the video. You'll find the current prices, including the price after the discount, in the <a href="/replikanto-guide#pricing" data-link="replikanto-guide">Replikanto price table</a>. It's checked against the official website.</div>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>Enter the discount code</h3>
<p>In the cart, type the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span> in the coupon field and click <span class="en">Apply coupon</span>. The price updates right away. Then continue to payment and fill in the usual details.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/03-cart-coupon.webp" alt="The FlowBots shopping cart with the coupon field and the Apply coupon button" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">4</span>Generate a Machine ID in NinjaTrader</h3>
<p>The payment page has a field called <span class="en">Signed NinjaTrader Machine ID</span>. To fill it in:</p>
<ol>
  <li>In NinjaTrader's main window (<span class="en">Control Center</span>), click <span class="en">Help</span> and then <span class="en">3rd party licensing</span>.</li>
  <li>In the <span class="en">Vendor name</span> field, type exactly <span class="en">FlowBots</span>, with a capital F and B.</li>
  <li>In the <span class="en">User defined ID</span> field, write whatever you like, for example your name. It doesn't matter.</li>
  <li>Click <span class="en">Submit</span>, and NinjaTrader generates a long code.</li>
</ol>
<figure><img src="${t}/04-vendor-name.webp" alt="The 3rd Party Licensing window in NinjaTrader with FlowBots in the Vendor name field" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Copy the code and paste it into the <span class="en">Signed NinjaTrader Machine ID</span> field on the payment page.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/05-machine-id.webp" alt="The Machine ID NinjaTrader generates after clicking Submit (blurred)" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box"><strong>Good to know</strong>You can't create a user on the FlowBots website before buying. The username and password are created automatically after the first payment. And if you change computers, you can change the Machine ID even after the order.</div>

<h3><span class="n">5</span>Confirm the order by email</h3>
<p>Fill in your card details and click <span class="en">Place order</span>. The first time, you'll get an email asking you to confirm the account. Open the email, click the confirmation link, come back to the page and click <span class="en">Place order</span> again. This time the order goes through.</p>

<h3><span class="n">6</span>Download the file</h3>
<p>After the purchase is confirmed, a <span class="en">Downloads</span> area appears, and there you click the latest version of Replikanto.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/06-download.webp" alt="The Downloads area on the FlowBots website with the Replikanto download button" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box warn"><strong>There are two versions to download</strong>The version depends on your NinjaTrader version: one for NinjaTrader 8.1.6.0 and up, and one for NinjaTrader 8.1.5.2 and below. Check your version under <span class="en">Help &gt; About</span> before you download.</div>

<!-- ============ PART 2 ============ -->
<h2>Part 2: installing in NinjaTrader</h2>

<div class="box warn"><strong>Before installing: OneDrive</strong>NinjaTrader and Replikanto don't get along with cloud services like <span class="en">Microsoft OneDrive</span>. The cloud locks files while it syncs, which causes errors like <span class="en">Import Failed</span> and can even corrupt trading data. Before you install, turn off the backup of the <span class="en">Documents</span> folder in the OneDrive settings, and make sure the <span class="en">NinjaTrader 8</span> folder sits locally on your computer.</div>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Import the file into NinjaTrader</h3>
<p>Don't extract (<span class="en">Unzip</span>) the file you downloaded. In NinjaTrader, click <span class="en">Tools &gt; Import &gt; NinjaScript Add-On</span>, choose the ZIP file (usually in your downloads folder), click <span class="en">Open</span> and confirm every message that pops up.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/07-import-addon.webp" alt="The Tools, Import, NinjaScript Add-On menu in NinjaTrader" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Restart NinjaTrader and open Replikanto</h3>
<p>Close NinjaTrader and open it again. Without a restart the add-on won't work. Then click <span class="en">New</span>, scroll all the way down to <span class="en">FlowBots</span>, and choose <span class="en">Replikanto</span>.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/08-open-replikanto.webp" alt="The New menu in NinjaTrader with FlowBots and Replikanto at the bottom" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<!-- ============ PART 3 ============ -->
<h2>Part 3: settings and copying your first trade</h2>
<p>In the video I placed three charts side by side, each connected to a different account, so you can see how a trade on one account is copied to the rest. Everything is on simulation accounts, and I recommend that you practice on sim first too.</p>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Choose an instrument and a leader account</h3>
<p>The Replikanto window has three main settings on the left:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Instrument</span>:</strong> the instrument you trade, for example MNQ, NQ, ES or oil.</li>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Leader Account</span>:</strong> the leader account. Every trade you take on it is copied to the other accounts.</li>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Method</span>:</strong> how the quantity is copied. More on that right below.</li>
</ul>
<p>On the right, under <span class="en">Follower Accounts</span>, the follower accounts are listed. <strong>Check the circle in the <span class="en">On</span> column next to every account you want to copy to.</strong> An account you didn't check won't get the trade.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/09-control-center.webp" alt="The Replikanto window with Instrument, Leader Account, Method and the Follower Accounts list" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Choose how to copy the quantity</h3>
<p>There are several methods, and these are the two most relevant:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Exact Quantity</span>:</strong> exactly the same quantity. You entered one contract on the leader account, and every follower enters one contract.</li>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Ratio</span>:</strong> a different ratio for each account. It suits accounts of different sizes. For example, the leader account is 50K and you have a 150K account: set its ratio to 3, and every contract on the leader account becomes three on the large account.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="${t}/10-ratio.webp" alt="The Ratio method in Replikanto with a ratio of 2 for one of the follower accounts" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>Copying from micro to mini (Cross Order)</h3>
<p>In the <span class="en">Cross Order</span> column you can set a certain account to get the trade in a different contract. In the example in the video, the leader account trades the micro (MNQ), and one of the followers gets the same trade in the mini (NQ).</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/11-cross-order.webp" alt="The Cross Order column in Replikanto, a follower account set to NQ" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">4</span>Don't open extra tabs</h3>
<p>At the bottom of the window there's a <span class="en">+</span> that opens another copy tab.</p>
<figure><img src="${t}/12-tabs-warning.webp" alt="Two copy tabs open at the bottom of the Replikanto window" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<div class="box warn"><strong>Don't use it</strong>If you forgot a tab open, you could get a copy on top of a copy on top of a copy, and that can make a serious mess of your accounts. Always work with one tab.</div>

<h3><span class="n">5</span>A test trade, and how to close</h3>
<p>Enter a trade on the leader account, and check on every account that it went in with the quantity and contract you set. In the example I entered one contract: the account with a ratio of 2 entered two, and the account with the Cross Order entered one mini contract.</p>
<p>There are two ways to close:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The X in the <span class="en">Flatten</span> column:</strong> closes one account. Keep an eye on this column all the time, because that's where you see if an account was left open after you exited the trade.</li>
  <li><strong><span class="en">Flatten All</span>:</strong> closes all the accounts at once.</li>
</ul>
<figure><img src="${t}/13-flatten.webp" alt="Open positions on the follower accounts and the Flatten buttons in Replikanto" loading="lazy" /></figure>

<h3><span class="n">6</span>Trading from Tradovate or TradingView</h3>
<p>Replikanto doesn't work inside Tradovate, but you can work around it: set the account connected to Tradovate as the leader account in Replikanto. From then on, every trade you take on Tradovate or TradingView is copied to the other accounts open in NinjaTrader. The condition: NinjaTrader has to stay open in the background the whole time.</p>

<!-- ============ PART 4 ============ -->
<h2>Part 4: must-have settings for funded accounts</h2>
<p>Prop firms like Apex and Topstep can close an account for breaking the rules. Replikanto has three settings worth knowing so that this doesn't happen to you.</p>

<h3><span class="n">1</span>Compliance Mode</h3>
<p>Prop firms forbid copying trades remotely (<span class="en">Remote Copy</span>), to prevent people from managing other people's accounts. In FlowBots' <span class="en">Compliance Mode</span> version, remote copying and "hidden mode" (<span class="en">Stealth Mode</span>) are turned off in advance, so you work within the rules.</p>

<h3><span class="n">2</span>Follower Guard against opposite positions</h3>
<p>A long on one account and a short on another is considered a serious violation (<span class="en">Wash Trade</span>) at Apex and Topstep. It can happen by mistake: a follower account got stuck and didn't close a position, and then you opened an opposite trade on the leader account. To prevent this:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Right-click the Replikanto window and choose <span class="en">Properties</span>.</li>
  <li>Under <span class="en">Follower Guard</span>, check <span class="en">Flatten follower if it becomes out of sync</span>. This setting automatically closes a position stuck on a follower account.</li>
  <li>Never set a negative ratio (for example -1.0). A ratio like that opens an opposite trade on purpose.</li>
</ol>

<h3><span class="n">3</span>ATM Copy</h3>
<p>If you work with a stop and take profit together (<span class="en">OCO</span> orders), it's recommended to turn on the <span class="en">ATM Copy</span> column. Instead of copying every change to the exit orders, each follower account manages its exit by itself through NinjaTrader's ATM from the moment the entry filled. This cuts down on glitches when there are internet problems.</p>

<div class="box"><strong>My tip (Noam Antar, the author)</strong>Copy trading multiplies everything, both the profits and the losses. If you're just starting out, don't rush to connect ten accounts. First get a payout on one account, then grow to two, to five, and so on. It's also harder mentally to trade when you know every trade is multiplied. And one more thing: the copying isn't perfect. More than once a trade went in on only some of the accounts, or I exited and some stayed open. That's why the Replikanto window is always open on one of my screens, and on every entry and exit I check that all the accounts really took the trade.</div>

<p>Ran into a problem? The Replikanto page has <a href="/replikanto-guide" data-link="replikanto-guide">answers to common issues</a>: orders getting stuck on Tradovate, contract rolls, profit differences between accounts and accounts marked in yellow or red. And if you prefer a solution that runs in the cloud and doesn't need a computer left on, take a look at <a href="/tradesyncer-guide" data-link="tradesyncer-guide">Tradesyncer</a>.</p>

<div class="cta">
  <h2>Ready to start?</h2>
  <p>On the <a href="/replikanto-guide#pricing" data-link="replikanto-guide">Replikanto</a> page you'll find the current prices and a purchase link. At checkout, the code <span class="code">ANTAR</span> gives you a discount.</p>
</div>

<p class="disc">This guide is for information and instruction only and is not investment advice. The interface and terms of Replikanto and NinjaTrader may change, and each prop firm has its own rules on copying trades. Check your firm's rules before you start.</p>
`,faq:[{q:"Can you try Replikanto before buying?",a:"Yes. There's a 7-day trial license (Trial) that works on simulation accounts, so you can make sure everything works before moving to real accounts."},{q:"Where do you find the Machine ID?",a:"In NinjaTrader: Help and then 3rd party licensing. In the Vendor name field type FlowBots, in the User defined ID field type whatever you like, and click Submit. Paste the code it generates into the payment page."},{q:"Can you copy to accounts of different sizes?",a:"Yes. Choose the Ratio method and set a ratio for each account. For example a ratio of 3 for a 150K account when the leader account is 50K."},{q:"Can you copy a trade from micro to mini?",a:"Yes, through the Cross Order column. Set a different contract for the follower account, for example NQ when the leader account trades MNQ."},{q:"Can you copy trades you open on Tradovate or TradingView?",a:"Yes. Set the account connected to Tradovate as the leader account in Replikanto, and keep NinjaTrader open in the background. Every trade you take will be copied to the other accounts in NinjaTrader."}]},N={title:"The complete guide to Apex Trader Funding (2026)",seoTitle:"Apex Trader Funding - The Complete Guide 2026 | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"The complete guide to Apex Trader Funding: the Intraday and EOD plans, the evaluation, activating the PA account, the trailing stop lock, the payout terms, the platforms and the rules - with live data from the site's database.",excerpt:"The two evaluation plans, PA activation, the trailing stop lock, the payout terms and the platforms - with live data from the site's database.",category:"Firm guides",coverAlt:"Apex Trader Funding guide",intro:["Apex Trader Funding (Apex) is a prop firm for futures traders. Instead of depositing their own capital, the trader buys an evaluation in a simulated environment, and after passing it activates a simulated funded account (PA) they can withdraw from under the firm's rules.","This guide explains the two evaluation plans, the activation stage, the trailing stop lock and the payout terms. The changing numbers and terms are pulled live from the site's database and update automatically. The guide isn't a rating of or a recommendation for the firm."],sections:[{id:"how-it-works",title:"How the model works",blocks:[{p:"Apex offers two evaluation plans - Intraday Trail and EOD Trail - in four account sizes. The main difference between them is how the loss limit is calculated: intraday or end of day (EOD)."},{ol:["Choose a plan and an account size, and buy an evaluation.","Evaluation - a simulated account with a profit target and risk limits. It's valid for {eval_validity} days, and the subscription renews until cancelled.","Activate the PA account - after passing you must activate the funded account within {activation_window}, on the one-time activation fee plan or the no-activation-fee plan, depending on what you bought.","Payouts - after building up {qualifying_days} and meeting the other eligibility conditions.","Live account - by the firm's invitation only; it can't be bought."]},{note:"The PA activation fee is paid only once. The only recurring payment is the evaluation subscription, until it's cancelled or you move to a PA."}]},{id:"evaluation",title:"The evaluation stage",blocks:[{p:"The evaluation is a simulated account in which you need to reach a set target balance while staying within the loss limit and the other rules. The minimum number of trading days is {eval_min_days}, so the evaluation can be passed quickly too."},{h3:"Maximum loss limit (MLL)"},{p:"The loss limit sets the level below which the account fails. On the Intraday plan the drawdown trails in real time and updates against open profits too; on the EOD plan it updates only from the end-of-day balance."},{h3:"Daily loss limit (DLL)"},{p:"The EOD plan has a daily loss limit by account size (shown in the table). The daily loss limit in the Intraday evaluation: {dll_eval_intraday}."},{h3:"The consistency rule"},{p:"Consistency rule in the evaluation: {eval_consistency}. Apex's consistency rule applies to the funded account and is explained in the payouts section."},{live:"apexEval"},{h3:"Evaluation prices"},{p:"The prices are pulled live from the database, including active deals. For each size you can choose a plan with an activation fee after passing, or a no-activation-fee plan at a higher monthly price."},{live:"apexPricing"}]},{id:"pa",title:"After the evaluation - activating the PA account",blocks:[{p:"After passing the evaluation you must activate the PA account within {activation_window}; otherwise the account is closed. The activation fee is one-time only and isn't a renewing subscription."},{h3:"The trailing stop lock"},{p:"On the PA account the trailing drawdown keeps rising until the stop point (Trigger) - the starting balance + the drawdown size + $100. From that point the drawdown is locked at the starting balance + $100 (Lock) and never drops below it again."},{note:"The Lock is the protection level only - not the payout level. Payout eligibility starts only above the Trigger, subject to a minimum payout of {min_payout}. The exact balances for each size are in the payouts table below."},{h3:"Daily loss limit and scaling on the PA"},{p:"Daily loss limit on the PA: {pa_dll}. Both the allowed number of contracts and the daily limit change by tier, which updates daily from the previous trading day's closing balance. The full tables for each size are on the firm's page."},{p:"Number of funded accounts you can hold at once: {max_accounts}. The activity requirement on a PA account: {inactivity}"}]},{id:"live",title:"The Live account",blocks:[{p:"How the move happens: {live_selection}. You can't buy the move or ask for it."},{p:"{live_sim_transition}, and you can't hold a Live account and simulated accounts at the same time."},{ul:["Starting balance: {live_starting_balance} · Loss limit: {live_drawdown} · Contracts: {live_contracts}","Daily loss limit: {live_dll}","Profit split: {live_profit_split} · Number of accounts: {live_max_accounts}","Payouts: {live_payout_frequency}, minimum {live_min_payout} · {live_safety_net}","Activity: {live_inactivity}"]}]},{id:"payouts",title:"Payouts",blocks:[{p:"To request a payout you need to build up {qualifying_days}, each with a minimum daily profit by account size (shown in the table), and meet the consistency rule: your most profitable day must be less than {consistency_funded} of the profit made since the last payout."},{p:"A payout is possible only above the Trigger and subject to the minimum balance for a request (Trigger + {min_payout}). The minimum payout is {min_payout}, and there's a payout cap by account size and payout number. Profit split: {profit_split} to the trader."},{live:"apexPayouts"},{p:"Payout limit: {max_payouts}. Payment method for payouts: {payout_provider}. Payout processing: {payout_timeframe}."}]},{id:"platforms",title:"Platforms and market connection",blocks:[{p:"The connection to the market is through {connections}. Available asset types: {markets}."},{p:"Supported platforms (updated live from the database):"},{live:"platforms"}]},{id:"rules",title:"Important features and rules",blocks:[{ul:["Trading hours - all positions must be closed by {market_close}. {overnight}","News trading - {news}","Hedging - {hedging}","Automated trading and HFT - {automation} {hft}","Copy trading - {copy_trading}","DCA - {dca}","Minimum activity on a PA - {inactivity}","Number of accounts - {max_accounts}"]}]},{id:"getting-started",title:"How to start trading at Apex",blocks:[{ol:["Choose a plan (Intraday or EOD) and an account size.","Buy an evaluation and connect the platform to the account.","Trade the evaluation until you reach the target balance, while staying within the limits.","Activate the PA account within {activation_window}.","Build up {qualifying_days} and meet the payout rules.","Request a payout through the firm's dashboard."]}]},{id:"related",title:"Related guides",blocks:[{p:"Complete guides to more firms and platforms are being added to the blog over time."},{links:[{to:"/apex",label:"The full Apex firm page"}]}]}],faq:[{q:"What's the difference between the Intraday and EOD plans?",a:"The main difference is how the loss limit is calculated: on Intraday the drawdown trails in real time, including against open profits, while on EOD it updates only from the end-of-day balance. Also, the EOD evaluation has a daily loss limit, and on Intraday it is {dll_eval_intraday}."},{q:"How long is the evaluation valid?",a:"The evaluation is valid for {eval_validity} days, and the subscription renews every month until it's cancelled or you move to a PA."},{q:"Does Apex have a consistency rule?",a:"Consistency rule in the evaluation: {eval_consistency}. The funded account has a consistency rule of {consistency_funded} - your most profitable day must be less than {consistency_funded} of the profit made since the last payout."},{q:"What is the activation fee and when do you pay it?",a:"The activation fee is paid only once, when you activate the PA account, within {activation_window} of passing the evaluation. The fee varies by plan and size and is shown in the price table in this guide. There's also a no-activation-fee plan."},{q:"How many payouts can you make?",a:"{max_payouts}. After that the account ends."},{q:"What is the minimum payout?",a:"The minimum payout is {min_payout}, and a request is possible only when the balance meets the minimum balance for a request (Trigger + {min_payout}), as shown in the payouts table."},{q:"How many funded accounts can you hold?",a:"{max_accounts}."}],sources:[{label:"Apex Trader Funding - official website",href:"https://apextraderfunding.com"},{label:"Apex Trader Funding - help center",href:"https://support.apextraderfunding.com"}]},H={title:"Lucid Trading - the complete guide: plans, rules and payouts",seoTitle:"Lucid Trading - A Complete Guide to Its Plans, Rules and Payouts",seoDescription:"A comprehensive guide to Lucid Trading: the trading plans, the evaluation, the loss limits, the payout terms, the platforms and the path to a Live account.",excerpt:"The four plans, the evaluation and funded rules, the payout terms and the move to Live - with live data from the site's database.",category:"Firm guides",coverAlt:"Lucid Trading guide",intro:["Lucid Trading is a prop firm for futures traders. Instead of depositing their own capital, the trader picks a plan and starts trading in a simulated environment, where their performance decides how far they go: an evaluation, a simulated funded account (Sim Funded) they can withdraw from, and later a possible invitation to LucidLive - a real brokerage account.","This guide explains the plans, the evaluation, the funded account, the payout terms and the move to Live. The changing numbers and terms are pulled live from the site's database and update automatically. The guide isn't a rating of or a recommendation for the firm."],sections:[{id:"tracks",title:"Lucid's four plans",blocks:[{p:"The firm offers four main plans: LucidPro, LucidFlex, LucidDaily and LucidDirect. The payment model on the evaluation plans is {billing_pro} - the account is paid for once, with no recurring monthly subscription."},{p:"Each plan has different purchase terms, risk limits and payout rules. Choosing the plan is the key decision before buying, so it's worth understanding the differences before you pay."},{live:"lucidTracks"},{p:"Buying LucidDirect skips the evaluation for a higher upfront cost, and you start directly on a simulated funded account."},{note:"LucidLive isn't a plan you can buy - it's a later stage the firm invites suitable traders to, and it's explained further on in this guide."}]},{id:"how-it-works",title:"How the model works, in short",blocks:[{ol:["Choose an account type - an evaluation plan (Pro, Flex or Daily) or straight to funded (Direct).","Evaluation - a simulated account with a profit target; reaching the target qualifies you for an upgrade to a funded account.","Funded account (Sim Funded) - also a simulated account, where you build up simulated capital and can request payouts when you meet the plan's terms.","Payouts - once you meet the plan's eligibility conditions.","LucidLive - a move to a real brokerage account. The move isn't the same on every plan and depends on the plan's terms."]},{p:"Moving from the evaluation to the funded account carries no extra activation fee: the activation fee on Pro is {activation_pro}."}]},{id:"evaluation",title:"The evaluation stage",blocks:[{p:"The evaluation is the stage where the trader has to reach a set profit target while staying within the plan's risk limits and rules. The profit target is the amount the trader needs to make on the evaluation account to qualify for the next stage, and it varies by plan and account size. Some plans also require meeting a consistency rule - reaching the target alone isn't enough."},{h3:"The maximum loss limit (Drawdown / MLL)"},{p:"The maximum loss limit sets the loss level the account must never reach - reaching it breaches the account's terms. Lucid uses two calculation methods: end of day (EOD), where the loss limit updates from the account balance at the end of the trading day, and intraday, where the limit updates during the trading day, also counting unrealized profits and losses."},{p:"The drawdown type in the evaluation by plan: Pro - {dd_pro_eval} · Flex - {dd_flex_eval} · Daily - chosen at purchase: {dd_daily_eval_eod} or {dd_daily_eval_intraday}."},{h3:"Daily loss limit (DLL)"},{p:"The daily loss limit sets the loss allowed during a trading day. Hitting the daily limit counts as a temporary breach (Soft Breach): trading is blocked until the next session, but the account isn't failed."},{p:"Can you turn on a DLL on LucidPro? {dll_pro_option}. When the limit is on, its size depends on the account size (the exact amounts are in the table)."},{h3:"The consistency rule"},{p:"The consistency rule limits how much of the account's total profit can come from the most profitable trading day. For example, if the consistency requirement is 50% and the total profit is $2,000, the most profitable day can't exceed $1,000. If the trader reached the target but didn't meet the consistency rule, they need to keep building up profit."},{p:"The consistency requirement in the evaluation by plan: Pro - {consistency_pro_eval} · Flex - {consistency_flex_eval} · Daily - {consistency_daily_eval}."},{h3:"Trading days and how fast you can pass"},{p:"On LucidPro the evaluation can be completed in a single trading day (minimum trading days: {min_days_pro_eval}). On LucidFlex - {eval_speed_flex}, and on LucidDaily - {eval_speed_daily}."},{live:"lucidEval"},{p:"Each plan and account size has a maximum number of contracts, which may differ between Mini and Micro contracts. Once you meet the evaluation's requirements you can move to a simulated funded account (Sim Funded) with no extra activation fee, usually within a short time: {transfer_pro}."}]},{id:"funded",title:"After you pass - the funded account",blocks:[{p:"Once you meet all the evaluation's requirements, you move to a simulated funded account (Sim Funded). On this account you can build up profit and request payouts under the plan's terms. Note that the funded account's rules may differ from those of the evaluation."},{p:"What changes on each plan?"},{ul:["LucidPro - keeps an end-of-day (EOD) drawdown. The consistency rule on the funded account: {consistency_pro_funded}. The daily loss limit (DLL) depends on the account plan, and the scaling plan limits the number of contracts gradually.","LucidFlex - keeps an end-of-day (EOD) drawdown. The consistency rule on the funded account: {consistency_flex_funded}. There's a scaling plan, which sets the allowed number of contracts by the account's profit.","LucidDaily - the drawdown type on the funded account matches the evaluation's (EOD or intraday). Consistency rule: {consistency_daily_funded}, but there are specific restrictions on trading during economic news.","LucidDirect - there's no evaluation; the account starts directly as a simulated funded account, with a consistency rule of {consistency_direct_funded}."]}]},{id:"live",title:"The move to LucidLive",blocks:[{p:"After moving to a simulated funded account (Sim Funded), the trader may get an invitation to a LucidLive account - an account where trading is done with real money. The move isn't automatic or guaranteed, and it's always at the discretion of Lucid's risk management team."},{p:"Lucid reviews traders for the move based on their performance, the payouts they've made and other criteria. Even when one of the relevant criteria is met - such as reaching the plan's last payout stage or exceptional performance - the final decision stays with the firm."},{h3:"What happens when you move?"},{ul:["The trader gets one Live account for each eligible funded account that has made at least one payout.","The simulated accounts are closed, and closing them doesn't entitle you to a refund of the evaluation fees.","The new Live account starts at a $0 balance, with a loss limit set by the account size."]},{h3:"The main trading rules on Live"},{ul:["Loss limit: {live_drawdown} - calculated from the account balance at the end of the trading day.","Daily loss limit: {live_dll}.","Consistency rule: {consistency_live}.","Scaling plan - the allowed number of contracts changes with the account size and the profit built up."]},{p:"The maximum loss limit (MLL) on Live updates with the account's highest closing balance as profit builds up. Requesting a payout before reaching the starting drawdown level the firm sets may lock the limit in place."},{note:"Awaiting verification: the details of the Cashout option (getting paid instead of moving to Live) and the bonus structure on new Live accounts. These parts will be completed once verified with the site's management."}]},{id:"payouts",title:"Profit payouts",blocks:[{p:"Once you meet the plan's eligibility conditions you can request a profit payout. The profit split: {profit_split}. The minimum payout: {min_payout}. Each plan sets different requirements - for profit, consistency, the account balance and the amount you can withdraw."},{live:"lucidPayouts"},{p:"There's no fixed monthly payout window on the plans reviewed, and you can submit a request once you meet all the requirements. Each plan has a minimum payout and maximum payout limits. Note that the eligibility conditions may reset after an approved payout, and that a submitted request can't be edited or cancelled - an account that falls below the required level may get the request rejected."},{p:"Trading that takes the account balance below the eligibility level may get the payout request rejected. Also, on some plans a payout may change the loss limit (drawdown) or reduce the allowed number of contracts under the scaling plan."},{note:"A field missing from the canonical database: the payout approval and transfer time (by the official source - up to 2 business days after approval). This part will show here once the field is added to the system."}]},{id:"platforms",title:"Trading platforms and connections",blocks:[{p:"Lucid Trading supports two main connection providers for futures trading: {connections}. Platform availability depends on the connection provider, so it's important to check that the platform you plan to trade on is supported by the connection offered - before buying the account."},{live:"platforms"},{p:"The supported market: {markets}."},{p:"Detailed connection guides for each platform (Rithmic, Tradovate, NinjaTrader, TradingView) - coming soon to the site."}]},{id:"rules",title:"Advantages, limits and trading rules",blocks:[{h3:"The main advantages"},{ul:["One-time payment - no recurring monthly subscription on the main account plans.","No extra activation fee - passing the evaluation on Pro, Flex and Daily carries no activation fee.","A plan with no evaluation - LucidDirect lets you start directly on a simulated funded account.","A path to Live - suitable traders may be invited to trade with real money, subject to the firm's decision.","A range of plans - you can choose between plans with different drawdown methods, risk limits and payout terms."]},{h3:"Rules and limits worth knowing"},{ul:["Trading hours - on Pro, Flex and Direct positions must be closed by 16:45 US Eastern Time (EST); positions left open are closed automatically. Hours may change on holidays and on a Live account.","News trading - Pro: {news_pro} · Flex: {news_flex} · Direct: {news_direct}. On Daily: in the evaluation {news_daily_eval}, and on the funded account {news_daily_funded}.","Holding positions overnight: {overnight}.","Hedging: {hedging} - you may not open opposite positions in the same contract, within a single account or across accounts.","High-frequency trading (HFT): {hft}. Micro scalping: {micro_scalping} - the firm prohibits trading methods that abnormally exploit simulation mechanics or fast execution.","Automated trading: {automation} - automated systems and trade copiers are allowed under the firm's policy; exploiting system delays or simulated prices is prohibited.","DCA (averaging): {dca}.","Copy trading: {copy_trading} - allowed between accounts of the same trader.","Account limit: {max_accounts}.","Inactivity: {inactivity} - an account without minimum trading activity over this period may be deleted."]}]},{id:"getting-started",title:"How do you start trading with Lucid Trading?",blocks:[{ol:["Choose a plan - between the evaluation plans (Pro, Flex, Daily) and LucidDirect, which lets you start on a simulated funded account with no evaluation. Before choosing, check the profit target, the loss limits, the trading rules and the payout terms.","Buy the account - after choosing the plan and the account size, you buy it on Lucid Trading's official website.","Connect to a trading platform - after buying, use the account's connection details to log in to a supported trading platform, depending on the provider you chose.","Start trading - on the evaluation plans you need to reach the profit target and keep the account's rules to move to the next stage; on the Direct plan you can start directly on a simulated funded account, under the plan's rules.","Move to a simulated funded account - after passing the evaluation, the trader moves to a Sim Funded account; at this stage trading and payout rules apply that may differ from those of the evaluation.","Request a profit payout - once you meet the funded account's eligibility conditions, you can submit a payout request under Lucid Trading's policy."]},{links:[{to:"/lucid",label:"The full firm page"},{to:"/comparison",label:"Compare all the firms"}]}]},{id:"related",title:"More guides (coming soon)",blocks:[{ul:["How do you buy an evaluation at Lucid Trading? (coming soon)","What do you do after passing an evaluation at Lucid Trading? (coming soon)","How do you withdraw money from Lucid Trading? (coming soon)","How do you connect Lucid Trading to Rithmic? (coming soon)","How do you connect Lucid Trading to Tradovate? (coming soon)","How do you connect Lucid Trading to NinjaTrader? (coming soon)","How do you connect Lucid Trading to TradingView? (coming soon)"]}]}],faq:[{q:"Does Lucid charge a monthly subscription?",a:"No. Lucid Trading works on a one-time payment model, with no renewing monthly subscription. Moving from an evaluation to a funded account carries no extra activation fee either."},{q:"How many funded accounts can you hold at once?",a:"The funded account limit: {max_accounts}. The limit applies to all plans together."},{q:"How do payouts and the profit split work?",a:"The profit split on the main plans is {profit_split}, and the minimum payout is {min_payout}. The eligibility conditions and payout caps vary by plan and account size - the full details are in this guide's payout tables."},{q:"What would get my account blocked?",a:"Breaching the maximum loss limit, prohibited hedging, high-frequency trading (HFT) or exploiting the firm's systems. Suspicious micro-scalping patterns may lead to a manual review rather than an automatic failure, and can still lead to restrictions or the account being closed."},{q:"Can you trade the news and hold trades overnight?",a:"The policy depends on the plan: on Pro, Flex and Direct news trading is {news_pro}; on the Daily funded account it is {news_daily_funded}. Holding positions overnight: {overnight} - positions must be closed by the times the firm sets."},{q:"What happens when you move to a Live account?",a:"The move to LucidLive isn't guaranteed and is always subject to the risk management team's decision. On the new Live account trading is done with real money, with a loss limit of type {live_drawdown} and {live_dll}."},{q:"What happens if I haven't traded for a long time?",a:"Evaluation and funded accounts have a rule of {inactivity}. An evaluation account that failed and wasn't reset is also deleted after 30 days."},{q:"How long does it take to get a payout from Lucid Trading?",a:"According to the official website, once the payout request is approved the funds are sent to the payment method within up to two business days. When you actually receive the money depends on the payment method and the service provider."}],sources:[{label:"Lucid Trading - official website",href:"https://lucidtrading.com/"},{label:"Lucid General FAQ",href:"https://lucidtrading.com/general-faq/"},{label:"Lucid Trading Help Center",href:"https://support.lucidtrading.com/en/"},{label:"Rules and Guidelines",href:"https://support.lucidtrading.com/en/collections/12931279-rules-and-guidelines"},{label:"LucidPro Funded Account",href:"https://support.lucidtrading.com/en/articles/12890069-lucidpro-funded-account"},{label:"LucidFlex Evaluation Account",href:"https://support.lucidtrading.com/en/articles/12945790-lucidflex-evaluation-account"},{label:"LucidFlex Payouts",href:"https://support.lucidtrading.com/en/articles/12945796-lucidflex-payouts"},{label:"New Live Structure",href:"https://support.lucidtrading.com/en/articles/16558826-new-live-structure"},{label:"Lucid Trading Supported Platforms",href:"https://support.lucidtrading.com/en/articles/11404614-lucid-trading-supported-platforms"}]},B={title:"Topstep - the complete guide (2026)",seoTitle:"Topstep - The Complete Guide 2026 | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"The complete guide to Topstep: the evaluation (Trading Combine), the funded XFA account, the loss limits, the payout terms, the TopstepX platform and the move to a Live account - with live data from the site's database.",excerpt:"The evaluation, the XFA account, the payout paths and the move to Live - with live data from the site's database.",category:"Firm guides",coverAlt:"The complete guide to Topstep 2026",intro:["Topstep is a prop firm for futures traders. The firm lets traders prove their trading and risk management skills through an evaluation (Trading Combine), move up to a simulated funded account (Express Funded Account) they can withdraw profits from, and later - under the firm's rules - move to a Live account with real money.","This guide explains the program's structure, the evaluation, the funded account, the payout terms and the move to Live. The changing numbers and terms are pulled live from the site's database and update automatically. The guide isn't a rating of or a recommendation for the firm."],sections:[{id:"model",title:"How the model works, in short",blocks:[{p:"Topstep works through a gradual process: the trader starts with an evaluation in a simulated trading environment, and has to reach a profit target within risk limits set in advance. After passing the evaluation you can activate an Express Funded Account (XFA) - a simulated funded account that lets you submit payout requests under the firm's terms."},{ol:["Choose an account size and buy an evaluation (Trading Combine).","Evaluation - a simulated account with a profit target and risk limits.","Simulated funded account (Express Funded Account) - build up profit and submit payout requests under the eligibility terms.","Payouts - once you meet the terms of the path you chose.","Move to a Live Funded Account - trading the firm's real money. The move doesn't happen automatically when the evaluation ends; it's subject to a process and to Topstep's decision."]},{p:"Each account size has a profit target, a maximum loss limit and a maximum number of contracts. The evaluation works on a {eval_billing} model - with no fixed expiry date as long as the subscription is active."}]},{id:"evaluation",title:"The evaluation stage (Trading Combine)",blocks:[{p:"The evaluation takes place in a simulated trading environment and is meant to test the trader's trading and risk management skills. To pass it you need to reach the profit target, meet the consistency rule and keep within the account's limits. The minimum number of trading days: {eval_min_days}."},{h3:"The Maximum Loss Limit"},{p:"The maximum loss limit is the level that fails the evaluation when reached. On the regular plan the drawdown type is {eval_drawdown}: the loss level updates from the account's highest balance at the end of a trading day - it rises as profit builds up and doesn't drop after losses. Once the level reaches the starting balance it's locked. The level is enforced in real time, including profits and losses on open trades."},{h3:"The Daily Loss Limit"},{p:"Daily loss limit in the regular evaluation: {eval_dll_default}. You can add a daily loss limit (DLL) at purchase with the RTA add-on (Responsible Trading Advantage) - hitting it closes positions and stops trading for that session, but doesn't fail the evaluation by itself. The limit for each size is in the table."},{h3:"The consistency rule (Consistency Target)"},{p:"Your most profitable day must stay at {eval_consistency} or less of the total profit. If one day goes over the limit, the profit target rises accordingly: the new required profit is the best day divided by 0.55. The best day is locked at the end of the session and doesn't reset even when losing days reduce the total profit, and you can pass the evaluation in the minimum number of trading days as long as the limit isn't exceeded."},{h3:"Contract limit and trading days"},{p:"The allowed number of contracts is set by account size, taking into account the conversion ratio between Mini and Micro contracts. You don't have to trade every day, and you can pass the evaluation once you meet the firm's terms."},{live:"topstepEval"},{h3:"Payment, purchase paths and resets"},{p:"The evaluation works on a {eval_billing} model. The purchase paths differ in the monthly payment and in the activation fee required after passing: the Standard path requires a one-time payment to activate the account, and the No Activation Fee path carries no activation fee. An evaluation reset returns the account to its starting state: {eval_reset}."},{live:"topstepPricing"}]},{id:"xfa",title:"After you pass - the XFA account",blocks:[{p:"After passing the Trading Combine you can activate an Express Funded Account (XFA) - an account where trading is simulated, but you can withdraw real profits from it under the firm's terms. You activate the account in the user area, which includes accepting an agreement, choosing a payout path and paying the activation fee if one is required."},{p:"An XFA account starts at a balance of {xfa_starting_balance} - the evaluation's profits don't carry over to it. The account size you chose sets the buying power and the capital available for trading, but it isn't cash available to withdraw."},{h3:"The payout paths: Standard or Consistency"},{p:"When activating the account you choose a payout path: the Standard path is based on building up profitable trading days that meet the threshold the firm sets, with no additional consistency requirement; the Consistency path is based on active days and meeting a consistency rule. The choice is made at activation and can't be changed afterwards."},{live:"topstepXfa"},{h3:"Which rules change after the move"},{ul:["Profit target: {xfa_profit_target} - there's no fixed profit target for further trading; payout eligibility terms apply instead.","Maximum loss limit (MLL): still applies - reaching it closes the account. The drawdown type: {xfa_drawdown}.","Contract limit (Scaling Plan): the allowed number of contracts changes with the account balance and the trader's progress.","Daily loss limit (DLL): may apply depending on the account's settings - hitting it stops trading for that session, but doesn't fail the account by itself.","Consistency rule: applies on the Consistency path; the Standard path has no such requirement for payouts."]},{p:"You can hold several active XFA accounts at once, subject to the firm's limits: {xfa_max_accounts}. Each account is managed under its own terms and the payout path chosen for it."}]},{id:"payouts",title:"Profit payouts",blocks:[{p:"Topstep allows profit payouts from XFA accounts and Live accounts, subject to each account's eligibility terms. The profit split: {xfa_split}. The minimum payout: {xfa_min_payout}."},{h3:"What happens to the account after a payout"},{p:"A payout reduces the account balance and affects the maximum loss limit (MLL). After the first payout on an XFA account the MLL is set at {xfa_mll_floor} for good - the balance left in the account is the trader's safety margin, and reaching the loss level closes the account. A new eligibility cycle also starts, according to the payout path chosen."},{h3:"Payout methods, fees and processing times"},{ul:["Payout methods: {xfa_methods}.","Fee charged by Topstep: {xfa_fee}.","Estimated transfer time: {xfa_timeframe}.","Tax form required: {xfa_docs}."]},{p:"The payout request is submitted through the Topstep Dashboard once you meet the eligibility terms. The request is reviewed and approved, and the time it takes to receive the money depends on the payment method and the destination country."}]},{id:"live",title:"The move to a Live account",blocks:[{p:"A Live Funded Account (LFA) lets the trader trade futures with the firm's real capital, unlike an XFA account where trading is simulated. The move to Live isn't automatic or guaranteed - it happens through a Call Up process in which Topstep's risk management team reviews the trader's performance, trading consistency and payout history."},{p:"The Live account's size is set by {live_tier_sizing}. Once the move is approved: {live_call_up}."},{h3:"The capital structure on a Live account"},{p:"The capital is split into two parts: {live_capital_split}. The reserved capital (Reserve) isn't available for withdrawal until it's released, and it's released through {live_reserve_unlock}, with {live_reserve_review}. The minimum capital available for trading: {live_min_tradable}."},{live:"topstepLive"},{p:"Automated trading on a Live account: {live_automation}. Inactivity: {live_inactivity}."},{h3:"Shoulder Tap - a temporary return to a simulated account"},{p:"The move to Live isn't one-way: the risk team can carry out {live_shoulder_tap} after unusual trading behavior. It's a standard risk management process, not a punishment - the goal is to rebuild consistency before returning to a Live account."},{h3:"Back2Funded - reactivating an XFA"},{p:"An XFA account closed for a rule breach before its first payout can be reactivated up to twice, within 30 calendar days and without a new evaluation. The reactivation cost: {xfa_back2funded}. Choosing a daily loss limit (DLL) at reactivation gives a $50 discount. The option isn't available after a first payout or to Focused Trader Plan participants."}]},{id:"platforms",title:"Trading platforms and connections",blocks:[{p:"Topstep's main trading platform is TopstepX, which lets you trade futures, track performance and manage your accounts. The platform includes charts, order execution, account data and risk management tools. New Trading Combine evaluations are bought through TopstepX."},{live:"platforms"},{p:"There are connection options to external tools - the connections are subject to the firm's terms and aren't available for trading on a Live account."},{links:[{to:"/topstepx-guide",label:"The complete TopstepX guide"}]}]},{id:"key-points",title:"Key features and rules worth knowing",blocks:[{h3:"The key features"},{ul:["An evaluation with no time limit - you can keep going in the evaluation as long as the subscription is active.","A choice of payment path - between a path with an activation fee and a path with none.","Managing accounts at once - {xfa_max_accounts}, subject to the firm's limits.","A chance to trade real money - suitable traders may move up to a Live account, by the firm's decision."]},{h3:"Rules and limits worth knowing"},{ul:["A renewing subscription - a Trading Combine evaluation carries a monthly charge as long as the subscription is active.","Keeping the evaluation's rules - you need to reach the profit target and meet the consistency rule and the risk limits.","Account closure after a breach - reaching the maximum loss limit may fail the evaluation or close the funded account.","Platform purchase - new evaluations are bought through TopstepX.","Trading during economic news in the evaluation: {eval_news}. Around major events (FOMC, CPI, NFP) there are temporary restrictions on opening trades in some contracts - check the firm's policy before trading."]}]},{id:"getting-started",title:"How do you start trading with Topstep?",blocks:[{ol:["Sign up - go to Topstep's official website and create a user account.","Choose an evaluation - choose the Trading Combine account size and the payment path that suits you: Standard Path or No Activation Fee Path.","Buy the evaluation - complete the payment and get access to the evaluation account through the TopstepX platform.","Pass the evaluation - trade in a simulated environment, reach the profit target and meet the consistency rule and the risk limits.","Activate a funded account - after passing the evaluation, activate an Express Funded Account, complete the activation requirements and choose the payout path.","Trade and withdraw profits - keep trading on the funded account, build up profit and submit payout requests under Topstep's eligibility terms."]},{links:[{to:"/top-step",label:"The full firm page"},{to:"/comparison",label:"Compare all the firms"}]}]},{id:"related",title:"More guides (coming soon)",blocks:[{ul:["How do you buy an evaluation at Topstep? (coming soon)","How do you withdraw money from Topstep? (coming soon)","A comparison of the prop firms (coming soon)","A video guide to Topstep (coming soon)"]}]}],faq:[{q:"Does Topstep have a daily loss limit (DLL)?",a:"Daily loss limit in the regular Trading Combine: {eval_dll_default}. You can choose a daily loss limit at purchase with the RTA add-on - hitting it stops trading for that session, but doesn't fail the evaluation by itself. The limit for each account size is in the evaluation table."},{q:"What's the difference between the Standard and No Activation Fee paths?",a:"The Standard path has a lower monthly payment but requires an activation fee after passing the evaluation; the No Activation Fee path has a higher monthly payment with no activation fee after passing. The choice depends on how long you expect to take to pass and the total cost of each path."},{q:"What's the difference between the Standard and Consistency paths on the XFA account?",a:"The Standard path is based on building up profitable trading days that meet the threshold the firm sets ({xfa_standard_days} of at least {xfa_standard_day_min} each), with no consistency requirement. The Consistency path requires active days and meeting a consistency rule. On both paths you can withdraw profits under the terms."},{q:"How do profit payouts work at Topstep?",a:"Once you meet the eligibility terms you can request a payout from an XFA or Live account. The minimum payout is {xfa_min_payout}, you can withdraw up to {xfa_payout_pct} per request (subject to the cap by path and size), and the profit split is {xfa_split}."},{q:"What happens to the account after the first payout?",a:"On an XFA account the maximum loss limit (MLL) is set at {xfa_mll_floor} for good after the first payout, and the remaining balance is the safety margin. A new eligibility cycle also starts, according to the payout path chosen."},{q:"Is the move to a Live account automatic?",a:"No. The move to Live happens through a Call Up process in which the risk management team reviews the trader's performance, and it's subject to the firm's decision - it isn't guaranteed. Once the move is approved: {live_call_up}."},{q:"Can you trade during economic news?",a:"In the evaluation: {eval_news}. However, around major events such as FOMC, CPI and NFP there are temporary restrictions on opening trades in some contracts. Check the firm's policy before trading on the account."},{q:"How long does it take to get a payout?",a:"The request is first reviewed and approved by Topstep, and the time it takes to receive the money depends on the payment method and the destination country. Estimated transfer time: {xfa_timeframe}. The firm's fee: {xfa_fee}."}],sources:[{label:"Topstep - official website",href:"https://www.topstep.com/"},{label:"Topstep Help Center",href:"https://help.topstep.com/"}]},$={title:"Take Profit Trader - the complete guide (2026)",seoTitle:"Take Profit Trader - The Complete Guide | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"The complete guide to Take Profit Trader: the evaluation, the PRO and PRO+ accounts, the trading rules, the loss limits, profit payouts, the platforms and up-to-date discount codes.",excerpt:"How the firm works, the rules at each stage and the payout terms, with live data from the site's database.",category:"Firm guides",coverAlt:"Take Profit Trader guide 2026",intro:["Take Profit Trader (TPT) is a prop firm specializing in futures trading. The firm lets traders prove their trading and risk management skills through an evaluation (Test), and then move up to a PRO account, where they can build up profit and submit payout requests under the firm's rules.","Take Profit Trader's model is based on simulated trading in the evaluation and on the PRO account. The trader has to reach a profit target within risk limits set in advance, and after passing the evaluation can move up to a PRO account. Later, suitable traders may move to a PRO+ account, where trading is done with real money."],sections:[{id:"overview",title:"Take Profit Trader",blocks:[{p:"Take Profit Trader's funding program has three main stages:"},{ul:["Test: an evaluation in a simulated trading environment.","PRO: a simulated funded account that allows profit payouts under the firm's terms.","PRO+: a live trading account; moving to it depends on a review of the trader's performance and on the firm's decision."]},{p:"The firm offers several account sizes, each with its own profit target, loss limit and maximum number of contracts. The evaluation is paid for with a renewing monthly subscription, which ends automatically once you pass the evaluation."},{live:"pricingTable"}]},{id:"how-it-works",title:"How does the model work?",blocks:[{p:"In short: choose an account → evaluation (Test) → simulated funded account (PRO) → profit payouts → a possible move to a live trading account (PRO+)."},{p:"The trader chooses an account size and starts with the Test evaluation, where they need to reach the profit target and meet the firm's consistency, trading days and risk limit requirements. After passing the evaluation and completing the activation process, they can move up to a PRO account."},{p:"On the PRO account trading continues in a simulated environment, but the trader can build up profit and submit payout requests under the firm's terms. Later, suitable traders may be invited to move to a PRO+ account, where trading is done in the real market."},{p:"The move to PRO+ isn't automatic or guaranteed. The firm reviews the trader's performance, risk management, consistency and trading discipline, and the final decision is at its discretion."}]},{id:"evaluation",title:"The evaluation stage",blocks:[{p:"A Take Profit Trader evaluation takes place in a simulated trading environment and is meant to test the trader's trading and risk management skills. To pass it you need to reach the profit target, meet the consistency and trading days requirements and keep within the account's limits."},{live:"evaluationTable"},{h3:"The profit target"},{p:"The profit target is set by the account size you choose. Passing the evaluation requires reaching the target along with the firm's other trading rules."},{h3:"The Maximum Trailing Drawdown"},{p:"In the evaluation an End-of-Day (EOD) Trailing Drawdown applies."},{p:"The loss level updates from the account's highest balance at the end of a trading day. It rises as profit builds up and doesn't drop after losses. When the level reaches the account's starting balance, it's locked and stops rising."},{note:"Important: although the level updates at the end of the day, it's enforced in real time. Reaching it during trading, even through losses on open trades, fails the evaluation."},{h3:"The consistency rule"},{p:"The consistency rule limits how much of the account's total profit can come from the most profitable trading day."},{p:"If the trader reaches the profit target but doesn't meet the consistency requirement, the evaluation isn't failed. Instead, they need to keep building up profit until they meet the requirement. The required profit target may update according to their performance."},{h3:"Minimum trading days"},{p:"Take Profit Trader requires a minimum number of active trading days to pass the evaluation. A trading day counts as active when at least one trade was made in it."},{p:"Reaching the profit target before completing the required number of days isn't enough to pass the evaluation. You also need to complete the trading days requirement."},{h3:"The contract limit (Maximum Position Size)"},{p:"The maximum number of contracts is set by account size. Going over the allowed position limit breaches the firm's rules."},{h3:"Trading hours and allowed instruments"},{p:"Trading is limited to the instruments and hours Take Profit Trader has approved. Positions must be closed according to the set trading hours, and you may not hold trades open between sessions."},{h3:"Payment and evaluation length"},{p:"The evaluation runs on a renewing monthly subscription, with no fixed time limit to complete it as long as the subscription is active."},{p:"Once you pass the evaluation, the subscription is cancelled automatically. If you breach the evaluation's rules, the subscription isn't cancelled automatically and you need to cancel it yourself to avoid further charges."},{h3:"Evaluation reset"},{p:"You can reset the evaluation account under the firm's policy. A reset lets you start over with the account's original terms, costs a fee and doesn't change the subscription's renewal date."},{live:"resetTable"}]},{id:"pro",title:"What happens after you pass the evaluation?",blocks:[{p:"After passing the evaluation, the trader can activate a PRO funded account. Trading on this account continues in a simulated environment, but you can build up profit and submit payout requests under the firm's terms."},{h3:"Activating a PRO account"},{p:"Once passing the evaluation is approved, the trader needs to complete the activation process in their personal area, which includes signing the firm's documents and paying an activation fee, unless they're eligible for an exemption through a benefit or credit."},{p:"After the account is activated there's no renewing monthly subscription."},{h3:"What changes after passing the evaluation?"},{ul:["Profit target: there's no longer a fixed profit target for moving to a next stage. Payout eligibility terms apply instead.","Maximum loss limit (drawdown): the PRO account uses an Intraday Trailing Drawdown, unlike the EOD mechanism in the evaluation.","The consistency rule: the evaluation's consistency requirement doesn't apply to payouts from a PRO account.","Contract limit: the allowed number of contracts is set by account size, with no scaling plan based on profit."]},{h3:"The intraday drawdown mechanism"},{p:"On a PRO account, the loss level updates in real time with the account's peak balance, including unrealized open profit. The level can rise with profits, but doesn't drop after losses. When it reaches the account's starting balance, it's locked and stops rising."},{p:"Reaching the loss level at any point, even during an open trade, closes the account."},{h3:"What is the Buffer on a PRO account?"},{p:"The Buffer is a profit margin the trader needs to build up in the account before they can withdraw profits normally. Its size is set by the account's maximum loss limit."},{live:"bufferTable"},{p:"Once the Buffer is built up, you can request a payout from the profit above it, under the profit split the firm set. It's important to tell apart the option to submit a payout request from the first trading day and the requirement to build up the Buffer first."},{p:"The firm also allows a partial payout from the Buffer after the account is closed, under its payout policy."},{h3:"Managing PRO accounts"},{p:"You can hold several active funded accounts at once, subject to the firm's limits."},{p:"To keep a PRO account active, you also need to meet a periodic trading activity requirement and the firm's risk management rules."}]},{id:"pro-plus",title:"The next account and the move to Live at Take Profit Trader",blocks:[{p:"After a period of trading on a PRO account, the trader may get an invitation to move to a PRO+ account, where trading is done in the real market with the firm's capital."},{p:"The move isn't automatic or guaranteed. Take Profit Trader reviews the trader's performance over time, including risk management, consistency, how trades are executed and trading discipline. The final decision is at the firm's discretion."},{h3:"How does the move to PRO+ happen?"},{p:"A trader chosen for the move gets an official invitation from the firm and needs to complete the onboarding process, including documents and agreements with the broker."},{p:"Once the move is complete, the PRO account is frozen, and part of the profit built up on it is kept as a frozen balance under the firm's policy. The rest of the profit beyond that amount can be withdrawn under the PRO account's split terms."},{p:"If a PRO+ account is closed with a negative balance, the firm may offset the loss against the profit frozen on the PRO account, under its policy."},{h3:"How does a PRO+ account work?"},{p:"A PRO+ account starts with a zero trading balance, with a loss allowance set by the original account. Unlike the PRO account, which uses an Intraday Trailing Drawdown, a PRO+ account uses an End-of-Day (EOD) Drawdown."},{p:"The loss level updates with the profit built up at the end of the trading day, but it's also enforced during the day, including losses on open trades. When the level reaches the zero balance, it's locked."},{h3:"Special limits and rules"},{ul:["Trading activity: periodic trading activity is required to keep the account active.","Economic news: positions must be closed and open orders cancelled around news events the firm prohibits trading through.","Opposite positions: you may not hold positions in opposite directions in the same instrument or in closely related instruments.","Market limits: avoid holding positions when the market reaches the price limits set by the exchange.","Account limit: you can hold a limited number of active funded accounts, under the firm's policy."]},{p:"Breaking certain rules may lead to the account being closed."},{h3:"The PRO+ Development account"},{p:"Take Profit Trader also runs a live trading account called PRO+ Development, for traders the firm chooses to bring in under more limited risk management. This account may have stricter contract and loss limits than a regular PRO+ account, under the firm's policy."},{h3:"Payouts from a PRO+ account"},{p:"A PRO+ account has no Buffer requirement for profit payouts, and its profit split differs from the PRO account's. The details of the payout terms, the profit split and how balances are handled if the account is closed are in the payouts chapter."}]},{id:"payouts",title:"Profit payouts at Take Profit Trader",blocks:[{p:"Take Profit Trader lets traders submit profit payout requests from PRO and PRO+ accounts, subject to the eligibility terms and the firm's policy. The payout terms vary by account type, mainly in the Buffer requirement and the profit split."},{h3:"Payouts from a PRO account"},{p:"On a PRO account you can request a profit payout from the very first trading day, with no requirement to build up a minimum number of profitable trading days. However, you first need to build up the required Buffer."},{p:"The Buffer equals the account's maximum loss limit. Once it's built up, you can withdraw profit under the firm's profit split, while keeping the Buffer in the account."},{p:"The firm lets you submit payout requests every day, as long as there's profit available to withdraw and the account meets the terms."},{h3:"What happens to the Buffer when a PRO account is closed?"},{p:"If a PRO account is closed, the firm lets you withdraw part of the Buffer built up, under its profit split policy."},{p:"It's important to tell apart the profit available to withdraw while the account is active from the amount you can receive from the Buffer after it's closed."},{links:[{to:"/blog/takeprofit-buffer-withdrawal",label:"How to withdraw money from the Take Profit buffer"}]},{h3:"Payouts from a PRO+ account"},{p:"A PRO+ account has no Buffer requirement for profit payouts, and the trader's profit split differs from the PRO account's. Payouts are made from the profit available on the live trading account, subject to the firm's rules and the account's limits."},{h3:"Payout fees and limits"},{p:"The firm charges no wallet withdrawal fee when the requested amount is above the threshold set in its policy. Smaller payouts carry a fee, and the payment provider may charge additional fees."},{links:[{to:"/blog/takeprofit-to-interactive-brokers-via-wise",label:"A guide to withdrawing from Take Profit to Interactive Brokers through Wise"}]}]},{id:"platforms",title:"Trading platforms and connections at Take Profit Trader",blocks:[{p:"Take Profit Trader lets traders choose from a range of futures trading platforms, through two main data providers: CQG and Rithmic."},{live:"platforms"},{p:"The data provider is chosen when you sign up, and it affects which platforms you can use. Before buying an account it's important to check that the platform you plan to trade on is supported by the connection offered."},{ul:["Rithmic: a connection that works with compatible trading platforms, including Quantower, Bookmap, MotiveWave, Jigsaw, R|Trader Pro, MultiCharts and ATAS, and other platforms on the firm's official list.","CQG: a connection used by platforms such as Tradovate, NinjaTrader and TradingView."]},{h3:"Switching platforms"},{p:"You can switch between platforms that support the same data provider during the subscription period, with no special approval from the firm. You switch using the connection details in your personal area, following the login instructions of the platform you want."},{p:"Note that on a Rithmic connection there are limits on logging in to several platforms at once, while on Test and PRO accounts based on CQG you can be connected to two platforms at once."}]},{id:"key-points",title:"Features and points worth knowing at Take Profit Trader",blocks:[{h3:"Key features"},{ul:["An evaluation with no fixed time limit: you can keep going in the evaluation as long as the subscription is active.","Profit payouts: you can submit payout requests from a PRO account every day, after building up the required Buffer.","No consistency rule on the PRO account: the consistency requirement applies in the evaluation, but not to payouts from the funded account.","A range of platforms: the firm supports various trading platforms through CQG and Rithmic connections.","Managing accounts at once: you can hold several active PRO accounts, subject to the firm's limits."]},{h3:"Rules and limits worth knowing"},{ul:["A renewing monthly subscription: an evaluation carries a monthly charge as long as the subscription is active. Failing the evaluation doesn't cancel the subscription automatically.","Activation fee: moving to a PRO account carries an activation fee, unless you're eligible for an exemption or credit.","Drawdown mechanism: the evaluation uses an EOD drawdown, while the PRO account uses an intraday drawdown that also counts open profits.","Buffer requirement: regular payouts from a PRO account require building up profit above the Buffer set for the account.","Trading limits: the firm prohibits automated trading and opposite positions, and PRO and PRO+ accounts have restrictions on trading during certain economic news.","Account activity: PRO and PRO+ accounts require periodic trading activity under the firm's policy."]}]},{id:"getting-started",title:"How do you start trading with Take Profit Trader?",blocks:[{ol:["Sign up: go to Take Profit Trader's official website and create a user account.","Choose an evaluation account: choose the Test account size and the connection provider that suits you, complete the purchase and get the login details.","Connect to the trading platform: log in to a supported platform through CQG or Rithmic, depending on the provider you chose.","Pass the evaluation: trade the simulated account and work toward the profit target, while meeting the consistency rule, the trading days requirement and the risk limits.","Activate a PRO account: after passing the evaluation and completing the approval process, activate the PRO account under the firm's terms and payment requirements.","Trade and withdraw profits: trade the PRO account, build up the required Buffer and submit profit payout requests under the firm's policy."]}]},{id:"related",title:"Related guides",blocks:[{links:[{to:"/take-profit",label:"The Take Profit Trader page on the site"},{to:"/blog/takeprofit-buffer-withdrawal",label:"Withdrawing from the buffer"},{to:"/blog/takeprofit-to-interactive-brokers-via-wise",label:"Withdrawing through Wise"}]}]}],faq:[{q:"What account types does Take Profit Trader offer?",a:"The firm offers three main stages: Test - a simulated evaluation account; PRO - a simulated funded account that allows profit payouts; and PRO+ - an account for trading with real money, which you move to by the firm's decision."},{q:"How is the maximum loss (drawdown) calculated at Take Profit Trader?",a:"The evaluation uses an EOD Trailing Drawdown, which updates from the end-of-day balance. The PRO account uses an Intraday Trailing Drawdown, which also counts open profits in real time. The PRO+ account uses an EOD mechanism. Breaching the loss limit may close the account."},{q:"What are Take Profit Trader's consistency rules?",a:"In the evaluation you need to spread your profit across trading days, so the most profitable day isn't too large a share of the total profit. A minimum number of trading days is also required. The PRO account has no consistency rule for payouts."},{q:"How do profit payouts to a bank account work?",a:"Once you meet the payout terms, the funds move from the PRO or PRO+ account to your personal wallet (TPT Wallet). From there you can request payment through Plaid for US banks, or through Wise and PayPal, depending on service availability. How long it takes to receive the money depends on the payment method."},{q:"Can you trade during economic news releases?",a:"In the evaluation, news trading is allowed. On PRO and PRO+ accounts there are restrictions around certain releases, including FOMC, NFP and CPI. During the prohibited time windows you may not hold positions or active orders."},{q:"Can you open opposite positions (hedging) or use bots?",a:"No. The firm prohibits holding positions in opposite directions in the same instrument or in related instruments, including across different accounts. Trading with bots and automated systems is also prohibited, and trades must be placed manually."},{q:"How many accounts can you run at once at Take Profit Trader?",a:"You can hold an unlimited number of Test accounts, but the number of active PRO and PRO+ accounts is limited. There's also a separate limit on activating passed evaluations within a set period."},{q:"Which trading platforms does Take Profit Trader support?",a:"The firm supports platforms such as TradingView, Tradovate, NinjaTrader, Quantower, Bookmap and R|Trader, through the CQG and Rithmic connection providers. Platform availability and license costs vary by provider and platform."},{q:"Is there a recommended promo code for signing up with Take Profit Trader?",a:"Deals and discount codes may change from time to time. Check the up-to-date benefits on the firm's page on AntarTrade and make sure the discount applies before completing the purchase."}],sources:[{label:"Take Profit Trader - Official Website",href:"https://takeprofittrader.com/"},{label:"Finding Your Accounts",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15170470213789-Finding-Your-Accounts"},{label:"Test Subscriptions",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15141145057053-Test-Subscriptions"},{label:"Resetting Your Test Account",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15140989806493-Resetting-Your-Test-Account"},{label:"The Core Rules",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/sections/15168850786589-The-5-Core-Rules"},{label:"Rule 1: Hit Your Profit Target",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15169070804125-Rule-1-Hit-Your-Profit-Target"},{label:"Rule 3: EOD Maximum Trailing Drawdown",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15170265979165-Rule-3-Do-Not-Hit-End-Of-Day-EOD-Maximum-Trailing-Drawdown"},{label:"Rule 5: Be Consistent",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15170316538013-Rule-5-Be-Consistent"},{label:"Universal Trading Policies",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/34431153546397-TakeProfitTrader-Universal-Trading-Policies-UTP"},{label:"Understanding the Simulation for Your PRO Account",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172725020701-Understanding-the-Simulation-for-Your-PRO-account"},{label:"How to Activate a PRO Account Using Credit",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/28923632631581-How-to-Activate-a-PRO-Account-Using-Credit"},{label:"PRO Account Rules",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15171769361053-PRO-Account-Rules"},{label:"PRO Account Profit Split & Withdrawal Rules",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172219527581-PRO-Account-Profit-Split-Withdrawal-Rules"},{label:"Rules for Multiple Accounts",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172695563933-Rules-for-Multiple-Accounts"},{label:"PRO+ Account Upgrade Process - Overview and Guidelines",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15171978600349-PRO-Account-Upgrade-Process-Overview-and-Guidelines"},{label:"Advantages of PRO+",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15171929948829-Advantages-of-PRO"},{label:"PRO+ Account Rules",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172006753821-PRO-Account-Rules"},{label:"PRO+ Development Accounts",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/36429526878237-PRO-Development-Accounts"},{label:"PRO+ Development: 30-Day Live-Capital Cooldown",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/39331980656925-PRO-Development-30-Day-Live-Capital-Cooldown"},{label:"How to Withdraw from PRO Account to the Wallet",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172253980061-How-to-Withdraw-from-PRO-Account-to-the-Wallet"},{label:"How to Withdraw from the Wallet",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15965203144477-How-to-Withdraw-from-the-Wallet"},{label:"Speed of Payouts Update: Q&A",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/37697356049949-Speed-of-Payouts-Update-Q-A"},{label:"Payout System",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172296875165-Payout-System"},{label:"Withdrawal Fees",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172354954525-Withdrawal-Fees"},{label:"Choosing Your Platform",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15173017163549-Choosing-Your-Platform"},{label:"Connection Instructions",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/categories/15135936007709-Connection-Instructions"},{label:"Connecting TradingView with Your CQG Account",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15173215468061-Connecting-TradingView-with-Your-CQG-Account"},{label:"Connecting NinjaTrader 8 with Rithmic",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172869346589-Connecting-NinjaTrader-8-with-Rithmic"},{label:"Being Connected in Two Places At the Same Time",href:"https://takeprofittraderhelp.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/15172958310173-Being-Connected-in-Two-Places-At-the-Same-Time"}]},j={title:"FTMO - the complete guide to FTMO futures (2026)",seoTitle:"FTMO - The Complete Guide 2026 | AntarTrade",seoDescription:"The complete guide to FTMO: the Growth and Pro plans, the evaluation, the funded account, the trading rules, the loss limits, profit payouts and the trading platforms.",excerpt:"Evaluation, Sim-Funded and Live Funded: the differences between Growth and Pro, the rules at each stage and the payout terms, with live data from the site's database.",category:"Firm guides",coverAlt:"FTMO futures guide 2026",intro:["FTMO is a prop firm that offers, among other things, futures trading programs. Through FTMO Futures, traders can prove their trading and risk management skills through an evaluation, and then move up to a simulated funded account (Sim-Funded Account), where they can build up profit and submit payout requests under the firm's terms.","The model is based on simulated trading, while meeting profit targets and risk limits set in advance. After passing the evaluation and completing the firm's requirements, you can move up to a Sim-Funded account. Later, traders who meet FTMO's criteria may be invited to a Live Funded account, where trading is done with real capital."],sections:[{id:"overview",title:"FTMO",blocks:[{p:"FTMO Futures offers two main plans:"},{ul:["Growth: a plan with an evaluation that has no separate daily loss limit, and a soft daily loss limit (Soft Limit) on the funded account.","Pro: a plan with a hard daily loss limit (Hard Limit), along with profit target, risk management and payout terms that differ from the Growth plan."]},{p:"Both plans come in different account sizes, and differ in the evaluation's terms, the risk limits and the payout rules. On both you need to pass an evaluation before getting a Sim-Funded account."},{p:"The evaluation is paid for with a renewing monthly subscription. After passing the evaluation, there's no extra activation fee to get the funded account."},{live:"ftmoPricing"}]},{id:"how-it-works",title:"How does the model work?",blocks:[{p:"In short: choose a plan and an account size → evaluation → simulated funded account (Sim-Funded Account) → profit payouts → a possible move to a live trading account (Live Funded Account)."},{p:"After passing the evaluation, verifying your identity and signing the firm's agreement, you can move up to a Sim-Funded account. On this account trading continues in a simulated environment, but you can build up profit and submit payout requests under the plan's terms. At this stage there's no longer a profit target or a consistency rule, but the risk limits still apply."},{p:"Later, traders with consistent performance and a history of approved payouts may be invited to a Live Funded account, where trading is done with FTMO's real capital."}]},{id:"evaluation",title:"The evaluation stage",blocks:[{p:"The firm offers two evaluation plans, Growth and Pro. On both you need to reach the profit target, meet the consistency rule and keep within the risk limits. The main differences between the plans concern the profit targets, the daily loss limit and the consistency requirements."},{live:"ftmoEval"},{h3:"The profit target"},{p:"The profit target is the amount the trader needs to build up on the evaluation account to qualify for a Sim-Funded account. The target is set by plan and account size. Reaching the profit target alone isn't enough to pass the evaluation; you also need to meet the consistency rule and the firm's other terms."},{h3:"The Maximum Drawdown"},{p:"Both plans use an End-of-Day (EOD) Trailing Drawdown. The loss level is calculated from the account's highest balance recorded at the end of a trading day. It rises as profit builds up and doesn't drop after losses."},{p:"Although the loss level updates from the end-of-day balance, it's enforced in real time against the account's equity, including profits and losses on open positions and commissions. When the loss level reaches the account's starting balance, it's locked and stops rising."},{h3:"The Daily Loss Limit"},{p:"The Growth and Pro plans differ in the daily loss limit:"},{ul:["Growth: the evaluation has no separate daily loss limit. Risk management relies on the maximum loss limit (Maximum Drawdown).","Pro: there's a hard daily loss limit (Hard Limit). Hitting it closes the positions and fails the evaluation."]},{p:"On the Pro plan, the daily limit is calculated from the account balance at the start of the trading day and enforced in real time, including the results of open trades and commissions."},{h3:"The consistency rule"},{p:"Both plans have a consistency rule, which limits how much of the total profit made in the evaluation can come from the most profitable trading day. The consistency requirement differs between Growth and Pro: {growth_eval_consistency} on Growth and {pro_eval_consistency} on Pro."},{p:"If the trader reached the profit target but doesn't meet the consistency rule, the evaluation isn't failed. They need to keep building up profit until the most profitable day's share meets the requirement."},{h3:"Minimum trading days"},{p:"FTMO has no separate requirement for a fixed number of trading days to pass the evaluation. However, the consistency rule in practice requires spreading your profit across several trading days. The minimum possible number of days to pass differs between Growth and Pro. The firm checks whether you meet the evaluation's terms after the trading day ends."},{h3:"The contract limit (Maximum Contracts)"},{p:"The maximum number of contracts is set by plan and account size. The limit refers to all contracts held at the same time across all instruments, not to each instrument separately. Going over the allowed position limit breaches the trading rules."},{h3:"Payment and evaluation length"},{p:"The evaluation runs on a renewing monthly subscription, with no fixed time limit to complete it as long as the subscription is active. Once you pass the evaluation and meeting its terms is approved, the subscription ends automatically and there's no extra activation fee for the Sim-Funded account."},{h3:"Evaluation reset"},{p:"If you fail the evaluation, you can buy a reset under the firm's policy. A reset returns the evaluation account to its starting terms, but doesn't change the subscription's monthly billing date. Alternatively, you can wait for the subscription to renew, which gives you a new evaluation account."}]},{id:"sim-funded",title:"What happens after you pass the evaluation?",blocks:[{p:"After passing the evaluation and completing the firm's requirements, the trader moves up to a simulated funded account (Sim-Funded Account), for the plan and account size chosen. Trading continues in a simulated environment, but you can build up profit and submit payout requests under FTMO Futures' terms."},{h3:"Activating the funded account"},{p:"Once passing the evaluation is approved, the trader needs to complete identity verification (KYC) and sign the firm's agreement to get a Sim-Funded account. The account size and plan stay the same as the evaluation account that was passed. There's no extra activation fee or renewing monthly subscription for the funded account."},{h3:"What changes after passing the evaluation?"},{ul:["Profit target: a Sim-Funded account has no fixed profit target for completing another stage. Payout eligibility terms apply instead.","The consistency rule: the evaluation's consistency requirement doesn't apply on the funded account.","Maximum loss limit (drawdown): both plans keep the EOD Trailing Drawdown.","Daily loss limit on Growth: a Soft Limit applies. Hitting it closes the positions and stops trading until the next trading day, without closing the account by itself.","Daily loss limit on Pro: a Hard Limit applies. Hitting it closes the positions and ends the account.","Contract limit (Scaling Plan): the number of contracts allowed at the start of the funded account is lower than the maximum, and it may grow with the profit built up on the account."]},{live:"ftmoFunded"},{h3:"The contract scaling plan (Scaling Plan)"},{p:"Sim-Funded accounts have a scaling plan, which sets the maximum number of contracts you can hold by the account's profit. The tiers are calculated from the profit built up above the account's starting balance at the end of the trading day. When the account reaches a higher profit tier, the contract limit updates under the scaling plan, from the next trading day."},{p:"Note that scaling also works in reverse: if the account's profit drops to a lower tier, the allowed number of contracts may drop accordingly."},{h3:"Managing Sim-Funded accounts"},{p:"You can hold several active Sim-Funded accounts at once, subject to FTMO Futures' total account limit ({max_accounts}). The limit is shared by the Growth and Pro plans, and you can't get around it by opening more user accounts."},{p:"Later, traders who meet the firm's criteria may be invited to a Live Funded account, where trading is done with real money. The move isn't automatic or guaranteed."}]},{id:"live",title:"The next account and the move to Live Funded",blocks:[{p:"After trading on a simulated funded account (Sim-Funded Account), traders with consistent performance and a history of approved payouts may be invited to a Live Funded account, where futures are traded with FTMO's real capital (the move isn't automatic or guaranteed, and it's at the firm's discretion)."},{h3:"How does the move to a Live account happen?"},{p:"FTMO reviews traders' performance on Sim-Funded accounts, including trading consistency and the history of approved payouts. You can't apply for a Live Funded account yourself. If the firm decides to invite a trader to this stage, its trading department contacts them and guides them through the rest of the process."},{h3:"What changes on a Live Funded account?"},{ul:["Sim-Funded Account: trading is done in a simulation based on real market data, with the option to receive cash payouts according to performance and the firm's terms.","Live Funded Account: trading is done in the real market, with FTMO's capital."]},{h3:"Limits on getting a Live account"},{p:"Eligibility for a Live Funded account is also subject to geographic restrictions and regulatory requirements. FTMO doesn't allow residents of certain countries to move to a Live account, even when they may hold a Sim-Funded account and request payouts from it. The list of countries and restrictions may change with the firm's policy and legal requirements."}]},{id:"payouts",title:"Profit payouts",blocks:[{p:"On FTMO Futures' Sim-Funded accounts you can submit profit payout requests once you meet the plan's eligibility terms. The Growth and Pro plans differ in the profitable trading days required, the payout frequency, the share of profit you can request and the payout cap."},{live:"ftmoPayouts"},{h3:"Payout Eligibility"},{p:"To be eligible for a payout, the trader needs to build up a set number of profitable trading days (Qualifying Days), on which the realized daily profit meets the minimum the firm set."},{ul:["Growth: eligibility is based on completing profitable trading days under the plan's and the account size's requirements.","Pro: you also need to complete profitable trading days, but the number of days and the daily profit threshold differ from the Growth plan."]},{p:"Qualifying days are checked at the end of the trading day. A day whose profit is below the required threshold doesn't count as a qualifying day for a payout. Once the terms are met you can submit a payout request, subject to the plan's limits and the profit available on the account."},{h3:"How much of the profit can you withdraw?"},{p:"The plans differ in the share of profit you can request in each payout cycle:"},{ul:["Growth: you can request a payout of part of the available profit above the account's starting balance. The profit not withdrawn stays in the account.","Pro: you can request a payout of all the available profit above the account's starting balance, subject to the payout cap and the firm's other terms."]},{p:"Both plans have a payout cap per request, by plan and account size."},{h3:"The new profit requirement (Minimum New Profit)"},{p:"In each payout request, a minimum part of the requested amount must be based on new profit made in the current payout cycle. This means you can't necessarily withdraw all the profit left in the account from earlier cycles without building up new profit. The requirement applies even when the trader has already completed the qualifying trading days for a payout."},{h3:"The profit split"},{p:"FTMO Futures works on a profit split model between the trader and the firm. Once the payout request is approved, the trader receives their share under the set split, and the rest goes to the firm. The split is the same on the Growth and Pro plans."},{h3:"How a payout affects the account and the drawdown"},{p:"The requested payout amount is deducted from the Sim-Funded account balance. It's important to tell apart the account balance and the maximum loss level (Maximum Drawdown). Withdrawing profit reduces the account balance, but doesn't lower the loss level already set."},{p:"On the Pro plan you can request a payout of all the available profit above the account's starting balance, subject to the payout cap and the other terms. However, the payout reduces the account balance and doesn't lower the drawdown level already set. So a payout that leaves the account balance at the maximum loss level may end the account. On the Growth plan too, take into account the distance left between the account balance after the payout and the loss level."},{h3:"The payout approval process"},{p:"The payout request is submitted through FTMO Futures' personal area. When you confirm the request in the personal area, open positions and pending orders are closed. The requested amount is deducted from the account balance and the request goes to the firm for approval."},{p:"After submitting the request you can go back to trading the account, subject to the risk limits and the remaining balance. Payment is made once the request is approved and the relevant requirements are completed."}]},{id:"platforms",title:"Trading platforms and connections at FTMO",blocks:[{p:"FTMO lets you trade futures through various trading platforms connected to the firm's trading infrastructure. The platforms supported today are:"},{ul:["NinjaTrader","Tradovate","TradingView"]}]},{id:"key-points",title:"Features and points worth knowing at FTMO",blocks:[{live:"ftmoRules"},{h3:"Key features"},{ul:["Two evaluation plans: a choice between Growth and Pro, with differences in the loss limits, the consistency requirements and the payout terms.","An evaluation with no fixed time limit: you can keep going in the evaluation as long as the subscription is active and the account keeps the rules.","A move with no extra activation fee: after passing the evaluation you can move up to a Sim-Funded account with no separate activation payment.","Trading during news ({news}): subject to the risk management rules and the ban on prohibited trading patterns.","Copying trades between your own accounts ({copy_trading}): as long as each account keeps the rules on its own.","Automated trading ({automated}): you can use algorithmic strategies and automated systems that don't break the firm's rules."]},{h3:"Rules and limits worth knowing"},{ul:["Trading hours ({trading_hours}): positions must be closed and active orders cancelled before the allowed trading day ends. Holding positions between sessions or over the weekend is prohibited.","Hedging ({hedging}): you may not hedge or offset risk between different accounts, including accounts at other firms. Opposite positions within the same account are excluded from the ban.","External copy trading: you may not copy the trades or trading decisions of other traders, including through signal services, copier software or automated systems.","HFT ({hft}): high-frequency strategies, exploiting delays in market data and using systems that give an unfair advantage in the simulation are prohibited.","Risk management: you may not rely on the platform's drawdown mechanism as your only way out of trades. The firm also prohibits abnormal trading patterns that don't reflect reasonable risk management.","Consistency in how you trade: a major, inconsistent change in trading patterns, position size or activity concentrated around news, which doesn't reflect a sustainable strategy in real market conditions, may be considered a rule breach.","Exchange price limits: you may not open new positions within the defined distance of CME's daily price limits.","Inactivity: {inactivity_funded}. This limit doesn't apply to Live Funded accounts."]}]},{id:"getting-started",title:"How do you start trading with FTMO?",blocks:[{ol:["Sign up and choose a plan and an account size: choose between Growth and Pro and the account size you want. Before buying, get to know the plan's profit targets, loss limits, consistency rule and payout terms.","Buy an evaluation: complete the subscription purchase and get access to the evaluation account and the login details for the supported trading platform.","Complete the evaluation: trade in a simulated environment until you reach the profit target and meet the consistency rule, while keeping the risk limits and the firm's rules.","Move to a Sim-Funded account: once passing the evaluation is approved, complete identity verification (KYC) and sign the firm's agreement. You then get a Sim-Funded account with no extra activation fee.","Start trading and build up payout eligibility: keep trading the simulated funded account, within the plan's limits. Once you meet the eligibility terms you can submit profit payout requests."]}]},{id:"related",title:"Related guides",blocks:[{links:[{to:"/ftmo",label:"The FTMO page on the site"},{to:"/comparison",label:"Compare the prop firms"}]}]}],faq:[{q:"What's the difference between the Growth and Pro plans?",a:"On Growth the evaluation's consistency rule is {growth_eval_consistency}, there's no daily loss limit in the evaluation, and on the funded account you can request up to {growth_payout_pct} of the available profit in each payout cycle. On Pro the consistency rule is {pro_eval_consistency}, there's a hard daily loss limit, and you can request up to {pro_payout_pct} of the available profit, subject to the caps and the payout terms."},{q:"Can you pass the evaluation in one day?",a:"No. There's no separate minimum number of trading days, but the consistency rule requires your most profitable day not to exceed {growth_eval_consistency} of the total profit on Growth or {pro_eval_consistency} on Pro. So in practice you need at least {growth_min_days} profitable trading days on Growth and at least {pro_min_days} on Pro, assuming the evaluation's other terms are also met."},{q:"What happens if you fail the evaluation?",a:"You can buy a reset for a fee and start the evaluation over, with no limit on the number of resets. Alternatively, if the subscription stays active, you get a new evaluation account at the next monthly renewal. A paid reset doesn't change the monthly billing date."},{q:"Is there an activation fee after passing the evaluation?",a:"No. FTMO Futures charges no extra activation fee for a Sim-Funded account. After you pass the evaluation the monthly subscription ends, and there's no subscription charge for the funded account."},{q:"Is there a consistency rule on the funded account?",a:"No. The consistency rule applies only in the evaluation. A Sim-Funded account has no profit target or consistency rule, but the risk limits and payout terms still apply."},{q:"What are the terms for withdrawing profit on a Sim-Funded account?",a:"Growth requires {growth_qdays} qualifying trading days in each payout cycle, and Pro requires {pro_qdays} days. The minimum daily profit varies by account size: on Growth it ranges from {growth_qday_range}, and on Pro from {pro_qday_range}. The profit split on both plans: {profit_split}."},{q:"Can you withdraw all the profit on the funded account?",a:"On Growth you can request up to {growth_payout_pct} of the available profit, and on Pro up to {pro_payout_pct}, subject to the payout cap. The minimum payout is {min_payout}, and new profit made in the current payout cycle is required: {new_profit}."},{q:"Does the account stay active after a profit payout?",a:"The account can stay active as long as the remaining balance stays within the risk limits. The payout reduces the account balance but doesn't lower the drawdown level already set. So, especially on Pro, a request to withdraw all the available profit may end the account."},{q:"Can you trade during news and copy trades?",a:"Trading during news: {news}, subject to the firm's rules. Copying trades: {copy_trading}. You may not copy trades from other traders or hedge between accounts."},{q:"How many accounts can you hold at once?",a:"The limit on active Sim-Funded accounts: {max_accounts}, shared by the Growth and Pro plans. There's no limit on the number of evaluation accounts, however, and each one is charged its own subscription."}],sources:[{label:"FTMO Futures - Official Website",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/"},{label:"How FTMO Futures Works",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/how-it-works/"},{label:"Trading Objectives & Rules",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/trading-objectives-and-rules/"},{label:"Comparison Table",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/comparison-table/"},{label:"Forbidden Trading Practices",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/forbidden-trading-practices/"},{label:"FTMO Futures - FAQ",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/"},{label:"How Do the Growth and Pro Products Differ?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/how-do-the-growth-and-pro-products-differ/"},{label:"Who Is an FTMO Trader, and How Do I Become One?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/ftr-who-is-an-ftmo-trader-and-how-do-i-become-one/"},{label:"Who Is Eligible for a Live Funded Account?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/who-is-eligible-for-a-live-funded-account/"},{label:"What Are the Trading Objectives?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/ftr-what-are-the-trading-objectives/"},{label:"How Long Does It Take to Become an FTMO Trader?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/ftr-how-long-does-it-take-to-become-an-ftmo-trader/"},{label:"How Does the Payment for the Evaluation Work?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/how-does-the-payment-for-the-evaluation-work-is-it-one-time-fee-or-a-subscription/"},{label:"What Is the Maximum Number of Contracts I Can Trade?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/what-is-the-maximum-number-of-contracts-i-can-trade/"},{label:"How Many Accounts Can I Have?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/ftr-how-many-accounts-can-i-have/"},{label:"How Does a Sim-Funded Account Work from the Technical Side?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/how-does-a-sim-funded-account-work-from-the-technical-side/"},{label:"How Do I Withdraw My Payout from a Sim-Funded Account?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/how-do-i-withdraw-my-payout-from-a-sim-funded-account/"},{label:"Which Platforms Can I Use for Trading?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/ftr-which-platforms-can-i-use-for-trading/"},{label:"Which Instruments Can I Trade with FTMO Futures?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/which-instruments-can-i-trade-with-ftmo-futures/"},{label:"How to Start",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/ftr-how-to-start/"},{label:"I Paid for My Evaluation, When Will I Get the Account?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/i-paid-for-my-evaluation-when-will-i-get-the-account/"},{label:"I Have Successfully Passed the Evaluation, What to Do Now?",href:"https://ftmo.com/en/futures/faq/i-have-successfully-passed-the-evaluation-what-to-do-now/"}]},Y={title:"Top One Futures - the complete guide (2026)",seoTitle:"Top One Futures - The Complete Guide 2026",seoDescription:"The complete guide to Top One Futures: the Elite Daily, Elite Access and instant funding plans, the evaluation and funded account rules, profit payouts, the platforms and the move to Live.",excerpt:"Top One's plans, the evaluation and funded rules, the payout terms and the path to Live, with prices and live data from the site's database.",category:"Firm guides",coverAlt:"Top One Futures: a complete guide to the evaluation, plans and accounts",intro:["Top One Futures is a prop firm for futures traders. The firm offers evaluation plans alongside simulated instant funding plans (Instant Funding), where you start trading with no evaluation first. Further along there's a path to a Live account, where trading is done with real capital, under the firm's policy and subject to a review by the risk management team.","This guide explains the plans, the evaluation, the funded account, the payout terms and the move to Live. The changing prices and terms are pulled live from the site's database and update automatically. The guide isn't a rating of or a recommendation for the firm."],sections:[{id:"tracks",title:"Top One Futures' plans",blocks:[{p:"The firm offers two ways to reach a simulated funded account: an evaluation, or joining a funded account directly with no evaluation."},{ul:["Elite Daily: an evaluation plan with an end-of-day (EOD) drawdown, a consistency rule in the evaluation and daily payouts on the funded account.","Elite Access: an evaluation plan with a one-time payment, no daily loss limit and no consistency rule in the evaluation. After passing you pay an activation fee, and the funded account has a consistency rule for payouts.","Elite Access Intraday: the same evaluation as Elite Access, but the funded account has an intraday drawdown.","Instant Sim Funded: an instant funded account with no evaluation, with an end-of-day drawdown, a consistency rule and a profit target in each payout cycle.","Ignite: an instant funded account with no evaluation, with an end-of-day drawdown, its own consistency rule and a profit target in each payout cycle."]},{live:"firmTracks"},{p:"The full prices by account size, including activation and reset fees, are in the following tables."},{live:"firmPricing"}]},{id:"how-it-works",title:"How does Top One's model work?",blocks:[{ol:["Choose a plan, an account size and a trading platform.","On the Elite plans: an evaluation on a simulated account, with a profit target and loss limits. On the Instant and Ignite plans you skip this stage.","Simulated funded account (Sim Funded): build up profit and submit payout requests under the plan's terms. A payout doesn't happen automatically, and it's subject to meeting the requirements and the firm's approval.","Pre-Live and Live: after a number of approved payouts the firm may review the trader for a move to an account with real capital. The move isn't automatic."]},{note:"An instant funded account (Instant or Ignite) also has loss limits, a consistency rule and profit targets for payouts. Instant funding skips only the evaluation stage."}]},{id:"evaluation",title:"The evaluation stage",blocks:[{p:"On the Elite Daily and Elite Access plans you need to pass one evaluation: reach the profit target in closed trades, without crossing the loss limits and while keeping the trading rules. The two plans differ in the consistency rule, the daily loss limit and the length of the evaluation."},{h3:"The maximum loss limit (Drawdown)"},{p:"On both plans the evaluation's drawdown is an end-of-day (EOD) drawdown: the loss level updates from the highest balance recorded at the end of a trading day, not from intraday profit peaks. Note that the active level is also checked during trading, and dropping below it may fail the account even if the price recovers by the end of the day."},{h3:"The Daily Loss Limit"},{p:"Elite Daily has a daily loss limit already in the evaluation. It's calculated from the previous trading day's closing balance, and includes open losses, realized losses and trading costs. Hitting the limit stops trading until the next trading day, as long as the maximum loss limit wasn't crossed."},{p:"Elite Access has no daily loss limit in the evaluation. On this plan you can also choose the No Daily Loss Limit add-on at purchase, which removes the daily limit on the funded account. The add-on can only be chosen on the first purchase, not added at activation or on a reset."},{h3:"The consistency rule"},{p:"The consistency rule limits how much of the total profit can come from the most profitable trading day. On Elite Daily the evaluation has a consistency rule of {ed_eval_consistency}. A trader who reached the target without meeting the requirement isn't failed, and keeps building up profit until it's met. Elite Access has no consistency rule in the evaluation."},{h3:"Trading days and evaluation length"},{p:"Elite Daily has no fixed time limit to complete the evaluation. Elite Access has {ea_eval_time} to complete the evaluation, and you can pass it in a single trading day if all the requirements are met."},{live:"firmEval",tracks:["Elite Daily","Elite Access"]},{note:"Elite Access Intraday takes the same evaluation as Elite Access. The difference between them starts only on the funded account."},{p:"A failed evaluation can be reset and started over, for a fee. The reset prices are in the price table above."}]},{id:"funded",title:"After the evaluation: the funded account (Sim Funded)",blocks:[{p:"After passing the evaluation you move up to a simulated funded account, where you build up profit and submit payout requests. The rules on the funded account may differ from those of the evaluation."},{h3:"Activating the account"},{p:"On Elite Daily the funded account is issued automatically after passing, with no activation fee, and should be available for trading from the start of the next trading session. On Elite Access you pay a one-time activation fee through the dashboard, and only then get the account."},{h3:"What changes on each plan"},{ul:["Elite Daily: an end-of-day drawdown and a daily loss limit, with no consistency rule on the funded account.","Elite Access: an end-of-day drawdown, a daily loss limit (unless the add-on was bought), and a consistency rule of {ea_funded_consistency} for payouts.","Elite Access Intraday: the funded account has an intraday drawdown, which also updates from open profits during trading.","Instant Sim Funded and Ignite: you start directly here, with an end-of-day drawdown, a daily loss limit and a consistency rule for payouts ({instant_consistency} on Instant, {ignite_consistency} on Ignite)."]},{p:"The Elite plans have a contract scaling plan: at the start fewer contracts than the maximum are allowed, and the number rises as the account's profit grows."},{live:"firmFunded"},{p:"Even after getting the funded account you need to keep within the loss limits, the contract limits and the trading rules. Breaking the rules may lead to the account being closed, and keeping them doesn't guarantee automatic payout approval."}]},{id:"live",title:"The move to Pre-Live and Live",blocks:[{p:"Top One may promote traders from Sim Funded accounts to a Pre-Live stage and then to a Live account, based on performance, payout history and a review by the risk management team. The move isn't automatic."},{h3:"When does it happen?"},{p:"After the number of approved payouts the plan requires, the account goes into review. Elite Daily requires {ed_payouts_to_live}, and most other plans {ea_payouts_to_live}. The firm may review a move earlier too. During the review the trader's other accounts are temporarily disabled."},{h3:"The Pre-Live stage"},{p:"In the Pre-Live stage the trader keeps building up simulated profit under conditions similar to a Live account. There's no consistency rule and no daily loss limit, but a maximum loss limit, contract limits and a position scaling plan still apply. You can request a payout every 24 hours. During this time you may hold only one evaluation account, with no payouts from it."},{h3:"The Live account"},{ul:["Trading is done on a brokerage account with real capital, with an end-of-day maximum loss limit, contract limits and a position scaling plan.","There's no consistency rule and no daily loss limit.","You can request a payout every 24 hours, with no total payout cap.","You start with one Live account, and may get more according to the firm's milestones.","The trader pays for professional market data (Professional Market Data).","A trader who moved to Live doesn't keep trading Sim Funded accounts. Breaking the rules in Pre-Live or Live leads to a waiting period before buying new simulated accounts, after which one Sim Funded account at a time is allowed."]}]},{id:"payouts",title:"Profit payouts",blocks:[{p:"The profit split on the funded account is {split} on all plans. Beyond that, each plan has its own eligibility terms: a profit target, a consistency rule, a buffer, profitable trading days and payout caps."},{ul:["Elite Daily: you can request a payout every day. You need to keep the buffer, and only the profit above it is available to withdraw, up to the cap. From the second payout a progress rule applies: part of the amount needs to come from new profit made since the previous payout.","Elite Access: a payout after a number of profitable trading days that meet the required threshold, keeping a balance above the buffer and meeting the consistency rule. Here too, from the second payout new profit is required, and the consistency calculation starts over in each payout cycle.","Instant Sim Funded: eligibility depends on a profit target and a consistency rule. The first payout's target is 6% of the starting balance, the second 5% above the new balance, and from the third on 4%. Each payout has a cap by account size.","Ignite: in each payout cycle you need to reach a profit target and meet a consistency rule. The first target is calculated from the starting balance, and the next ones from the updated balance after the previous payout. There's no minimum number of trading days."]},{live:"firmPayouts"},{note:"On Instant Sim Funded the profit target in the table is the first payout's target. From the second payout the target is lower and calculated from the updated balance."},{p:"Before requesting a payout you need to close your trades and make sure the amount is within the allowed range. After an approved payout the balance updates, and on some plans the profit target or the consistency calculation changes too. A payout may also narrow the safety margin from the drawdown. Payment is made through {payout_methods}."}]},{id:"platforms",title:"Trading platforms and connecting the account",blocks:[{p:"Top One Futures works with a Tradovate connection, through which you can trade on several platforms:"},{live:"platforms"},{p:"The login details come in the firm's dashboard or from the platform provider. On Tradovate you need to fill in the market data declaration (Market Data Subscriber Certification) on your first login and choose Non-Professional. The firm's help center also describes connecting through WealthCharts."},{note:"You can't switch platforms after the purchase, not even after passing an evaluation, getting a funded account or a reset. Moving to another platform requires buying a new account, so check before buying that the platform suits you."},{links:[{to:"/ninjatrader-guide",label:"The NinjaTrader guide"},{to:"/tradingview-guide",label:"The TradingView guide"}]}]},{id:"rules",title:"Features and trading rules worth knowing",blocks:[{h3:"Key features"},{ul:["Daily payouts on the Elite Daily plan, subject to the eligibility terms.","No consistency rule on the Elite Daily funded account. The rule applies only in the evaluation.","Two instant funding plans with no evaluation: Instant Sim Funded and Ignite.","A progression path through Pre-Live to trading with real capital, subject to a risk management review."]},{h3:"Rules and limits"},{ul:["Trading hours (Israel time): {hours}. No holding positions overnight or over the weekend ({overnight}).","Trading during news: allowed in the evaluation. On the Elite Daily and Elite Access funded accounts: {news_funded}.","Copying trades: {copy_trading}. Copying between different traders is prohibited.","Automated trading: {automation}.","Hedging: {hedging}. Exploiting glitches or price delays and coordinated trading are prohibited too.","High-frequency trading (HFT): {hft}.","Number of funded accounts: on Elite Daily {ed_max_accounts}, on Elite Access {ea_max_accounts}.","Inactivity: {inactivity} is required, otherwise the account may be closed."]},{links:[{to:"/economic-calendar",label:"The economic news calendar"}]}]},{id:"getting-started",title:"How do you start trading with Top One Futures?",blocks:[{ol:["Choose a plan: on Top One's website choose a plan, an account size and a trading platform.","Sign up and buy: open a user account and complete the payment.","Connect to the platform: the login details arrive in your personal area or from the platform provider, and you complete the trading account's activation.","Start trading: on Elite Daily and Elite Access you start with an evaluation. On Instant and Ignite you start directly on a simulated funded account."]},{p:"Before you start, get to know the account rules, the loss limits and the payout terms of the plan you chose."},{links:[{to:"/top-one",label:"The full firm page"},{to:"/comparison",label:"Compare all the firms"}]}]},{id:"related",title:"More guides",blocks:[{links:[{to:"/blog/how-to-pass-prop-firm-evaluation",label:"How to pass a prop firm evaluation"},{to:"/blog/tradovate-daily-stop",label:"Setting a daily stop on Tradovate"}]}]}],faq:[{q:"What's the difference between Elite Daily and Elite Access?",a:"Elite Daily has a consistency rule in the evaluation and not on the funded account, and no activation fee after passing. Elite Access has no consistency rule in the evaluation, but the funded account has a consistency rule for payouts, and after passing you pay a one-time activation fee. On Elite Daily you can request a payout every day, and on Elite Access after a number of profitable trading days."},{q:"Can you pass the evaluation in a single trading day?",a:"On Elite Access yes, if you reach the target and meet all the requirements. Elite Daily has a consistency rule in the evaluation, so you can't pass with all the profit made in one day."},{q:"Is there a daily loss limit?",a:"It depends on the plan. Elite Daily has a daily limit both in the evaluation and on the funded account. Elite Access has no daily limit in the evaluation, but has one on the funded account, unless the No Daily Loss Limit add-on was bought at purchase. Instant and Ignite also have a daily loss limit by account size."},{q:"What's the trader's share of the profit?",a:"On all four plans the profit split on the funded account is 90% to the trader and 10% to the firm, subject to payout approval and the account rules."},{q:"How many funded accounts can you hold at once?",a:"On Elite Daily up to 5 funded accounts, and on Elite Access up to 10. On the instant funding plans the limit varies by plan and account size. The full details are in this guide's funded account tables."},{q:"Can you trade during economic news?",a:"In the evaluations it's allowed. On the Elite Daily and Elite Access funded accounts you may not open or close trades in the two minutes before and after an important economic release. Instant and Ignite have no specific news restriction."},{q:"Can you switch trading platforms after buying?",a:"No. The platform is set at purchase and doesn't change, not even after passing an evaluation or a reset. To move to another platform you need to buy a new account."},{q:"Can you move to a Live account with real money?",a:"Yes, but not automatically. After the number of approved payouts the plan requires, the firm may review the trader for a move to Pre-Live and then to Live. In Pre-Live trading is still simulated, and on Live it's with real capital."}],sources:[{label:"Top One Futures - official website",href:"https://toponefutures.com/"},{label:"Differences between Elite, Elite Access and Elite Daily",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/14617600-differences-between-elite-elite-access-and-elite-daily-accounts"},{label:"Elite ACCESS Accounts - Overview",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/14595016-elite-access-accounts-overview"},{label:"Elite Daily V2 - I've Passed the Evaluation, What's Next",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/14073378-elite-daily-v2-i-ve-passed-the-evaluation-what-s-next"},{label:"Elite Access - Activation Fees Explained",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/16150794-elite-access-activation-fees-explained"},{label:"No Daily Loss Limit Add-On",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/17635087-no-daily-loss-limit-add-on-trade-without-daily-limits"},{label:"Instant Sim Funded - Payout Requirements",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/12718038-instant-sim-funded-account-payout-requirements"},{label:"Ignite Accounts - Payout Requirements",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/12707415-ignite-accounts-payout-requirements"},{label:"Account Comparison: Elite vs. Instant vs. Ignite",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/13449146-top-one-futures-account-comparison-elite-vs-instant-vs-ignite-accounts"},{label:"Path to Trading Live",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/11003130-path-to-trading-live"},{label:"Can I Switch My Trading Platform",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/17149077-can-i-switch-my-trading-platform-tradovate-ninjatrader-or-blackarrow"},{label:"Can I Trade News",href:"https://help.toponefutures.com/en/articles/10907951-can-i-trade-news"}]},G={title:"My Funded Futures - the complete guide to MFFU for 2026",seoTitle:"My Funded Futures (MFFU) - The Complete Guide 2026",seoDescription:"The complete guide to My Funded Futures: the Builder, Rapid and Pro plans, the evaluation rules, the funded account, profit payouts, the trading platforms and the move to Live.",excerpt:"MFFU's plans, the evaluation and funded rules, the payout terms and the move to Live, with prices and live data from the site's database.",category:"Firm guides",coverAlt:"MFFU guide: rules, plans and accounts at My Funded Futures",intro:["My Funded Futures (MFFU) is a prop firm for futures traders. The trader proves themselves on an evaluation account, moves on to a simulated funded account (Sim Funded) they can withdraw profits from, and later may move to a Live account with real capital, subject to the firm's terms and the risk management team's approval.","This guide explains the four plan families, Builder, Rapid, Rapid EOD and Pro, as well as the evaluation, the funded account, payouts and the move to Live. The changing prices and terms are pulled live from the site's database and update automatically. The guide isn't a rating of or a recommendation for the firm."],sections:[{id:"tracks",title:"My Funded Futures' plans",blocks:[{p:"The plans differ in the consistency rule, the risk limits, the payout terms and the path to Live:"},{ul:["Builder: an end-of-day (EOD) drawdown, no consistency rule in the evaluation, a soft daily loss limit and its own payout and progression terms. There's also a 50K version with a $1,500 drawdown.","Rapid: the evaluation has an end-of-day drawdown, and on the funded account the drawdown becomes intraday (Intraday Trailing). Daily payouts.","Rapid EOD: an end-of-day drawdown in both the evaluation and the funded account, and daily payouts.","Pro: an end-of-day drawdown, payouts in fixed cycles and its own terms for the move to Live. There's also a One Day add-on, which lets you pass the evaluation in one day."]},{live:"firmTracks"},{p:"New purchases are a one-time payment, with no monthly subscription and no activation fee after the evaluation. Older accounts bought on the subscription model keep their original terms."},{live:"firmPricing"}]},{id:"how-it-works",title:"How does the model work?",blocks:[{ol:["Choose a plan and an account size.","Evaluation: reach the profit target while keeping within the loss limits and the trading rules.","Simulated funded account (Sim Funded): after passing and the account is approved, build up profit and submit payout requests under the plan's terms. The risk and payout rules here may differ from those of the evaluation.","Live account: after trading on the funded account, the trader may move to trading with real capital. The move is decided by the plan's milestones or the risk management team, and it isn't guaranteed."]}]},{id:"evaluation",title:"The evaluation stage",blocks:[{p:"On all plans the evaluation's drawdown is an end-of-day (EOD) drawdown: the loss level updates from the peak balance at the end of the trading day, not from open profits during the day. Once the level has updated, it's also enforced during trading. Crossing the level fails the evaluation."},{h3:"Daily loss limit"},{p:"Builder has a soft daily loss limit (Soft Pause): hitting it stops trading until the next session, but doesn't fail the account as long as the maximum loss limit wasn't crossed. Rapid, Rapid EOD and Pro have no separate daily loss limit in the evaluation."},{h3:"The consistency rule"},{p:"The consistency rule limits how much of all the profit made in the evaluation can come from the most profitable trading day. Builder has no consistency rule in the evaluation. On Rapid the rule is {rapid_eval_consistency}, on Rapid EOD {rapid_eod_eval_consistency}, and on Pro {pro_eval_consistency}. The Pro One Day add-on has no consistency rule in the evaluation."},{p:"Going over the consistency rule doesn't fail the evaluation. The trader keeps building up profit until the requirement is met."},{h3:"Contracts and trading days"},{p:"Each plan and account size has a maximum number of contracts, in Mini and Micro contracts. On Builder and Pro One Day you can complete the evaluation in a single trading day. On Rapid, Rapid EOD and regular Pro you need to trade several days."},{live:"firmEval"},{p:"A failed evaluation can be reset and started over, for a fee. The reset returns the account to its starting state and resets your progress."}]},{id:"funded",title:"What happens after you pass the evaluation?",blocks:[{p:"Once you meet all the evaluation's requirements, the firm sends a confirmation email and you upgrade the account to funded through the dashboard. Sometimes an additional review is needed before approval. The current plans have no activation fee."},{h3:"What changes on each plan"},{ul:["Builder: keeps an end-of-day drawdown. The funded account has a consistency rule of {builder_funded_consistency} for payouts, along with a soft daily loss limit and its own payout terms.","Rapid: the drawdown changes from end of day in the evaluation to intraday (Intraday Trailing) on the funded account. There's no consistency rule for payouts, but there's a buffer requirement.","Rapid EOD: keeps an end-of-day drawdown. There's no consistency rule for payouts, and there's a buffer requirement.","Pro: keeps an end-of-day drawdown, with no consistency rule for payouts, a buffer and fixed payout cycles."]},{h3:"What is a buffer?"},{p:"A buffer is an amount of profit that has to build up in the account before you can request a regular payout. It's different from the maximum loss limit (drawdown), which sets how far the account can fall."},{live:"firmFunded"},{p:"The number of funded accounts you can hold at once depends on the plan and the account size, and is shown in the tables above."}]},{id:"live",title:"The move to Live",blocks:[{p:"After trading on a simulated funded account, the trader may move to a Live account, where trading is done with real capital through a brokerage account. The trading rules, loss limits and payout terms on Live may differ from those of the simulation."},{h3:"When do you move?"},{ul:["Builder: {builder_to_live}.","Rapid and Rapid EOD: {rapid_to_live}.","Pro: {pro_to_live}, reaching a profit milestone, or an invitation from the risk team. Meeting a criterion opens a review, and doesn't guarantee approval."]},{h3:"What happens during the move?"},{ul:["The Sim Funded accounts become inactive (Dormant), and you can't trade simulation and Live at the same time.","Under the firm's policy, you can't decline the move to Live once you've been selected.","On Rapid and Pro part of the simulated profit is kept in a Reserve. This money covers losses on Live, or is paid out as a bonus when you reach milestones."]},{h3:"Rules and costs on Live"},{ul:["The loss limit, the daily limit and the number of contracts are set by plan and account size, and may differ from the simulation.","On a Live account, professional market data costs, trading commissions and a platform license are deducted from the balance.","Crossing the maximum loss limit closes the Live account. On Builder and Pro there's then a waiting period during which you can't buy new evaluations."]}]},{id:"payouts",title:"Profit payouts",blocks:[{p:"Payout eligibility depends on the account balance, the buffer, the consistency rule and the request date. The profit split differs between plans ({builder_split} on Builder and Pro, {rapid_split} on Rapid), so the amount you request isn't necessarily the net amount you receive."},{ul:["Builder: a payout after the eligibility period, completing the buffer and additional profit above it. The consistency rule is calculated in each payout cycle and resets after an approved payout. There's a minimum and a cap by account size, and the number of payouts in the simulation is limited.","Rapid and Rapid EOD: you can request a payout every day, after the first waiting period, completing the buffer and the minimum amount. There's no consistency rule for payouts. The difference between the two plans is the drawdown type, not the payout frequency.","Pro: payouts in fixed cycles, measured from the first trade on the funded account. There's a buffer requirement, a minimum amount and a total payout cap during the simulation stage. There's no consistency rule, and Pro One Day follows the same payout rules."]},{live:"firmPayouts",tracks:["Builder","Rapid","Rapid EOD","Pro"]},{p:"You submit the request in your personal area. Most requests are approved at once, and some go through a manual review. Payment is made through {payout_methods}, after identity verification (KYC)."},{p:"A payout reduces the balance and the margin from the drawdown. On some plans, after the first payout the drawdown locks or changes, so check the balance and the updated level before you keep trading. Approved payouts also count toward the milestones for the move to Live."}]},{id:"platforms",title:"Trading platforms",blocks:[{p:"MFFU works with several connection providers, through which you can trade on a range of platforms:"},{live:"platforms"},{p:"The firm's help center also lists Volumetrica, ATAS and DeepChart/DeepDOM as supported platforms."},{p:"The login details are in your personal area after the purchase. When connecting through Tradovate, NinjaTrader or TradingView you need to activate access to the account and sign the market data agreement. On TradingView you connect to Tradovate through the Trading Panel. Other platforms may require a data provider, settings or a software license."},{links:[{to:"/ninjatrader-guide",label:"The NinjaTrader guide"},{to:"/tradingview-guide",label:"The TradingView guide"},{to:"/atas-guide",label:"The ATAS guide"}]}]},{id:"rules",title:"Features and trading rules worth knowing",blocks:[{h3:"Key features"},{ul:["Four plan families with different drawdown types, consistency requirements and payout terms.","A one-time payment and no activation fee after the evaluation.","Daily payouts on the Rapid plans.","A path to trading with real capital, by the milestones and the risk management policy."]},{h3:"Rules and limits"},{ul:["Trading hours (Israel time): {hours}. You can't hold trades past the end of the session ({overnight}).","Trading during news: allowed in the evaluations and on Builder. On the Rapid and Pro funded accounts: {news_funded}.","Copying trades: {copy_trading}. Copying or coordinating trading between different traders is prohibited.","Automated trading: {automation}.","Hedging: {hedging}, including Mini and Micro on the same asset.","High-frequency trading (HFT): {hft}. Exploiting gaps in the simulated environment is prohibited too.","Inactivity: {inactivity} is required. On an evaluation account this may close the account, and on a funded account cancel the profit.","Breaking the rules may lead to failing the evaluation, cancelled profit or the account being closed."]},{links:[{to:"/economic-calendar",label:"The economic news calendar"}]}]},{id:"getting-started",title:"How do you start trading with My Funded Futures?",blocks:[{ol:["Sign up: open a user account on the MFFU website and go to your personal area.","Choose a plan: Builder, Rapid, Rapid EOD or Pro, by drawdown type, trading rules and payout terms.","Choose a size and buy: choose an account size, check the plan's terms and pay.","Connect to the platform: get the login details in your personal area and connect to one of the supported platforms.","Start the evaluation: trade toward the profit target, within the loss limits and the plan's other rules."]},{p:"Before you start, make sure the right account is selected on the platform, the market data is active and the plan's rules are clear."},{links:[{to:"/my-funded-futures",label:"The full firm page"},{to:"/comparison",label:"Compare all the firms"}]}]},{id:"related",title:"More guides",blocks:[{links:[{to:"/blog/how-to-pass-prop-firm-evaluation",label:"How to pass a prop firm evaluation"},{to:"/blog/tradovate-daily-stop",label:"Setting a daily stop on Tradovate"}]}]}],faq:[{q:"Can you trade several MFFU accounts at once?",a:"Yes, but the number depends on the plan and the account size. On Builder the limit is one funded account in most sizes, and the other plans allow more. The full details are in this guide's funded account tables."},{q:"Do you pay an activation fee after passing the evaluation?",a:"No. MFFU's current plans have no activation fee for moving from an evaluation account to a Sim Funded account."},{q:"Are evaluation accounts charged monthly?",a:"Not on new purchases. Since August 2026 MFFU works with a one-time payment, with no monthly renewal. Older accounts bought on the subscription model keep their original terms."},{q:"Can you reset a failed evaluation account?",a:"Yes. The firm offers a paid reset, which returns the account to its starting state."},{q:"What happens if you don't trade the account for a long time?",a:"Accounts on the one-time payment model have an inactivity rule: if no trade is opened for 7 days in a row, an evaluation account may be closed, and on a funded account the profit may be cancelled. If you plan to be away, contact support in advance."},{q:"Does going over the consistency rule fail the evaluation?",a:"No. On the plans with a consistency rule, going over it doesn't fail the account. You keep building up profit until the requirement is met."},{q:"How do you receive the payout money?",a:"MFFU sends payments through Rise (Riseworks). After the request is approved and identity is verified (KYC), the money reaches the trader's Rise account, and from there you withdraw it using the methods supported in your country."},{q:"Can you decline the move to Live, or hold Sim Funded and Live at the same time?",a:"No to both. Under the firm's policy you can't decline the move once you've been selected, and with the move the Sim Funded accounts become inactive."}],sources:[{label:"My Funded Futures - official website",href:"https://myfundedfutures.com/"},{label:"Moving from Evaluation to Sim-Funded Account",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/16498635-moving-from-evaluation-to-sim-funded-account"},{label:"Consistency Rule at My Funded Futures",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/11994562-consistency-rule-at-my-fundedfutures"},{label:"Builder Plan 50K - A Comprehensive Guide",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/14290805-builder-plan-50k-a-comprehensive-guide"},{label:"Understanding Rapid Live",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/13134718-understanding-rapid-live"},{label:"Comprehensive Look into the Redesigned Pro Plan",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/17573707-comprehensive-look-into-redesigned-pro-plan"},{label:"Understanding Live Funded Account",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/10101257-understanding-live-funded-account-at-myfunded-futures"},{label:"Payout Policy Overview",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/13745661-payout-policy-overview-best-and-fastest-prop-firm-payouts"},{label:"Guide to Your First Payout",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/11542406-guide-to-your-first-payout-quick-and-easy-process"},{label:"Overview of Supported Platforms",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/8528335-overview-of-supported-platforms-at-mffu"},{label:"News Trading Policy",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/8230009-news-trading-policy"},{label:"Fair Play and Prohibited Trading Practices",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/8444599-fair-play-and-prohibited-trading-practices"},{label:"Inactivity Rule - One-Time Payment Model",href:"https://help.myfundedfutures.com/en/articles/16596524-inactivity-rule-one-time-payment-model"}]},X={"long-short":T,"order-types":A,"trading-styles":I,"technical-vs-fundamental-analysis":x,overtrading:P,"trading-plan":S,"trading-psychology":q,"risk-reward-1-to-3-losing-streaks":O,"how-to-pass-prop-firm-evaluation":F,"tik-nostro":L,"footprint-guide":R,"rithmic-daily-stop":C,"tradovate-daily-stop":D,"tradovate-drawdown":W,"takeprofit-buffer-withdrawal":M,"atas-guide-hebrew":_,"tradingview-tutorial":z,"replikanto-install-guide":E,"apex-trader-funding-guide":N,"lucid-trading-guide":H,"topstep-guide":B,"take-profit-trader-guide":$,"ftmo-futures-guide":j,"topone-futures-guide":Y,"my-funded-futures-guide":G};export{X as default};
